// Generat din PDF-urile tale: întrebări Writing Task 2 cu eseu-model, idei și exemple.
const ESSAYS = [{"id":"art1","topic":"art","type":"Opinion","key":true,"q":"Art is not as important in today's world as science. To what extent do you agree?","ideas":["Art and science are interdependent rather than rivals","The arts generate income and employment"],"ex":["For example, Leonardo da Vinci studied human anatomy in order to paint more realistically, and Steve Jobs said that a calligraphy course influenced the typography of the first Mac computers.","For instance, the Guggenheim Museum, which opened in Bilbao in 1997, transformed a declining industrial city into a popular tourist destination."],"essay":["It is often claimed that science matters more to modern society than the arts. This essay disagrees with this view because art and science depend on each other, and because the arts bring economic benefits to communities.","The primary reason is that art and science are interdependent rather than rivals. This is because design, imagination and creativity are at the heart of many inventions, which means that scientists need artistic thinking to solve problems. As a result, removing the arts from society would also slow down scientific progress. In other words, a society that ignores the arts would lose the imagination that every great discovery needs. For example, Leonardo da Vinci studied human anatomy in order to paint more realistically, and Steve Jobs said that a calligraphy course influenced the typography of the first Mac computers.","Another important point is that the arts generate income and employment. That is to say, cultural attractions bring tourists, who spend money in hotels and restaurants, and this creates jobs and helps to regenerate cities. In this sense, art is an investment rather than a luxury. Moreover, young people can find careers in design, tourism and the media thanks to these cultural industries. For instance, the Guggenheim Museum, which opened in Bilbao in 1997, transformed a declining industrial city into a popular tourist destination.","In conclusion, this essay disagrees that art is less important than science because creativity supports innovation, and because cultural projects bring both income and jobs to society. For these reasons, both fields deserve equal respect."],"wc":255},{"id":"art2","topic":"art","type":"Opinion","key":false,"q":"International art and literature is essential in today's world. Do you agree?","ideas":["Foreign art and literature build understanding between cultures","International art inspires creativity at home"],"ex":["For example, the South Korean film Parasite won the Oscar for Best Picture in 2020, and it introduced millions of viewers around the world to everyday life in Korea.","For instance, Pablo Picasso was influenced by African masks when he painted Les Demoiselles d'Avignon, and this helped to start the Cubist movement."],"essay":["It is often said that art and literature from other countries are vital in the modern world. This essay agrees with this statement because international works help people to understand other cultures, and because they inspire creativity in local artists.","The primary reason is that foreign art and literature build understanding between cultures. This is because films, books and paintings show how other people live and think, and this helps audiences to feel empathy instead of prejudice. As a result, societies become more tolerant and more open to cooperation. Moreover, modern technology makes it easy for anyone to discover foreign works at the click of a button. For example, the South Korean film Parasite won the Oscar for Best Picture in 2020, and it introduced millions of viewers around the world to everyday life in Korea.","Another important point is that international art inspires creativity at home. That is to say, when artists come across new styles and techniques, they combine them with their own traditions and create original work. Without this exchange, local art could become repetitive and lose its energy. For this reason, international contact keeps the arts of every country fresh and original. For instance, Pablo Picasso was influenced by African masks when he painted Les Demoiselles d'Avignon, and this helped to start the Cubist movement.","In conclusion, this essay agrees that international art and literature are essential today because they encourage cultural understanding and stimulate new ideas among local artists. Therefore, governments should keep their borders open to foreign culture."],"wc":254},{"id":"art3","topic":"art","type":"Two-part Question","key":true,"q":"Many people support the arts. Why is that?","ideas":["Taking part in the arts improves mental wellbeing","The arts create income and jobs"],"ex":["For example, the British health system uses \"social prescribing\", in which doctors send patients to art and music groups instead of only giving them medicine.","For instance, the Edinburgh festivals attract visitors from all over the world every summer and provide work for thousands of residents."],"essay":["A large number of people give their support to the arts, for example by donating money or by visiting galleries. This essay will explain that this happens mainly because the arts improve mental wellbeing and because they bring economic benefits to local communities.","The first reason is that taking part in the arts improves mental wellbeing. This is because creative activities offer a break from daily pressure and give people a way to express feelings that are difficult to describe in words. They also bring people together, which reduces loneliness and stress. In addition, many people feel that a calmer mind helps them to work and study better. For example, the British health system uses \"social prescribing\", in which doctors send patients to art and music groups instead of only giving them medicine.","Another reason is that the arts create income and jobs. In other words, museums and festivals attract visitors who spend money in hotels, shops and restaurants, so local people benefit directly. Therefore, many people see support for the arts as a wise investment rather than a cost. This economic argument is especially convincing for governments with limited budgets. For instance, the Edinburgh festivals attract visitors from all over the world every summer and provide work for thousands of residents.","In conclusion, the arts have a large number of supporters because they improve mental health and because they bring money and employment to communities. These two benefits explain why public interest in culture remains strong."],"wc":247},{"id":"art4","topic":"art","type":"Discussion","key":true,"q":"The government should spend more money supporting the arts, while others think funding should go on health care and education. Discuss both sides and give your opinion.","ideas":["Basic needs such as hospitals and schools must come first","The arts also deserve more public money, because the choice is not \"either-or\""],"ex":["For example, during the Covid-19 pandemic, hospitals in many countries were overwhelmed, which showed how essential healthcare funding is.","For instance, during the pandemic the UK created the Culture Recovery Fund to save theatres and museums, which proves that a government can support culture without neglecting other services."],"essay":["Some people believe that governments should give more public money to the arts, while others argue that hospitals and schools deserve this funding. This essay agrees that the arts should receive more support, although health and education must remain a priority.","On the one hand, basic needs such as hospitals and schools must come first. This is because they affect everyone's survival and opportunities, and a society cannot enjoy culture if its citizens are ill or uneducated. Therefore, it is understandable that many taxpayers want these services to be protected. Without them, a country cannot build a healthy and skilled population. For example, during the Covid-19 pandemic, hospitals in many countries were overwhelmed, which showed how essential healthcare funding is. However, I believe that this does not mean the arts should be ignored.","On the other hand, the arts also deserve more public money, because the choice is not \"either-or\". That is to say, cultural spending brings income, jobs and wellbeing, so it supports the economy and public health at the same time. A balanced budget can therefore pay for both. In addition, private sponsors and museum tickets can cover part of the cost. For instance, during the pandemic the UK created the Culture Recovery Fund to save theatres and museums, which proves that a government can support culture without neglecting other services.","In conclusion, although hospitals and schools are essential, this essay believes that governments should also increase arts funding because it brings economic and social benefits. A fair balance between the two is the best solution."],"wc":258},{"id":"art5","topic":"art","type":"Two-part Question","key":false,"q":"Traditional arts should be preserved better. Do you agree? How can this be done?","ideas":["Traditional arts should be preserved because they carry the identity and history of a community"],"ex":["For example, Horezu pottery and the traditional Romanian blouse, the ia, are both included on UNESCO's list of intangible cultural heritage.","For instance, Japan protects its master craftspeople through the \"Living National Treasures\" system, which gives them financial support to pass on their knowledge."],"essay":["It is argued that traditional art forms need better protection. This essay agrees with this view because they carry a community's identity, and it will suggest that school lessons and financial support for craftspeople are the most effective ways to protect them.","Traditional arts should be preserved because they carry the identity and history of a community. This is because crafts, costumes and songs pass down the values of earlier generations, and once they disappear, a part of the national memory is lost forever. Therefore, protecting these traditions is a way of protecting the community itself. For example, Horezu pottery and the traditional Romanian blouse, the ia, are both included on UNESCO's list of intangible cultural heritage. There are two practical ways to achieve this: teaching traditional crafts in schools and supporting craftspeople financially. If children learn these crafts in class, they will value them, and some of them will continue the tradition as adults. Furthermore, grants make it possible for craftspeople to earn a living from their skills and to train apprentices. In this way, old skills become real jobs instead of museum exhibits. For instance, Japan protects its master craftspeople through the \"Living National Treasures\" system, which gives them financial support to pass on their knowledge.","In conclusion, this essay agrees that traditional arts must be preserved because they carry a community's identity, and this can be done through education and funding for skilled craftspeople. With these measures, traditions can survive for future generations."],"wc":246},{"id":"art6","topic":"art","type":"Opinion","key":false,"q":"Many people believe the arts should be censored. Do you agree?","ideas":["Censorship restricts freedom of expression","Censorship often backfires"],"ex":["For example, the communist regime in Romania censored books and music before 1989, and many writers were unable to publish what they truly thought.","For instance, the Nazi exhibition \"Degenerate Art\" in 1937 was meant to mock modern artists, but it attracted huge crowds, while in the UK the BBFC classifies films by age instead of forbidding them."],"essay":["It is argued that books, films and other artworks should be controlled by the authorities. This essay disagrees with this opinion because censorship restricts freedom of expression, and because it often produces the opposite result to the one intended.","The primary reason is that censorship restricts freedom of expression. This is because artists use their work to criticise society and to question those in power, and censors can silence this important debate. As a result, citizens are deprived of new ideas and honest information. In the long term, a society without open debate cannot correct its own mistakes. For example, the communist regime in Romania censored books and music before 1989, and many writers were unable to publish what they truly thought.","Another important point is that censorship often backfires. That is to say, when a work is banned, people become more curious about it and want to see it even more, so the ban makes it more popular. It is true that children need protection, but age ratings solve this problem without banning anything. In this way, parents can decide what is suitable for their own children. For instance, the Nazi exhibition \"Degenerate Art\" in 1937 was meant to mock modern artists, but it attracted huge crowds, while in the UK the BBFC classifies films by age instead of forbidding them.","In conclusion, this essay disagrees that the arts should be censored because it limits free expression and often makes banned works more attractive. Therefore, societies should protect young people with age ratings rather than with bans."],"wc":258},{"id":"art7","topic":"art","type":"Opinion","key":false,"q":"Social media is changing the way we appreciate art. Is this a positive or negative trend?","ideas":["Social media helps new artists to reach an audience","Social media makes art accessible to everyone"],"ex":["For example, the poet Rupi Kaur became famous by posting her poems on Instagram, and her books later sold millions of copies.","For instance, Google Arts & Culture allows users to take virtual tours of museums around the world."],"essay":["It is said that online networks are transforming the way people discover and judge art. This essay believes that this is a positive trend because it gives artists a wider audience and makes art accessible to ordinary people.","The primary reason is that social media helps new artists to reach an audience. This is because artists no longer depend on galleries or publishers to be noticed; they can share their work with millions of people for free. As a result, more talented people can build a career. Moreover, artists can receive instant feedback from their fans and improve their work. For example, the poet Rupi Kaur became famous by posting her poems on Instagram, and her books later sold millions of copies.","Another important point is that social media makes art accessible to everyone. In other words, people who cannot afford to travel or to buy tickets can still discover masterpieces from their own homes, and this encourages more people to become interested in culture. Although some critics fear that art is judged only by the number of likes, these risks are smaller than the benefits. In addition, museums can use these platforms to attract younger visitors. For instance, Google Arts & Culture allows users to take virtual tours of museums around the world.","In conclusion, this essay believes that the influence of social media on art is positive because it helps new artists to become known and gives everyone access to culture. Therefore, museums and artists should use these platforms even more."],"wc":253},{"id":"art8","topic":"art","type":"Opinion","key":false,"q":"Art is a healthy hobby and more people should be encouraged to take it up as a hobby. What is your opinion?","ideas":["Artistic activities reduce stress and improve mental wellbeing","Art hobbies create communities"],"ex":["For example, Winston Churchill started painting as a hobby in order to relax during difficult periods of his life.","For instance, the British \"social prescribing\" system sends patients to art and music groups in order to improve their wellbeing."],"essay":["Some people think that painting, music and other creative activities are good for health, and that more people should practise them in their free time. This essay agrees with this view because art reduces stress, and because it creates a sense of community.","The primary reason is that artistic activities reduce stress and improve mental wellbeing. This is because creativity gives people a break from daily pressure and a way to express emotions that are difficult to describe in words. As a result, they feel calmer and more positive. Over time, this regular relaxation can also protect people from serious stress-related problems. For example, Winston Churchill started painting as a hobby in order to relax during difficult periods of his life.","Another important point is that art hobbies create communities. That is to say, people who join a choir, a painting class or a theatre group meet others with similar interests, and this reduces loneliness. Governments could therefore promote art hobbies as a low-cost way to improve public health. At the same time, shared hobbies help neighbours to build stronger and friendlier communities. For instance, the British \"social prescribing\" system sends patients to art and music groups in order to improve their wellbeing.","In conclusion, this essay agrees that art is a healthy hobby and that people should be encouraged to try it because it reduces stress and brings people together. Therefore, schools and local authorities should make creative activities easy to join."],"wc":243},{"id":"environment1","topic":"environment","type":"Two-part Question","key":false,"q":"Many species are becoming extinct. Do you think this is a problem? What can be done about it?","ideas":["The loss of species is a serious problem because ecosystems are interdependent","Creating protected areas and punishing poaching strictly"],"ex":["For example, the reintroduction of wolves into Yellowstone National Park in 1995 restored the balance of the ecosystem, showing how important a single species can be.","For instance, Costa Rica more than doubled its forest area since the 1980s by paying landowners for ecological services, and giant pandas were reclassified from \"endangered\" to \"vulnerable\" in 2016."],"essay":["A growing number of animal and plant species are disappearing from the planet. This essay believes that this is a serious problem because ecosystems depend on every species, and it will suggest that protected areas and strict laws against poaching are the most effective solutions.","The loss of species is a serious problem because ecosystems are interdependent. This is because every plant and animal plays a role in the food chain, so when one disappears, others suffer and the whole system becomes weaker. Moreover, unknown species may contain substances that could become medicines in the future. The main causes are the destruction of habitats, poaching and climate change. For example, the reintroduction of wolves into Yellowstone National Park in 1995 restored the balance of the ecosystem, showing how important a single species can be.","Two effective solutions are creating protected areas and punishing poaching strictly. If governments protect forests and wetlands, animals can keep their homes, and if poaching carries heavy fines or prison sentences, illegal hunting becomes less attractive. Paying local people to protect nature also gives them a reason to preserve it. International cooperation also helps because many species migrate across borders. For instance, Costa Rica more than doubled its forest area since the 1980s by paying landowners for ecological services, and giant pandas were reclassified from \"endangered\" to \"vulnerable\" in 2016.","In conclusion, the extinction of species is a serious problem because it damages entire ecosystems, but protected areas and strong laws can reduce it. International cooperation will make these measures even more effective. Future generations will then inherit a richer natural world."],"wc":266},{"id":"environment2","topic":"environment","type":"Discussion","key":true,"q":"Do you think it is the government's or the individual's responsibility to protect the environment?","ideas":["Governments have the power to make changes on a large scale","Individuals must act because their daily choices influence the environment"],"ex":["For example, the Montreal Protocol of 1987 helped to repair the ozone layer, and the Paris Agreement of 2015 set common climate goals for almost every country.","For instance, Germany's bottle deposit system leads to very high return rates, and the plastic bag charge introduced in England in 2015 reduced the use of bags in supermarkets dramatically."],"essay":["Some people believe that governments must protect the environment, while others think that every individual is responsible. This essay believes that governments have the main responsibility, although individuals must also contribute.","On the one hand, governments have the power to make changes on a large scale. This is because only the state can pass laws, impose taxes on polluters and fund clean energy, and pollution does not respect borders, so international agreements are also necessary. Industry produces far more emissions than a single household, so rules for companies matter most. However, individual choices also count. Therefore, public policy sets the framework in which people live. For example, the Montreal Protocol of 1987 helped to repair the ozone layer, and the Paris Agreement of 2015 set common climate goals for almost every country.","On the other hand, individuals must act because their daily choices influence the environment. That is to say, people decide how much they travel, buy and throw away, and many say that they care about nature but do nothing because it is inconvenient or expensive. Incentives can help them change their habits. In the end, small daily actions multiply when millions of people repeat them. For instance, Germany's bottle deposit system leads to very high return rates, and the plastic bag charge introduced in England in 2015 reduced the use of bags in supermarkets dramatically.","In conclusion, although individuals should recycle and save energy, this essay believes that governments carry the main responsibility because they can create laws and international agreements. Real progress needs cooperation between both. Neither group can succeed alone."],"wc":264},{"id":"environment3","topic":"environment","type":"Problem / Solution","key":true,"q":"Many people throw litter on to the streets or in green spaces. Why is this a problem? What solutions can you suggest?","ideas":["Littering is a problem because it damages the environment and public health","Combine fines with convenient bins and community campaigns"],"ex":["For example, wildlife organisations regularly report birds and sea animals that die after swallowing plastic from beaches and parks.","For instance, Singapore applies heavy fines for littering and banned chewing gum sales in 1992, and it is now one of the cleanest cities in the world."],"essay":["It is common to see rubbish left on pavements and in parks. This is a problem because it harms nature and public health, and the most viable solutions are heavy fines combined with more bins and community clean-ups.","Littering is a problem because it damages the environment and public health. This is because plastic and other waste can take hundreds of years to break down, and animals may eat it and die, while rubbish in streets attracts rats and spreads disease. In addition, dirty places look unpleasant and discourage tourists and residents from using them. The main causes are carelessness, a lack of bins and the packaging of fast food. Litter also spoils the view of tourist areas, which can reduce local income. For example, wildlife organisations regularly report birds and sea animals that die after swallowing plastic from beaches and parks.","A viable solution is to combine fines with convenient bins and community campaigns. If people know that they will pay a high penalty, they will think twice, and if bins are available everywhere, they have no excuse. Regular clean-up days also teach residents to take pride in their neighbourhood. Offenders who clean the streets as a community penalty also learn the value of a clean city. For instance, Singapore applies heavy fines for littering and banned chewing gum sales in 1992, and it is now one of the cleanest cities in the world.","In conclusion, littering harms nature and health, but strict fines, enough bins and community campaigns can solve the problem. A clean environment requires effort from both authorities and citizens."],"wc":264},{"id":"environment4","topic":"environment","type":"Opinion","key":true,"q":"Many drug companies and make-up companies test their products on animals. Do you think this is right?","ideas":["Testing on animals can be justified for medicines because it saves human lives"],"ex":["For example, the discovery of insulin and many vaccines depended on research with animals.","For instance, the European Union banned animal testing for cosmetics in 2013, and companies there continue to launch new products without it."],"essay":["It is a common practice for pharmaceutical and cosmetic firms to try their products on animals before selling them. This essay believes that testing medicines on animals can be justified, while testing cosmetics is wrong because it causes needless suffering.","Testing on animals can be justified for medicines because it saves human lives. This is because new drugs must be proven safe before people take them, and a living body shows effects that cannot yet be predicted by other methods. Many treatments that are used every day were developed in this way. Nevertheless, the number of animals should be as small as possible. Without such testing, new treatments could harm patients. In addition, strict rules ensure that animals are treated humanely during experiments. For example, the discovery of insulin and many vaccines depended on research with animals. Testing cosmetics on animals is wrong because the benefit is trivial and alternatives exist. That is to say, nobody needs a new lipstick enough to justify cruelty, and modern technology offers other options, such as computer models and tests on artificial tissue. Animals can also react differently from humans, so the results are not always reliable. Therefore, laws should require companies to use alternatives whenever possible. For instance, the European Union banned animal testing for cosmetics in 2013, and companies there continue to launch new products without it.","In conclusion, this essay believes that animal testing is acceptable only when it is necessary for medical progress, but not for beauty products. Governments should therefore support alternative methods in all research. This approach combines scientific progress with respect for animals."],"wc":266},{"id":"environment5","topic":"environment","type":"Problem / Solution","key":false,"q":"Beauty spots are often tourist attractions. What problems does this cause? What can be done about it?","ideas":["Too many tourists damage the very places that they come to enjoy","Limit visitor numbers and to charge fees that fund protection"],"ex":["For example, Maya Bay in Thailand became so damaged by boats and visitors that it was closed from 2018 to 2022 so that the coral could recover.","For instance, Machu Picchu has strict daily visitor limits, and Bhutan applies a daily sustainable development fee for tourists."],"essay":["Beautiful natural places attract large numbers of visitors from around the world. This causes problems such as litter, erosion and overcrowding, and these can be reduced by limiting the number of visitors and by promoting responsible tourism.","The main problem is that too many tourists damage the very places that they come to enjoy. This is because thousands of visitors leave rubbish, walk on fragile soil and disturb wildlife, and crowded paths spoil the experience for everyone. In addition, local residents may suffer from noise and rising prices, while the natural beauty slowly disappears. Moreover, erosion on popular trails can change the landscape permanently. For example, Maya Bay in Thailand became so damaged by boats and visitors that it was closed from 2018 to 2022 so that the coral could recover.","A viable solution is to limit visitor numbers and to charge fees that fund protection. If authorities allow only a certain number of people each day, nature has time to recover, and money from tickets can pay for cleaning and conservation. Promoting ecotourism and teaching visitors to respect the rules also helps to protect these areas. Local communities should also receive a share of the income so that they have a reason to protect nature. For instance, Machu Picchu has strict daily visitor limits, and Bhutan applies a daily sustainable development fee for tourists.","In conclusion, tourism can damage beauty spots through litter, erosion and crowds, but visitor limits, fees and ecotourism can protect them. Careful management allows both visitors and nature to benefit. In this way, beauty spots can remain attractive for future visitors."],"wc":266},{"id":"environment6","topic":"environment","type":"Problem / Solution","key":false,"q":"Many people are concerned about climate change. What problems does it cause? What solutions are there?","ideas":["The main problems of climate change are extreme weather and its effects on people","Switching to renewable energy and putting a price on carbon"],"ex":["For example, the floods in Pakistan in 2022 covered a third of the country, destroyed crops and forced millions of people to leave their homes.","For instance, Denmark produces more than half of its electricity from wind, and Sweden has had a carbon tax since 1991 while keeping a strong economy."],"essay":["Global warming worries a large part of the population. The main problems caused by it are extreme weather and rising sea levels, and the most effective solutions are renewable energy and taxes on carbon emissions.","The main problems of climate change are extreme weather and its effects on people. This is because a warmer atmosphere produces more floods, droughts and storms, and rising seas threaten coastal communities. As a result, crops fail, food becomes more expensive and millions of people may have to leave their homes. Poor countries, which have contributed least to the problem, often suffer the most. Rising temperatures also spread diseases to new regions. For example, the floods in Pakistan in 2022 covered a third of the country, destroyed crops and forced millions of people to leave their homes.","Two effective solutions are switching to renewable energy and putting a price on carbon. If countries produce electricity from wind and solar power instead of coal, they release far fewer greenhouse gases, and a carbon tax makes polluting more expensive, so companies look for cleaner methods. The cost of solar energy has also fallen sharply, which makes this change realistic. Cities can also invest in public transport and plant trees to cool urban areas. For instance, Denmark produces more than half of its electricity from wind, and Sweden has had a carbon tax since 1991 while keeping a strong economy.","In conclusion, climate change causes extreme weather and displacement, but renewable energy and carbon taxes can reduce its effects. Governments must act quickly because the consequences are already visible. Every country will benefit from a stable climate."],"wc":269},{"id":"education1","topic":"education","type":"Two-part Question","key":true,"q":"Schools should do more to instil discipline in students. How should this be done? Do you think the parents should take some responsibility for this?","ideas":["Schools can instil discipline by setting clear rules and by rewarding good behaviour"],"ex":["For example, in Japan pupils clean their own classrooms every day, which teaches them responsibility and respect for shared spaces.","For instance, many schools ask parents to sign a behaviour agreement at the start of the year, and pupils with active parents usually behave better."],"essay":["It is argued that educational institutions ought to teach young people better self-control and respect for rules. This essay believes that clear rules and rewards are the best way to achieve this, and that parents must share this responsibility because children learn their first habits at home.","Schools can instil discipline by setting clear rules and by rewarding good behaviour. This is because pupils behave better when they know exactly what is expected and what the consequences are, and positive recognition motivates them more than punishment. Poor discipline often comes from large classes, boredom and a lack of parental involvement, so smaller groups and interesting lessons also help. Strict but fair teachers also gain respect more quickly than those who only punish. For example, in Japan pupils clean their own classrooms every day, which teaches them responsibility and respect for shared spaces. Parents must also take responsibility because children first learn behaviour at home. That is to say, a child who sees respectful adults and who has fixed routines will bring these habits to school, while teachers cannot correct everything alone. Therefore, schools should cooperate closely with families through meetings and regular communication. Positive habits formed early are difficult to lose later. For instance, many schools ask parents to sign a behaviour agreement at the start of the year, and pupils with active parents usually behave better.","In conclusion, schools can build discipline through consistent rules, rewards and responsibilities, and parents must support this effort at home. Only joint action will produce well-behaved and respectful students. Schools and families should therefore speak with one voice."],"wc":265},{"id":"education2","topic":"education","type":"Discussion","key":true,"q":"Some people think children should be grouped together into classes, others do not agree. Discuss both sides and give your opinion.","ideas":["Learning in a class develops social skills and makes teaching efficient","Individual learning allows every child to progress at their own speed"],"ex":["For example, in Japan pupils work in groups and clean their classrooms together, which builds teamwork and responsibility.","For instance, the online platform Khan Academy lets students repeat lessons at their own pace, but it is used mainly to support teachers, not to replace classes."],"essay":["Some people believe that pupils should study in classes with other children, while others argue that they would learn better individually. This essay agrees with the first view, although individual attention is also valuable.","On the one hand, learning in a class develops social skills and makes teaching efficient. This is because children learn to cooperate, share ideas and respect others when they study together, and teachers can explain a lesson once to many pupils, which saves time and money. Children also learn from each other's questions and mistakes. In a group, pupils also learn how to solve disagreements peacefully. For example, in Japan pupils work in groups and clean their classrooms together, which builds teamwork and responsibility. However, I recognise that classes also have weaknesses.","On the other hand, individual learning allows every child to progress at their own speed. That is to say, in a large class, fast learners may become bored while slower pupils fall behind, and a teacher cannot give everyone attention. Technology can offer personal practice that fits each level, but it cannot replace social contact. Therefore, it is wise to include personal support for those who need it. For instance, the online platform Khan Academy lets students repeat lessons at their own pace, but it is used mainly to support teachers, not to replace classes.","In conclusion, although individual study helps every child to progress at their own speed, this essay believes that classes are better because they build social skills and use resources efficiently. A mix of group lessons and personal support is ideal. Teachers should plan both group tasks and individual practice."],"wc":270},{"id":"education3","topic":"education","type":"Advantages / Disadvantages","key":true,"q":"More and more people are choosing to educate themselves online. What are the advantages and disadvantages of this?","ideas":["It is flexible and affordable","Online learners need strong self-discipline and miss personal contact"],"ex":["For example, the Open University in the UK has offered distance learning since 1969, and platforms such as Coursera provide courses from top universities.","For instance, completion rates in massive open online courses are often very low, and during the pandemic UNESCO estimated that over a billion pupils were affected by school closures."],"essay":["An increasing number of learners are studying through the internet instead of attending classes. The main advantages of online education are flexibility and low cost, whereas the main disadvantages are the need for self-discipline and the lack of personal contact.","The main advantage of online education is that it is flexible and affordable. This is because students can study at any time and from any place, so people with jobs, families or disabilities, or those who live far from a university, can also learn. Moreover, online courses are usually cheaper than traditional ones, since there are no travel or accommodation costs. In addition, many courses offer certificates that employers recognise. Learners can also repeat difficult lessons as often as they need. For example, the Open University in the UK has offered distance learning since 1969, and platforms such as Coursera provide courses from top universities.","One major disadvantage is that online learners need strong self-discipline and miss personal contact. In other words, without a teacher and classmates, many students lose motivation and do not finish their courses, and they cannot easily ask questions or receive immediate feedback. In addition, practical skills are difficult to learn through a screen. As a result, students without a quiet place to study may fall behind. For instance, completion rates in massive open online courses are often very low, and during the pandemic UNESCO estimated that over a billion pupils were affected by school closures.","In conclusion, online education offers flexibility and lower costs, but it requires discipline and cannot fully replace human interaction. For this reason, many students may benefit most from combining online and classroom study. Universities and employers should also recognise good online qualifications."],"wc":282},{"id":"education4","topic":"education","type":"Opinion","key":true,"q":"Some people think that traditional subjects at school are a waste of time because soft skills and experience are more important to get a good job. Do you agree?","ideas":["Traditional subjects build the foundation for critical thinking","Many professions require deep academic knowledge"],"ex":["For example, Finland introduced multidisciplinary learning in its 2016 curriculum, but it did not abandon the core subjects, and its pupils continue to perform very well.","For instance, the World Economic Forum stresses critical thinking and creativity in its reports on the future of jobs, and both depend on a solid knowledge base."],"essay":["It is claimed that subjects such as mathematics or history are less useful than communication skills and practical experience when looking for work. This essay disagrees with this claim because academic subjects provide the foundation for critical thinking, and because they remain essential for many careers.","The primary reason is that traditional subjects build the foundation for critical thinking. This is because mathematics teaches logic, history teaches analysis of evidence and languages teach clear communication, so students cannot develop soft skills without this base. As a result, people who lack basic knowledge find it harder to solve problems or to evaluate information. Therefore, schools should teach both content and skills. For example, Finland introduced multidisciplinary learning in its 2016 curriculum, but it did not abandon the core subjects, and its pupils continue to perform very well.","Another important point is that many professions require deep academic knowledge. That is to say, doctors, engineers and teachers need years of study in traditional subjects before they can do their jobs safely, and soft skills alone are not enough. It is true that employers value teamwork and communication, but these are best taught together with academic content. For instance, the World Economic Forum stresses critical thinking and creativity in its reports on the future of jobs, and both depend on a solid knowledge base.","In conclusion, this essay disagrees that traditional subjects are a waste of time because they develop thinking skills and are necessary for many careers. Schools should therefore teach soft skills together with academic subjects. Balanced curricula give students the best chance in the job market."],"wc":266},{"id":"education5","topic":"education","type":"Opinion","key":false,"q":"Students should only focus on the subjects they excel at. Do you agree or disagree?","ideas":["A broad education prepares students for a changing job market","Students often discover their talents by trying different subjects"],"ex":["For example, in Germany children are directed to different school types at an early age, and this system is often criticised for forcing teenagers to choose their path too soon.","For instance, many famous scientists and artists began with a general education and found their field later, such as Leonardo da Vinci, who studied both art and anatomy."],"essay":["It is suggested that pupils should concentrate only on the subjects in which they perform best. This essay disagrees with this idea because a broad education prepares students for a changing job market, and because talents are often discovered by chance.","The primary reason is that a broad education prepares students for a changing job market. This is because employers' needs change quickly, so people who know only one field may struggle to adapt, while those with general knowledge can learn new skills more easily. As a result, wide knowledge gives them security and more options in their career. For example, in Germany children are directed to different school types at an early age, and this system is often criticised for forcing teenagers to choose their path too soon.","Another important point is that students often discover their talents by trying different subjects. That is to say, a pupil who thinks that they are weak in art or science may find a passion after a good teacher or a project, and narrow specialisation would never give them this chance. Of course, advanced students should receive extra challenges, but not at the cost of other subjects. For instance, many famous scientists and artists began with a general education and found their field later, such as Leonardo da Vinci, who studied both art and anatomy.","In conclusion, this essay disagrees that students should only study the subjects they are good at because a broad education provides flexibility and helps them to discover new abilities. Specialisation should come later, at university. This approach protects students from choosing too early."],"wc":266},{"id":"education6","topic":"education","type":"Opinion","key":false,"q":"Some people think the best way to get a good job is to have a university education. To what extent do you agree?","ideas":["A university education gives access to many well-paid professions","Practical training can also lead to a good job"],"ex":["For example, nobody can work as a doctor without finishing medical school.","For instance, in the United States student debt exceeds 1.7 trillion dollars, whereas Germany's dual system combines school with paid work in a company and leads to stable jobs."],"essay":["It is often believed that a degree is the most reliable path to a well-paid career. This essay partly agrees with this view because university graduates generally earn more, although vocational training can be equally valuable.","The primary reason is that a university education gives access to many well-paid professions. This is because careers such as medicine, law and engineering legally require a degree, and graduates on average earn more over their lives than people without one. As a result, a degree opens doors that would otherwise remain closed. In addition, many employers use a degree as a first filter when they select applicants. For example, nobody can work as a doctor without finishing medical school.","Another important point is that practical training can also lead to a good job. That is to say, degrees are expensive, and many graduates start working with large debts, while apprentices earn money while they learn and gain real experience. Therefore, a university is not the only road to success, and the best option may be to combine study and work. Employers also value practical experience, which apprentices gain from the start. For instance, in the United States student debt exceeds 1.7 trillion dollars, whereas Germany's dual system combines school with paid work in a company and leads to stable jobs.","In conclusion, this essay partly agrees that university is the best way to get a good job because many careers require a degree, but apprenticeships also offer good prospects without large debts. Students should choose the route that suits their goals. Governments should therefore support both routes equally."],"wc":265},{"id":"education7","topic":"education","type":"Opinion","key":false,"q":"Schools should give children more homework to help their academic learning. Do you think this is a good idea?","ideas":["Excessive homework creates stress and takes time from family life","Homework increases inequality and brings only limited benefits"],"ex":["For example, Finland gives relatively little homework, yet its pupils achieve excellent results in international tests.","For instance, the researcher Harris Cooper of Duke University found only weak benefits of homework at primary level."],"essay":["Some people argue that teachers should assign more homework in order to improve results. This essay disagrees with this idea because too much homework causes stress and inequality, and because its academic benefit is limited, especially for younger pupils.","The primary reason is that excessive homework creates stress and takes time from family life. This is because children who spend their evenings on exercises have little time to rest, play or talk to their parents, and tiredness reduces learning at school the next day. As a result, some pupils begin to dislike education. Moreover, children need time with friends, which supports their emotional development. Tired pupils also remember less of what they learned during the day. For example, Finland gives relatively little homework, yet its pupils achieve excellent results in international tests.","Another important point is that homework increases inequality and brings only limited benefits. That is to say, pupils with quiet rooms and helpful parents complete tasks more easily than those without support, so the difference between rich and poor widens. It is true that homework builds responsibility, but short and well-planned tasks achieve this just as well. Teachers can also check whether tasks are useful before assigning them. For instance, the researcher Harris Cooper of Duke University found only weak benefits of homework at primary level.","In conclusion, this essay disagrees that schools should give more homework because it causes stress and inequality and has limited academic value. Quality is more important than quantity, so teachers should set fewer but more meaningful tasks. Schools should focus on learning, not on workload."],"wc":263},{"id":"education8","topic":"education","type":"Discussion","key":false,"q":"As children grow up they spend time both at school and at home. Do you think parents or teachers influence a child most?","ideas":["Teachers have a strong influence on children's knowledge and social skills","Parents influence a child most because they are the first role models"],"ex":["For example, in England civic education has been compulsory since 2002, and teachers use it to explain rights, rules and responsibilities.","For instance, children whose parents read regularly often become readers themselves, while the British programme Bookstart tries to help parents to start this habit with babies."],"essay":["Children spend their days between school and home, and some people believe that teachers shape them more, while others think parents have the stronger influence. This essay agrees with the second view because parents are the first and constant role models.","On the one hand, teachers have a strong influence on children's knowledge and social skills. This is because pupils spend many hours a day at school, where teachers introduce new subjects, correct mistakes and teach cooperation with other children. A good teacher can inspire a lifelong passion for a subject. However, I believe that this influence depends on the values already learned at home. Moreover, pupils trust their teachers and often follow their advice. For example, in England civic education has been compulsory since 2002, and teachers use it to explain rights, rules and responsibilities.","On the other hand, parents influence a child most because they are the first role models. That is to say, children copy the language, attitudes and habits that they see at home from the earliest age, and these patterns stay for life. Parents also spend years with their children, whereas teachers change every year. In addition, parents decide the daily routines that shape a child's character. For instance, children whose parents read regularly often become readers themselves, while the British programme Bookstart tries to help parents to start this habit with babies.","In conclusion, although teachers play an important role, this essay believes that parents influence children more because they provide the first and most lasting examples. Schools can only be successful when they work together with families. Teachers can improve this influence by communicating with parents regularly."],"wc":274},{"id":"education9","topic":"education","type":"Discussion","key":false,"q":"Some people think schools should teach children right from wrong, while other people think this responsibility should fall on the parents. Discuss both sides and give your opinion.","ideas":["Schools should teach right from wrong because not every child learns values at home","Parents are the first teachers of values"],"ex":["For example, civic education has been compulsory in England since 2002, and Japan introduced moral education as an official primary school subject in 2018.","For instance, many schools organise meetings with parents to discuss behaviour rules so that both sides give a consistent message."],"essay":["Some people believe that moral education belongs in schools, while others think that it is the duty of parents. This essay believes that both have a role, but that schools should support what children learn at home.","On the one hand, schools should teach right from wrong because not every child learns values at home. This is because some parents work long hours or lack the knowledge to explain ethical questions, and schools can reach every child equally. Lessons on citizenship also explain the rules of society in an organised way, which helps children understand how to live with others. Teachers can also use stories and discussions to practise honest behaviour. For example, civic education has been compulsory in England since 2002, and Japan introduced moral education as an official primary school subject in 2018. However, schools cannot do this alone.","On the other hand, parents are the first teachers of values. That is to say, children learn honesty, respect and kindness by watching how their family behaves every day, and a school lesson cannot replace this example. If parents and teachers send different messages, children become confused, so cooperation is necessary. In addition, parents decide the family traditions that give meaning to moral rules. For instance, many schools organise meetings with parents to discuss behaviour rules so that both sides give a consistent message.","In conclusion, although schools can teach moral values to all children, this essay believes that parents remain the first and most important teachers of right and wrong. A partnership between the two is the most effective approach. A strong partnership benefits every child."],"wc":268},{"id":"education10","topic":"education","type":"Opinion","key":false,"q":"Education should be free for everyone. To what extent do you agree?","ideas":["Free education gives everyone an equal chance","Society as a whole benefits from educated citizens"],"ex":["For example, the Nordic countries offer free education, and Germany abolished tuition fees at public universities in 2014.","For instance, in Romania many students study on state-funded places, which allows young people from modest families to obtain a degree."],"essay":["It is argued that every person should be able to study without paying fees. This essay agrees with this statement because free education guarantees equal opportunities, and because it benefits the whole society.","The primary reason is that free education gives everyone an equal chance. This is because tuition fees prevent talented students from poor families from studying, and a society wastes the abilities of these people. When all children can attend school and university, success depends on effort and ability rather than on family income. In addition, free education reduces the number of young people who leave school early because of money. Equal access also reduces social tension in the long term. For example, the Nordic countries offer free education, and Germany abolished tuition fees at public universities in 2014.","Another important point is that society as a whole benefits from educated citizens. That is to say, graduates earn more and pay higher taxes, and an educated population has lower unemployment and better health, so the money spent returns to the state. It is true that free education raises costs for taxpayers, but the long-term benefits are larger. Furthermore, a skilled workforce attracts companies and investment to the country. For instance, in Romania many students study on state-funded places, which allows young people from modest families to obtain a degree.","In conclusion, this essay agrees that education should be free because it creates equal opportunities and brings lasting benefits to society. Governments should therefore consider education as an investment, not as an expense. This is why many countries consider education a basic right."],"wc":264},{"id":"education11","topic":"education","type":"Opinion","key":false,"q":"Some people think that history is a useful subject to learn, while others think it is a waste of time. What do you think?","ideas":["History teaches us to learn from past mistakes","History develops critical thinking and analysis"],"ex":["For example, after the Second World War, European countries created cooperation that became the European Union to prevent another conflict, and German schools teach pupils in detail about the Holocaust.","For instance, the World Economic Forum lists analytical thinking among the most important skills for the future of work."],"essay":["It is debated whether the study of the past has any value in modern life. This essay believes that history is a very useful subject because it helps societies to avoid repeating mistakes, and because it develops critical thinking.","The primary reason is that history teaches us to learn from past mistakes. This is because by studying wars, crises and political decisions, students see the causes and consequences of events, and this knowledge helps them to avoid similar errors. As a result, societies that remember their past are better prepared to protect peace and democracy. Furthermore, the study of history helps citizens to understand why their country has its current borders and laws. For example, after the Second World War, European countries created cooperation that became the European Union to prevent another conflict, and German schools teach pupils in detail about the Holocaust.","Another important point is that history develops critical thinking and analysis. That is to say, students must compare sources, decide which ones are reliable and build arguments from evidence, and these are skills that employers value in every profession. Some people say that dates are boring, but good teaching focuses on understanding, not memorisation. Students who analyse sources are also less easily misled by false news. For instance, the World Economic Forum lists analytical thinking among the most important skills for the future of work.","In conclusion, this essay believes that history is a valuable subject because it prevents repeated mistakes and teaches analytical thinking. Schools should keep it in the curriculum and teach it in an interesting way. A nation that knows its past can plan its future more wisely."],"wc":274},{"id":"education12","topic":"education","type":"Problem / Solution","key":false,"q":"Education in rural areas is often not as good as in cities. Why is that? What do you think can be done about it?","ideas":["Rural schools lack qualified teachers, equipment and internet access","Attract teachers with bonuses and to use technology and transport"],"ex":["For example, in Romania the dropout rate from school is higher in rural areas than in cities.","For instance, several countries offer salary bonuses for rural teachers, and during the pandemic many villages used online platforms to continue classes."],"essay":["Schools in the countryside frequently offer a lower standard of education than those in cities. The main causes of this are a shortage of teachers and poor infrastructure, and the most effective solutions are financial incentives for teachers and online learning.","The main cause is that rural schools lack qualified teachers, equipment and internet access. This is because many teachers prefer to live in cities, where salaries and living conditions are better, and villages cannot afford modern equipment. In addition, poor roads make it difficult for children to reach school. As a result, rural pupils receive fewer lessons and fewer opportunities. In some villages, children must travel long distances on bad roads every day. Many young families leave villages for this reason. For example, in Romania the dropout rate from school is higher in rural areas than in cities.","A viable solution is to attract teachers with bonuses and to use technology and transport. If governments pay extra to teachers who work in villages and provide housing, more of them will accept these jobs, and school buses together with online lessons help to reach isolated pupils. These measures reduce the gap between city and countryside. In the long term, stronger rural schools also reduce migration to big cities. For instance, several countries offer salary bonuses for rural teachers, and during the pandemic many villages used online platforms to continue classes.","In conclusion, rural education is weaker because of a lack of teachers and resources, but incentives, transport and online learning can improve it. Equal education for all children benefits the entire country. Both governments and local communities should take part."],"wc":270},{"id":"education13","topic":"education","type":"Problem / Solution","key":false,"q":"Many children think school is boring, which impacts their ability to focus and negatively impacts their academic performance. What causes this problem? What solutions can you suggest for this problem?","ideas":["The main cause of boredom is passive teaching that is not connected to real life","Moreover, the same lesson may be repeated for years without any new challenge","Introduce interactive, project-based learning"],"ex":["For example, many teachers still rely on long lectures and textbook exercises, even though young children can concentrate for only a short time.","For instance, Finnish schools use project-based and phenomenon-based learning, and their pupils are among the best in the world."],"essay":["A large number of pupils find school uninteresting, and this lowers their concentration and results. The main causes of this problem are passive teaching methods and irrelevant lessons, and the most viable solutions are project-based learning and interactive activities.","The main cause of boredom is passive teaching that is not connected to real life. This is because pupils who only listen and copy notes for hours lose interest quickly, especially when they cannot see why a subject is useful. As a result, they stop paying attention, make more mistakes and gradually lose confidence.","Moreover, the same lesson may be repeated for years without any new challenge. Unmotivated pupils may also disturb their classmates. For example, many teachers still rely on long lectures and textbook exercises, even though young children can concentrate for only a short time.","A viable solution is to introduce interactive, project-based learning. If students work on practical projects, solve real problems and use games and technology, they become active participants and see the purpose of what they learn. Teachers can also vary the activities during the lesson to keep attention high. In this way, children learn that school is connected to their own interests. Short breaks and movement activities can also help. For instance, Finnish schools use project-based and phenomenon-based learning, and their pupils are among the best in the world.","In conclusion, school becomes boring because lessons are passive and unrelated to life, but interactive projects and varied activities can restore motivation. Interested pupils concentrate better and achieve higher results. Teachers who enjoy their subject can make an enormous difference."],"wc":264},{"id":"education14","topic":"education","type":"Advantages / Disadvantages","key":false,"q":"Having a gap year between school and university is a popular choice for many students. Do you think this is a positive trend?","ideas":["A gap year is positive because it helps students to mature and to gain experience","Moreover, they often improve their language skills while living abroad"],"ex":["For example, many school leavers in the UK and Australia spend a year working or volunteering abroad before starting their degree.","For instance, a university can offer a deferred place, which guarantees that the student can return after the gap year."],"essay":["Many young people choose to take a year off before entering university. This essay believes that this is a positive trend because it brings maturity and money, although it carries the risk of losing study habits.","A gap year is positive because it helps students to mature and to gain experience. This is because young people who work or travel for a year learn to be independent, meet different cultures and discover what they truly want to study. In addition, many save money, which reduces the need for loans when their studies begin. As a result, they often return more motivated.","Moreover, they often improve their language skills while living abroad. For example, many school leavers in the UK and Australia spend a year working or volunteering abroad before starting their degree. One drawback is that some students lose their rhythm and may not return to education. In other words, after a year of freedom it can be difficult to restart a routine of study, and some young people find a job that they do not want to leave. Furthermore, friends who started earlier will graduate sooner. However, a clear plan can reduce this risk. Some students also spend the money they saved on a holiday instead of on their studies. For instance, a university can offer a deferred place, which guarantees that the student can return after the gap year.","In conclusion, a gap year is a positive trend because it develops maturity and independence, although students must plan well to avoid losing momentum. With clear goals, the benefits outweigh the risks. Universities can support students by offering flexible start dates."],"wc":272},{"id":"education15","topic":"education","type":"Advantages / Disadvantages","key":false,"q":"Some schools encourage children to do volunteer work. What are the advantages and disadvantages of this?","ideas":["It develops empathy and social skills","Volunteering adds pressure and may lose its true meaning"],"ex":["For example, the US state of Maryland requires students to complete service-learning hours in order to graduate.","For instance, some students in schools with compulsory hours choose the easiest task just to complete the number required."],"essay":["In some countries, pupils are urged to help the community as volunteers. The main advantages of this practice are the development of empathy and social skills, whereas the main disadvantages are the extra workload and the risk that volunteering stops being voluntary.","The main advantage of school volunteering is that it develops empathy and social skills. This is because pupils who help others, for example by visiting elderly people or cleaning a park, understand the problems of their community and learn to cooperate. As a result, they become more responsible citizens, and they also gain experience that helps them in later applications. Many schools also report that volunteers become more confident when speaking to adults. Pupils also discover possible careers in healthcare or education. For example, the US state of Maryland requires students to complete service-learning hours in order to graduate.","One major disadvantage is that volunteering adds pressure and may lose its true meaning. In other words, pupils who already have a heavy timetable may feel tired, and if the activity is compulsory, they may do it only to collect hours without real interest. Moreover, children from poor families may have less time because they help at home. Teachers may also find it difficult to supervise so many separate projects. For instance, some students in schools with compulsory hours choose the easiest task just to complete the number required.","In conclusion, volunteering in schools builds empathy and teamwork, but it can increase workload and become a formality. Schools should therefore offer a free choice of projects and a reasonable number of hours. Voluntary participation keeps the activity meaningful."],"wc":269},{"id":"culture1","topic":"culture","type":"Discussion","key":true,"q":"Nearly all cities have museums or art galleries. Some people think they are a waste of funds, others disagree. Discuss both sides and give your opinion.","ideas":["Museums are expensive, and critics argue that the money could be spent on more urgent services","Museums are an investment rather than a waste"],"ex":["For example, the cost of running a large national museum can reach tens of millions of pounds each year.","For instance, national museums in the UK have offered free entry since 2001, and they attract millions of visitors every year, including many foreign tourists."],"essay":["Almost every city has at least one museum or gallery, and some people believe that these institutions waste public money, while others disagree. This essay agrees with the second view because museums educate people and attract tourists.","On the one hand, museums are expensive, and critics argue that the money could be spent on more urgent services. This is because buildings, security and experts cost large amounts every year, and many local residents rarely visit. Therefore, some taxpayers feel that hospitals or schools should receive the money first, especially since many collections can now be viewed online. However, I believe that this argument underestimates the value of museums. For example, the cost of running a large national museum can reach tens of millions of pounds each year.","On the other hand, museums are an investment rather than a waste. That is to say, they preserve national heritage, teach children about history and science, and bring tourists who spend money in local shops, hotels and restaurants. In this way, much of the cost returns to the city through taxes and jobs. A virtual visit can never replace seeing real objects. Many museums also work as research centres where experts study history and science. For instance, national museums in the UK have offered free entry since 2001, and they attract millions of visitors every year, including many foreign tourists.","In conclusion, although museums have high costs, this essay believes that they are worth the money because they protect heritage, educate people and support tourism. Governments should keep funding them and look for sponsors to share the cost. Sponsors and ticket income can reduce the burden on taxpayers."],"wc":275},{"id":"culture2","topic":"culture","type":"Opinion","key":true,"q":"Social media has brought with it globalisation. Do you think it is a positive or negative trend?","ideas":["Globalisation through social media encourages cultural exchange","The risk of cultural uniformity can be managed"],"ex":["For example, the South Korean film Parasite won the Oscar for Best Picture in 2020, and the series Squid Game became a global phenomenon in 2021, both helped by online sharing.","For instance, a Starbucks café in the Forbidden City in Beijing closed in 2007 after protests, which shows that local communities can defend their culture."],"essay":["It is said that online networks have connected cultures all around the world and have created a global society. This essay believes that this is a positive trend because it encourages cultural exchange, although it also carries a risk of losing local identity.","The primary reason is that globalisation through social media encourages cultural exchange. This is because people can watch, share and comment on films, music and traditions from other countries at no cost, and this makes them more curious and tolerant. As a result, local artists can reach worldwide audiences, which would be impossible without these platforms. In this way, ordinary users become cultural ambassadors for their own countries. For example, the South Korean film Parasite won the Oscar for Best Picture in 2020, and the series Squid Game became a global phenomenon in 2021, both helped by online sharing.","Another important point is that the risk of cultural uniformity can be managed. That is to say, critics fear that global chains and trends replace local businesses and customs, but communities can protect their identity through education and support for local products. These dangers are therefore smaller than the benefits of connection. Local governments can also fund festivals that promote regional food, music and crafts. For instance, a Starbucks café in the Forbidden City in Beijing closed in 2007 after protests, which shows that local communities can defend their culture.","In conclusion, this essay believes that globalisation through social media is positive because it promotes understanding between cultures, and its risks can be reduced by protecting local traditions. Governments should therefore support both openness and heritage. Cooperation between countries will then be easier."],"wc":275},{"id":"culture3","topic":"culture","type":"Two-part Question","key":true,"q":"Traditional lifestyles are slowly being forgotten. Do you think this is a bad thing? What can be done about it?","ideas":["It is a bad thing that traditional lifestyles are being forgotten because they preserve a community's identity"],"ex":["For example, Horezu pottery and the traditional Romanian blouse, the ia, are both included in UNESCO's list of intangible cultural heritage.","For instance, Japan supports its master craftspeople through the \"Living National Treasures\" system, which pays them to train apprentices."],"essay":["In many countries, old customs and ways of living are gradually disappearing. This essay believes that this is a bad thing because traditions carry a community's identity, and it will suggest that education and festivals are the best way to keep them alive.","It is a bad thing that traditional lifestyles are being forgotten because they preserve a community's identity. This is because customs, crafts and clothing show where people come from and what values they share, so when these disappear, young people lose a sense of belonging. Moreover, a world in which every place looks the same would be less interesting for everyone. As a result, older customs are often replaced by global habits. For example, Horezu pottery and the traditional Romanian blouse, the ia, are both included in UNESCO's list of intangible cultural heritage. This trend can be reversed through education, festivals and support for craftspeople. If schools teach traditional songs and crafts, children will value them, and if local festivals and cultural tourism create income, craftspeople will be able to continue their work. In addition, digital archives can record knowledge that might otherwise be lost. Young volunteers can also record the stories of older people for future generations. For instance, Japan supports its master craftspeople through the \"Living National Treasures\" system, which pays them to train apprentices.","In conclusion, the disappearance of traditional lifestyles is a loss because they carry identity, but schools, festivals and financial support can keep them alive. Every community should treat its customs as a valuable inheritance. In this way, the past will remain part of the future."],"wc":265},{"id":"culture4","topic":"culture","type":"Two-part Question","key":false,"q":"Tourism to remote areas in the world is becoming more popular. Why is this? What impact does this have on local cultures?","ideas":["Remote tourism is growing because travel is cheaper and people want authentic experiences"],"ex":["For example, Bhutan, a small mountain country, attracts thousands of visitors who want to experience its landscapes and Buddhist culture.","For instance, Maya Bay in Thailand was closed from 2018 to 2022 so that nature could recover, and Machu Picchu has daily visitor limits."],"essay":["More and more travellers are visiting distant and isolated places. This is mainly due to cheaper flights and the influence of social media, and its effect on local cultures is mixed, because it brings income but also pollution and commercialisation.","Remote tourism is growing because travel is cheaper and people want authentic experiences. This is because low-cost flights make distant destinations affordable, and photos on Instagram show unusual landscapes that inspire others to visit. In addition, many travellers are tired of crowded cities and want to see nature and traditional life. As a result, ecotourism has become a fashionable choice. Furthermore, television documentaries and travel blogs make remote regions look more attractive. For example, Bhutan, a small mountain country, attracts thousands of visitors who want to experience its landscapes and Buddhist culture. The impact on local cultures is both positive and negative. That is to say, visitors bring income that can support villages and encourage people to keep their traditions, but large numbers of tourists also cause waste, damage to nature and the commercialisation of culture. Rituals can become shows made only for money. Therefore, careful limits are necessary. Local guides who explain the culture respectfully can also reduce harm. For instance, Maya Bay in Thailand was closed from 2018 to 2022 so that nature could recover, and Machu Picchu has daily visitor limits.","In conclusion, remote destinations are popular because of cheap travel and the search for authenticity, and the effect on local cultures depends on how well tourism is controlled. Visitor limits and ecotourism can protect both nature and traditions. Both visitors and residents should benefit from this growth."],"wc":271},{"id":"culture5","topic":"culture","type":"Two-part Question","key":false,"q":"Some people think the world will one day have only one language. To what extent do you agree? Would this be a good thing?","ideas":["The primary reason why one world language is unlikely is that translation technology and local pride keep many languages alive","A single language would not be a good thing, as it would mean a loss of identity"],"ex":["For example, the Maori language has been strengthened by \"language nests\" for small children since 1982, and Welsh is taught to all pupils in Wales.","For instance, UNESCO estimates that around 40 per cent of the world's languages are in danger of disappearing, and apps such as Duolingo now offer courses in Hawaiian and Navajo to help them survive."],"essay":["It is sometimes predicted that all humans will eventually speak the same language. This essay disagrees with this prediction because technology reduces the need for a single language, and it believes that such a change would not be a good thing, since it would destroy cultural identity.","The primary reason why one world language is unlikely is that translation technology and local pride keep many languages alive. This is because automatic translation allows people to communicate without sharing a language, and communities increasingly fight to protect their own. Although some languages are disappearing because of migration and economic pressure, others are being revived through education. For example, the Maori language has been strengthened by \"language nests\" for small children since 1982, and Welsh is taught to all pupils in Wales.","Another important point is that a single language would not be a good thing, as it would mean a loss of identity. That is to say, every language carries a unique way of thinking, stories and traditions, so losing languages would impoverish humanity. It is true that one language would make trade and travel simpler, but this advantage does not justify the loss of cultural diversity. For instance, UNESCO estimates that around 40 per cent of the world's languages are in danger of disappearing, and apps such as Duolingo now offer courses in Hawaiian and Navajo to help them survive.","In conclusion, this essay disagrees that the world will have only one language because of technology and revival efforts, and it believes that this would be a loss for humanity. Governments should therefore support minority languages in schools and online."],"wc":270},{"id":"culture6","topic":"culture","type":"Two-part Question","key":false,"q":"All countries around the world celebrate national holidays. What role do they play? How important are they?","ideas":["National holidays play the role of uniting people and preserving collective memory"],"ex":["For example, in Romania, 1 December, the Great Union Day, brings people together to remember the union of Transylvania with Romania in 1918.","For instance, many countries organise concerts and public events on their national day, and they attract large numbers of residents and visitors who spend time together."],"essay":["Every country has special days on which citizens remember important events or celebrate their nation. This essay will explain that these holidays mainly create unity and preserve historical memory, and that they are important for national identity, although they can be too commercial.","National holidays play the role of uniting people and preserving collective memory. This is because on these days people from different backgrounds share the same ceremonies, parades and traditions, and this creates a feeling of belonging. In addition, children learn about key events in their history, so important lessons are not forgotten. As a result, many citizens feel proud of their country on these days. For example, in Romania, 1 December, the Great Union Day, brings people together to remember the union of Transylvania with Romania in 1918. National holidays are important because they strengthen identity and give people time to rest. That is to say, they remind citizens of what they have in common, which is especially valuable in diverse societies, and they give families a break from work. However, their meaning can be weakened if shops and advertisers use them only to sell products. Public holidays also encourage tourism, since many people travel to attend local events. For instance, many countries organise concerts and public events on their national day, and they attract large numbers of residents and visitors who spend time together.","In conclusion, national holidays unite citizens and keep history alive, and they are important for identity and social harmony. Governments should protect their original meaning and limit excessive commercialisation. In this way, they remain a valuable tradition for future generations."],"wc":268},{"id":"culture7","topic":"culture","type":"Opinion","key":false,"q":"People should be encouraged to take holidays in their own country to boost the economy. To what extent do you agree?","ideas":["Domestic holidays support the local economy","Staying at home reduces the environmental impact of travel"],"ex":["For example, during the \"staycation boom\" of 2020, many British holiday towns recovered strongly because residents could not travel abroad.","For instance, many countries promote discounted train tickets and local holiday vouchers in order to attract domestic tourists."],"essay":["It is suggested that citizens should spend their holidays at home rather than abroad in order to help the national economy. This essay partly agrees with this idea because domestic holidays support local businesses and reduce pollution, although travelling abroad should remain a free choice.","The primary reason is that domestic holidays support the local economy. This is because money spent in hotels, restaurants and shops stays within the country and pays the salaries of local workers. As a result, regions that depend on tourism, especially rural areas, can create jobs and avoid depopulation. Domestic tourism also helps seasonal workers to find work for a longer period of the year. For example, during the \"staycation boom\" of 2020, many British holiday towns recovered strongly because residents could not travel abroad.","Another important point is that staying at home reduces the environmental impact of travel. That is to say, short journeys by train or car produce much less pollution than long flights, so domestic tourism helps to fight climate change. However, governments should encourage rather than force people, because foreign travel offers cultural exchange and personal growth. In addition, travel inside the country is easier to organise and cheaper for families with children. For instance, many countries promote discounted train tickets and local holiday vouchers in order to attract domestic tourists.","In conclusion, this essay agrees that people should be encouraged to holiday in their own countries because it helps the economy and the environment, but freedom of choice must be respected. Incentives are better than rules. Therefore, a balanced policy works better than a ban on foreign trips."],"wc":268},{"id":"crime1","topic":"crime","type":"Discussion","key":true,"q":"It would be quicker if there was the same punishment for the same crimes, but some people think this is not a good policy because circumstances should be considered. Discuss both sides and give your opinion.","ideas":["The same punishment for the same crime brings consistency and speed","Circumstances matter, so sentences must be flexible"],"ex":["For example, in 1994 California introduced the \"three strikes\" law, which gave very long sentences automatically, and it was widely criticised and partly reformed in 2012.","For instance, many courts reduce sentences for first-time offenders who show regret and are willing to repay the victim."],"essay":["Some people believe that identical crimes should always receive identical sentences, while others argue that judges must take the circumstances into account. This essay agrees with the second view because fairness is more important than speed.","On the one hand, the same punishment for the same crime brings consistency and speed. This is because fixed sentences make the law predictable, so criminals know exactly what the consequence will be, and courts can finish cases faster because they do not need long discussions. It also prevents judges from treating people differently because of their wealth or background. However, I believe that this system is too rigid. For example, in 1994 California introduced the \"three strikes\" law, which gave very long sentences automatically, and it was widely criticised and partly reformed in 2012.","On the other hand, circumstances matter, so sentences must be flexible. That is to say, two people may commit the same act for very different reasons: one may steal food to feed a hungry child, while another steals for profit. A fair justice system must consider motive, age, mental health and previous behaviour, or it will punish innocent circumstances as if they were crimes. Such flexibility also lets judges choose the punishment that is most likely to prevent new crimes. For instance, many courts reduce sentences for first-time offenders who show regret and are willing to repay the victim.","In conclusion, although identical punishments are quicker and more consistent, this essay believes that judges should consider each case individually because justice depends on circumstances. Clear guidelines can still limit unfair differences."],"wc":262},{"id":"crime2","topic":"crime","type":"Problem / Solution","key":true,"q":"Too many criminals leave prison only to reoffend. Why is this? What measures can be introduced to solve this problem?","ideas":["Released prisoners find it hard to rebuild a normal life","Focus on rehabilitation, education and employment support"],"ex":["For example, in the United States a majority of former prisoners are arrested again within a few years of release.","For instance, Norway has one of the lowest reoffending rates in the world, at around 20 per cent, with prisons that concentrate on education and vocational training, and several American states have adopted \"Ban the Box\" policies that remove the question about criminal records from job applications."],"essay":["A large proportion of former prisoners commit new crimes after their release. This happens mainly because they face stigma and have difficulty finding work, and the most effective measures are rehabilitation programmes and help with employment.","The main cause of reoffending is that released prisoners find it hard to rebuild a normal life. This is because employers often refuse to hire people with a criminal record, and many ex-prisoners return to the same neighbourhood and friends. Without a job or a stable home, some see crime as the only way to survive. In addition, prisons that offer little education leave them without useful skills. For example, in the United States a majority of former prisoners are arrested again within a few years of release.","A viable solution is to focus on rehabilitation, education and employment support. If prisoners learn a profession during their sentence and receive mentoring and help to find a job after release, they have a real alternative to crime. This approach also saves money in the long term, because fewer people return to prison. For instance, Norway has one of the lowest reoffending rates in the world, at around 20 per cent, with prisons that concentrate on education and vocational training, and several American states have adopted \"Ban the Box\" policies that remove the question about criminal records from job applications.","In conclusion, many prisoners reoffend because of stigma and a lack of work, but rehabilitation and job support can break this cycle. These measures make society safer and prisons less crowded. In this way, former prisoners can become useful members of the community."],"wc":267},{"id":"crime3","topic":"crime","type":"Problem / Solution","key":false,"q":"Crime rates are increasing in many cities. Why is this? How can this be tackled?","ideas":["The main cause of rising crime in cities is poverty, unemployment and inequality","Combine stronger community policing with social investment"],"ex":["For example, before its transformation, Medellín in Colombia had one of the highest murder rates in the world, with many violent neighbourhoods suffering from extreme poverty.","For instance, Medellín built cable cars, libraries and public spaces in its poorest districts, and violence fell significantly."],"essay":["In a number of urban areas, more and more offences are being committed. The main causes of this problem are poverty and unemployment, and the most viable solutions are community policing and programmes that create opportunities for young people.","The main cause of rising crime in cities is poverty, unemployment and inequality. This is because people who cannot find work or who see huge differences in wealth around them may turn to theft or drugs to earn money. In addition, big cities are anonymous, so criminals feel less likely to be recognised or stopped by neighbours. As a result, crime grows fastest in poor and crowded districts. Over time, fear of crime also pushes businesses and families to leave these areas, which makes poverty even worse. For example, before its transformation, Medellín in Colombia had one of the highest murder rates in the world, with many violent neighbourhoods suffering from extreme poverty.","A viable solution is to combine stronger community policing with social investment. If officers work closely with residents and cities provide better lighting, youth clubs and job programmes, young people have fewer reasons to commit crimes and neighbours watch out for each other. This addresses the roots of the problem instead of only punishing offenders. For instance, Medellín built cable cars, libraries and public spaces in its poorest districts, and violence fell significantly.","In conclusion, crime in cities increases because of poverty, unemployment and anonymity, but community policing and social programmes can reduce it. Governments should therefore invest in prevention as well as in the police. This balanced approach is more effective than harsh punishment alone."],"wc":268},{"id":"crime4","topic":"crime","type":"Opinion","key":true,"q":"Some people think violent films encourage criminal behaviour. To what extent do you agree?","ideas":["Research has not proved a clear link between violent films and criminal behaviour","Crime is caused mainly by social and personal conditions"],"ex":["For example, a 2015 analysis by the American Psychological Association linked violent video games to aggression, but it found no sufficient evidence of a link to criminal violence.","For instance, age ratings and parental supervision already limit what children can watch in cinemas and on television."],"essay":["It is argued that violent movies make viewers more likely to commit crimes. This essay disagrees with this view to a large extent because the evidence is mixed, and because other factors, such as family and social conditions, are far more important.","The primary reason is that research has not proved a clear link between violent films and criminal behaviour. This is because millions of people watch violent films without ever becoming violent, which suggests that a film alone cannot turn a normal person into a criminal. Some studies find more aggression after violent media, but this is a short-term feeling, not real violence. Moreover, violent films are only one small part of the huge amount of media that young people consume. For example, a 2015 analysis by the American Psychological Association linked violent video games to aggression, but it found no sufficient evidence of a link to criminal violence.","Another important point is that crime is caused mainly by social and personal conditions. That is to say, poverty, an unstable family and bad friends influence behaviour much more than entertainment does, so blaming films ignores the real causes. It is true that young children can be disturbed by violent scenes, but this problem can be solved by sensible rules. For instance, age ratings and parental supervision already limit what children can watch in cinemas and on television.","In conclusion, this essay disagrees that violent films encourage crime because research shows no clear link and other factors matter more. Age ratings and parental guidance are the best way to protect young viewers."],"wc":262},{"id":"crime5","topic":"crime","type":"Discussion","key":false,"q":"Some teenagers commit serious crime. Some people think they should get the same punishment as adults, while others disagree. Discuss both sides and give your opinion.","ideas":["Serious crimes have real victims, and the punishment must reflect the harm","Teenagers should be treated differently because their brains are still developing"],"ex":["For example, in many countries the families of victims demand that violent teenagers receive the same sentences as adults.","For instance, in 2005 the US Supreme Court abolished the death penalty for offenders under 18, recognising that young people are less responsible for their actions than adults."],"essay":["Some people believe that teenagers who commit serious crimes should receive the same sentences as adults, while others disagree. This essay agrees with the second view because young offenders can still change, and a separate system is fairer.","On the one hand, serious crimes have real victims, and the punishment must reflect the harm. This is because a murder or a serious assault causes the same suffering whatever the age of the offender, and tough sentences may discourage other young people from committing similar acts. Victims and their families also expect justice. However, I believe that this argument ignores how young people develop. For example, in many countries the families of victims demand that violent teenagers receive the same sentences as adults.","On the other hand, teenagers should be treated differently because their brains are still developing. That is to say, adolescents are more impulsive and easier to influence than adults, and with education and counselling they have a high chance of changing. Putting them in adult prisons may only teach them to become better criminals, so rehabilitation in a separate system is more effective. With support, many of them become responsible adults. For instance, in 2005 the US Supreme Court abolished the death penalty for offenders under 18, recognising that young people are less responsible for their actions than adults.","In conclusion, although serious crimes deserve serious consequences, this essay believes that teenagers should not receive adult punishments because they can be rehabilitated. A separate youth system is the best compromise. In this way, society protects both victims and the future of young people."],"wc":265},{"id":"crime6","topic":"crime","type":"Two-part Question","key":false,"q":"Why are crime shows so popular on TV? Do you think this is a positive or negative trend?","ideas":["Crime shows are popular because they combine curiosity, mystery and emotion in a safe environment","Negative because it can give a false picture of crime and of justice"],"ex":["For example, the American series CSI attracted tens of millions of viewers at its peak and inspired many spin-offs.","For instance, lawyers and judges use the term \"CSI effect\" to describe jurors who expect spectacular forensic evidence in every trial."],"essay":["Programmes about detectives and criminals attract huge audiences around the world. This is mainly due to curiosity and the desire to solve a puzzle, and it should be seen as a mostly negative trend because it can create an exaggerated fear of crime.","Crime shows are popular because they combine curiosity, mystery and emotion in a safe environment. This is because viewers enjoy trying to solve the puzzle before the detective does, and they can feel excitement and fear while sitting comfortably at home. In addition, the stories explore human motives, which people find fascinating. As a result, these programmes fill prime-time schedules on many channels. For example, the American series CSI attracted tens of millions of viewers at its peak and inspired many spin-offs.","This trend is negative because it can give a false picture of crime and of justice. That is to say, constant violence on screen can make people believe that the world is more dangerous than it really is, and it can make jurors expect unrealistic scientific evidence in real courtrooms. On the positive side, such shows raise awareness of safety, but this benefit is smaller than the harm. For instance, lawyers and judges use the term \"CSI effect\" to describe jurors who expect spectacular forensic evidence in every trial.","In conclusion, crime shows are popular because of curiosity and the thrill of a puzzle, but this is a negative trend because it can increase fear and distort expectations of the justice system. Viewers should therefore remember that television is not reality. This is why viewers should enjoy these programmes critically."],"wc":265},{"id":"crime7","topic":"crime","type":"Discussion","key":false,"q":"Is personal safety a government responsibility or the responsibility of the individual?","ideas":["Governments are mainly responsible for safety because they have the power and the resources","Individuals must also take precautions to protect themselves"],"ex":["For example, in most countries emergency services and police patrols are financed from public budgets so that every district receives protection.","For instance, \"neighbourhood watch\" schemes, in which residents observe their area and inform the police, exist in many countries."],"essay":["Some people think that the state must protect citizens from crime, while others believe that everyone should take care of their own safety. This essay believes that the government has the main responsibility, although individuals must also take sensible precautions.","On the one hand, governments are mainly responsible for safety because they have the power and the resources. This is because police, courts and street lighting are paid for by taxes, and no individual can organise these services alone. In addition, laws that punish crime protect everyone equally, including people who cannot defend themselves, such as children and elderly citizens. Furthermore, only the state can create laws and prosecute offenders, which individuals are unable to do. For example, in most countries emergency services and police patrols are financed from public budgets so that every district receives protection. However, the state cannot be everywhere at once.","On the other hand, individuals must also take precautions to protect themselves. That is to say, locking doors, avoiding dangerous areas at night and being careful with personal data make crime much less likely. When neighbours cooperate, they can also reduce crime in their own street at very little cost. Citizens also have a duty to report suspicious activity to the police. For instance, \"neighbourhood watch\" schemes, in which residents observe their area and inform the police, exist in many countries.","In conclusion, although individuals should take sensible precautions, this essay believes that governments hold the main responsibility for personal safety because they provide police, laws and public services. Both sides must cooperate for a safe society. Cooperation is therefore the key to long-term security."],"wc":270},{"id":"character1","topic":"character","type":"Two-part Question","key":true,"q":"Some people work harder than others. Why is this? Can someone learn how to work hard?","ideas":["The main reason why some people work harder is the environment in which they grow up"],"ex":["For example, scholarship programmes that give talented students from poor families access to good schools help them to catch up with wealthier classmates and to develop strong work habits.","For instance, the psychologist Anders Ericsson showed that top performers in music and sport reached their level through years of deliberate practice, not only through talent."],"essay":["It is clear that some individuals put far more effort into their work than others. This is mainly due to their environment and their motivation, and it is certainly possible to learn how to work hard through training and regular practice.","The main reason why some people work harder is the environment in which they grow up. This is because children who have supportive families, good schools and clear goals learn that effort leads to rewards, so hard work becomes a normal habit for them. In contrast, people who never receive encouragement or opportunities may feel that effort is pointless, and they give up easily. For example, scholarship programmes that give talented students from poor families access to good schools help them to catch up with wealthier classmates and to develop strong work habits. Hard work can definitely be learned, because success results from a combination of natural ability and sustained practice. That is to say, people are not born with a fixed level of discipline; they can build it step by step by setting small goals and repeating them every day. Over time, these habits become automatic and the person works harder without feeling forced. For instance, the psychologist Anders Ericsson showed that top performers in music and sport reached their level through years of deliberate practice, not only through talent.","In conclusion, people differ in how hard they work because of their environment and motivation, but everyone can develop this quality through regular practice. Therefore, parents and teachers should create conditions in which effort is noticed and rewarded."],"wc":260},{"id":"character2","topic":"character","type":"Opinion","key":false,"q":"Old people have a better understanding of life. To what extent do you agree?","ideas":["Older people have much more experience of life","Wisdom about people and relationships grows with age"],"ex":["For example, many companies and universities run mentoring programmes in which retired professionals guide young employees and students in their careers.","For instance, General Electric introduced \"reverse mentoring\" in the 1990s, in which young employees taught senior managers about the internet, while the managers shared their experience of business."],"essay":["It is often argued that older generations understand life better than young people. This essay agrees with this statement to a large extent because elderly people have more experience, although young people understand technology better.","The primary reason is that older people have much more experience of life. This is because they have lived through economic crises, family difficulties and career changes, and these events teach them patience and good judgement. As a result, they can give valuable advice to younger people, who have not yet faced similar problems. Their calm advice can save young people from many costly mistakes. For example, many companies and universities run mentoring programmes in which retired professionals guide young employees and students in their careers.","Another important point is that wisdom about people and relationships grows with age. That is to say, elderly people have seen many situations and know how to stay calm and solve conflicts, while young people often react quickly and emotionally. It is true that technology changes so fast that the young sometimes know more, but this is knowledge of tools, not of life itself. For instance, General Electric introduced \"reverse mentoring\" in the 1990s, in which young employees taught senior managers about the internet, while the managers shared their experience of business.","In conclusion, this essay agrees that older people have a better understanding of life because of their long experience and their emotional maturity, although young people can teach them about new technology. Both generations can learn from each other. Respecting their knowledge is therefore wise for any society."],"wc":261},{"id":"character3","topic":"character","type":"Problem / Solution","key":false,"q":"Old people are becoming less respected by society and by the younger generation. Why is this happening? What can be done about it?","ideas":["Family life and society have changed","Create projects in which young and old people meet and help each other"],"ex":["For example, in big cities people move away from their home towns for work, and many elderly parents live alone or in care homes.","For instance, in the Humanitas care home in Deventer in the Netherlands, university students live for free next to elderly residents, and both sides benefit from the contact."],"essay":["It seems that elderly citizens receive less respect than in the past. This is mainly caused by changes in family structure and the fast pace of modern life, and it can be solved through programmes that bring generations together.","The main cause of this problem is that family life and society have changed. This is because families are smaller and often live far apart, so children spend little time with their grandparents. In addition, the media focus on youth, and technology changes so quickly that older people seem out of date. As a result, young people see them as less useful and listen to them less. Moreover, people who live alone have fewer chances to share stories and traditions with children. For example, in big cities people move away from their home towns for work, and many elderly parents live alone or in care homes.","A viable solution is to create projects in which young and old people meet and help each other. If students spend time with elderly people, they will understand their experience, and elderly people will feel valued. Schools and governments can also organise public events that celebrate older generations and teach children to show respect. For instance, in the Humanitas care home in Deventer in the Netherlands, university students live for free next to elderly residents, and both sides benefit from the contact.","In conclusion, older people are respected less because of family changes and rapid technological development, but shared programmes between generations can restore respect. Such cooperation would benefit all of society. Respect must be rebuilt through daily contact, not only through words."],"wc":268},{"id":"character4","topic":"character","type":"Two-part Question","key":false,"q":"Some people follow trends, while other people set them. Why do you think this is? Do you think it is better to set trends or follow them?","ideas":["People follow trends because it is safe and gives them social acceptance","Trend-setters are usually more confident and willing to take a risk"],"ex":["For example, fast fashion brands release new collections every few weeks, which encourages millions of shoppers to follow the latest styles.","For instance, Apple under Steve Jobs launched new trends with products such as the iPhone instead of following competitors."],"essay":["Some people copy fashions and habits, whereas others create new ones. This is mainly due to differences in confidence and personality, and I believe that it is better to set trends, although following them is often safer.","People follow trends because it is safe and gives them social acceptance. This is because copying others reduces the risk of making a mistake and helps people to feel part of a group. Companies also encourage this behaviour with constant advertising, so many people buy what is popular without thinking.","On the other hand, trend-setters are usually more confident and willing to take a risk. Consequently, many consumers feel pressure to follow the crowd even when they would prefer something different. For example, fast fashion brands release new collections every few weeks, which encourages millions of shoppers to follow the latest styles. It is better to set trends because creating new ideas brings innovation and success. That is to say, those who set trends open new markets and inspire others, while followers only imitate what already exists. Of course, this path is riskier, but the rewards are greater for individuals and for society. For instance, Apple under Steve Jobs launched new trends with products such as the iPhone instead of following competitors.","In conclusion, some people follow trends because it is safe and others set them because they are confident, but this essay believes that setting trends is better because it leads to innovation. Society needs both groups, yet creative leaders move it forward. Young people should therefore be encouraged to trust their own ideas."],"wc":262},{"id":"character5","topic":"character","type":"Opinion","key":true,"q":"Some people are born to be successful. Do you agree?","ideas":["Success results from natural ability combined with sustained practice","The environment and opportunities strongly influence success"],"ex":["For example, Mozart was a child prodigy, but his father, who was a musician, trained him from a very young age, and the research of Anders Ericsson shows that top performance comes from intensive deliberate practice.","For instance, scholarship programmes that give gifted students from poor backgrounds access to good schools reduce this gap and produce many successful graduates."],"essay":["It is sometimes claimed that certain individuals are born with a natural gift for success. This essay disagrees with this view because success results from a combination of talent and long practice, and because opportunities and environment matter greatly.","The primary reason is that success results from natural ability combined with sustained practice. This is because talent without training rarely reaches a high level, so even gifted people need years of effort to develop their skills. As a result, hard work is more reliable than luck in predicting who will succeed. In other words, effort is the factor that people can control. For example, Mozart was a child prodigy, but his father, who was a musician, trained him from a very young age, and the research of Anders Ericsson shows that top performance comes from intensive deliberate practice.","Another important point is that the environment and opportunities strongly influence success. That is to say, children who have access to good education and resources start with an advantage, while equally able children without these chances often fall behind. Therefore, success depends on circumstances at least as much as on birth. For instance, scholarship programmes that give gifted students from poor backgrounds access to good schools reduce this gap and produce many successful graduates.","In conclusion, this essay disagrees that people are born to be successful because success comes from talent combined with practice and from the opportunities that society provides. Governments should therefore invest in education so that every child has a fair chance. Believing in the power of effort also motivates children to try harder."],"wc":265},{"id":"character6","topic":"character","type":"Two-part Question","key":false,"q":"Do you think character is innate or something we can cultivate? How can we develop our character?","ideas":["Character can be cultivated because it is shaped by practice and experience, not only by birth","Two effective ways to develop character are systematic training and challenging experiences"],"ex":["For example, Nelson Mandela developed his capacity to lead through education and through the difficult experiences of his years in prison.","For instance, military academies such as West Point train future leaders in a systematic way, and volunteering programmes in schools teach empathy through real experience."],"essay":["People often debate whether personality is something we are born with or something we can shape. This essay believes that character can be cultivated, and that education and challenging experiences are the best ways to develop it.","Character can be cultivated because it is shaped by practice and experience, not only by birth. This is because habits such as discipline, patience and honesty grow when people repeat them in daily life, and many leaders show that these qualities can be learned. It is true that some traits, such as charisma, may be partly inborn, but they are only a starting point. In this way, character is slowly built, like a muscle that becomes stronger with exercise. For example, Nelson Mandela developed his capacity to lead through education and through the difficult experiences of his years in prison.","Two effective ways to develop character are systematic training and challenging experiences. That is to say, schools and organisations can teach teamwork, responsibility and resilience through projects and sport, and people grow stronger when they face real problems and overcome them. Therefore, parents should allow children to make some decisions and learn from mistakes. Mentors and teachers also play an important role by giving honest feedback. For instance, military academies such as West Point train future leaders in a systematic way, and volunteering programmes in schools teach empathy through real experience.","In conclusion, this essay believes that character is not only innate but can be developed through training and challenging experiences. Everyone, therefore, has the ability to improve who they are. This belief gives hope to anyone who wants to improve."],"wc":268},{"id":"character7","topic":"character","type":"Discussion","key":true,"q":"Should schools encourage individuality or conformity? Which one is most beneficial for a child's future?","ideas":["Conformity teaches discipline and teamwork","Individuality develops creativity and independent thinking"],"ex":["For example, in Japan pupils clean their classrooms together every day, which teaches responsibility and cooperation.","For instance, the educator Sir Ken Robinson argued in his 2006 TED talk \"Do schools kill creativity?\" that rigid systems waste talent, while Finland personalises learning but keeps clear rules."],"essay":["Some people think that schools should help pupils to develop their own personalities, while others believe that pupils should follow common rules. This essay believes that individuality is more beneficial for a child's future, as long as basic rules are respected.","On the one hand, conformity teaches discipline and teamwork. This is because uniforms, timetables and rules show children how to behave in a group and prepare them for working life, where everyone must follow certain standards. Without these rules, classrooms would become chaotic and learning would suffer. In addition, pupils who follow common standards learn to respect other people's time and property. For example, in Japan pupils clean their classrooms together every day, which teaches responsibility and cooperation. However, I believe that conformity alone is not enough.","On the other hand, individuality develops creativity and independent thinking. That is to say, children who are encouraged to ask questions and follow their interests become more confident and innovative, and these qualities are highly valued by modern employers. Schools should therefore combine clear rules with personal freedom. In the end, a child who can think independently will adapt more easily to new jobs. For instance, the educator Sir Ken Robinson argued in his 2006 TED talk \"Do schools kill creativity?\" that rigid systems waste talent, while Finland personalises learning but keeps clear rules.","In conclusion, although conformity teaches discipline and cooperation, this essay believes that encouraging individuality is more beneficial for a child's future because it develops creativity and confidence. A good school should balance both. In this way, schools would produce responsible and creative citizens."],"wc":265},{"id":"books1","topic":"books","type":"Opinion","key":true,"q":"Schools and parents should encourage children to read more.","ideas":["Reading regularly builds vocabulary, concentration and imagination in children","Reading for pleasure leads to better academic results"],"ex":["For example, the British programme Bookstart gives free books to babies and their parents, and it aims to make reading a daily habit from the earliest age.","For instance, analyses of the OECD's PISA tests link reading for enjoyment to higher school performance in many countries."],"essay":["It is argued that both teachers and families ought to motivate young people to spend more time with books. This essay agrees with this view because reading builds important language and thinking skills, and because it leads to better results at school.","The primary reason is that reading regularly builds vocabulary, concentration and imagination in children. This is because books expose young readers to new words and complex sentences, and they must focus for a long time to follow a story. As a result, children who read often express themselves more clearly and find it easier to pay attention in class. For example, the British programme Bookstart gives free books to babies and their parents, and it aims to make reading a daily habit from the earliest age.","Another important point is that reading for pleasure leads to better academic results. That is to say, children who enjoy books understand texts in every school subject more easily, including science and history. Therefore, schools should offer daily reading time and let pupils choose their own books, because forcing children to read can destroy the pleasure. As a result, reading becomes a source of success, not a burden. For instance, analyses of the OECD's PISA tests link reading for enjoyment to higher school performance in many countries.","In conclusion, this essay agrees that schools and parents should encourage children to read more because it develops language, concentration and imagination, and because it improves school results. Parents can set an example by reading at home, and schools can provide well-stocked libraries. This habit will also help them throughout their adult life, both at work and in personal development."],"wc":275},{"id":"books2","topic":"books","type":"Advantages / Disadvantages","key":true,"q":"What are the advantages and disadvantages of e-books?","ideas":["They are convenient and cheap","E-books can be tiring and distracting"],"ex":["For example, a single e-reader can hold several thousand books, which is why many commuters and travellers prefer it to heavy paperbacks.","For instance, in the United States printed books still sell more than e-books, which shows that many readers continue to prefer paper."],"essay":["Electronic books are becoming more and more common among readers of all ages. The main advantages of this format are portability and lower prices, whereas the main disadvantages are eye strain and distractions.","The main advantage of e-books is that they are convenient and cheap. This is because one small device can store thousands of titles, so readers can carry a whole library on a trip, and the text can be enlarged for people with weak eyesight. Moreover, digital copies usually cost less than paper copies and arrive instantly after payment. Furthermore, readers can look up the meaning of a word instantly by touching it, which helps students and language learners. For example, a single e-reader can hold several thousand books, which is why many commuters and travellers prefer it to heavy paperbacks.","One major disadvantage is that e-books can be tiring and distracting. In other words, reading from a bright screen for a long time causes eye strain, and notifications from other applications interrupt concentration. In addition, readers cannot lend or resell their digital books, and the battery may run out at an inconvenient moment. Some studies even suggest that readers understand slightly more when they read on paper. Therefore, many people feel that screens are less enjoyable for long novels. For instance, in the United States printed books still sell more than e-books, which shows that many readers continue to prefer paper.","In conclusion, e-books offer portability, low cost and adjustable text, but they can cause eye strain and distraction. Readers should therefore choose the format that suits their habits, and many will probably continue to use both paper and screens. Both formats clearly have a place in modern reading culture."],"wc":283},{"id":"books3","topic":"books","type":"Opinion","key":false,"q":"The government should stop supporting public libraries because most information is available online.","ideas":["Public libraries remain essential because not everyone has equal access to the internet","Online sources are convenient but less reliable than edited books"],"ex":["For example, the New York Public Library lends Wi-Fi hotspots to residents, while in the UK hundreds of libraries closed in the 2010s because of austerity, and local communities felt the loss.","For instance, the Encyclopaedia Britannica stopped printing in 2012 and moved online, yet the spread of misinformation on the internet shows how much we still need verified sources."],"essay":["Some people claim that authorities should end their funding for public libraries, since the internet provides most information. This essay disagrees with this opinion because libraries give equal access to resources, and because online information is often less reliable than edited books.","The primary reason is that public libraries remain essential because not everyone has equal access to the internet. This is because many families cannot afford computers or a fast connection, and libraries offer free internet, computers, help with writing a CV and a quiet place to study. As a result, they protect people with low incomes from being left behind. For example, the New York Public Library lends Wi-Fi hotspots to residents, while in the UK hundreds of libraries closed in the 2010s because of austerity, and local communities felt the loss.","Another important point is that online sources are convenient but less reliable than edited books. That is to say, anyone can publish anything on the internet, while published books are usually checked by editors and experts. Libraries also employ librarians, who teach people how to find trustworthy information and avoid false claims. For instance, the Encyclopaedia Britannica stopped printing in 2012 and moved online, yet the spread of misinformation on the internet shows how much we still need verified sources.","In conclusion, this essay disagrees that governments should stop supporting libraries because they provide equal access to knowledge, and because they offer more reliable information than the internet. Therefore, public funding should continue, and libraries should add digital services to their traditional role. In this way, they can serve both older readers and young students."],"wc":269},{"id":"business1","topic":"business","type":"Advantages / Disadvantages","key":true,"q":"It is better to work for yourself than for a company. What are the advantages and disadvantages of doing so?","ideas":["People enjoy autonomy and flexible schedules","Self-employed people have an unstable income and few protections"],"ex":["For example, freelance designers and drivers often arrange their working day around their children's school hours, which is almost impossible in a company with fixed hours.","For instance, in 2021 the UK Supreme Court decided that Uber drivers were \"workers\" with legal rights, which showed how unprotected many independent workers had been."],"essay":["Many people prefer to run their own business instead of being employed by a company. The main advantages of working for yourself are independence and a flexible schedule, whereas the main disadvantages are an unstable income and the lack of benefits.","The main advantage of self-employment is that people enjoy autonomy and flexible schedules. This is because self-employed workers make their own decisions, choose their clients and decide when and where they work. As a result, they can balance their job with family life and feel more motivated, since the success of the business belongs to them. In addition, there is no limit to what successful freelancers can earn if they work hard and find enough clients. For example, freelance designers and drivers often arrange their working day around their children's school hours, which is almost impossible in a company with fixed hours.","One major disadvantage is that self-employed people have an unstable income and few protections. In other words, they do not receive a regular salary, paid holidays, sick pay or a company pension, so a few bad months can cause serious financial stress. Moreover, working alone can be isolating. For instance, in 2021 the UK Supreme Court decided that Uber drivers were \"workers\" with legal rights, which showed how unprotected many independent workers had been.","In conclusion, working for yourself offers freedom and flexibility, but it also brings risk, an irregular income and no employee benefits. Therefore, people should consider their personality and their savings carefully before they leave a secure job. A realistic plan and some savings can reduce these risks considerably."],"wc":265},{"id":"business2","topic":"business","type":"Opinion","key":true,"q":"Many people are spending money rather than saving it. Is this a positive or negative trend?","ideas":["Spending everything leads to debt and financial stress","Excessive spending is often driven by advertising and social pressure, not by real needs"],"ex":["For example, the 2008 financial crisis began partly because households had borrowed far more than they could repay.","For instance, wedding celebrations in the UK often cost tens of thousands of pounds, and many couples start married life with debt."],"essay":["It is increasingly common for people to spend their income immediately instead of putting it aside. This essay believes that this is a negative trend because it leads to personal debt, and because it encourages people to buy things they do not need.","The primary reason is that spending everything leads to debt and financial stress. This is because people who do not save have no protection when something unexpected happens, so they borrow money with credit cards or \"buy now, pay later\" schemes. As a result, they pay interest, feel anxious and may even go bankrupt. In the long term, constant spending also prevents people from buying a home or preparing for retirement. For example, the 2008 financial crisis began partly because households had borrowed far more than they could repay.","Another important point is that excessive spending is often driven by advertising and social pressure, not by real needs. That is to say, people see products on social media and compare themselves with others, so they buy luxury items to show their status. It is true that spending supports shops and jobs, but this short-term benefit does not justify the long-term damage to families. For instance, wedding celebrations in the UK often cost tens of thousands of pounds, and many couples start married life with debt.","In conclusion, this essay believes that spending rather than saving is a negative trend because it creates debt and encourages unnecessary purchases. Therefore, families should save a part of their income every month, and schools should teach young people how to budget. In this way, they will feel more secure about the future."],"wc":271},{"id":"business3","topic":"business","type":"Problem / Solution","key":true,"q":"Small local businesses are being threatened by large chain stores. Why is this? What can be done about it?","ideas":["Large chains can offer lower prices, a wider choice and online shopping","A viable solution is for governments and communities to support small businesses directly"],"ex":["For example, the British chain Woolworths disappeared in 2008 and 2009, and Amazon has pushed many independent bookshops out of business.","For instance, \"Small Business Saturday\", which started in the United States, encourages residents to spend their money in local shops on one day of the year."],"essay":["Independent shops are finding it harder to survive because of big retail chains. The main reasons for this problem are lower prices and the growth of online shopping, and the most viable solutions are tax relief for small firms and campaigns that encourage people to buy locally.","The main cause of this problem is that large chains can offer lower prices, a wider choice and online shopping. This is because big companies buy goods in huge quantities, so they pay less per item, and they have the money to build websites and delivery systems. As a result, customers leave small shops, and town centres become empty, which also destroys local jobs. Customers who once visited the local baker or bookseller now buy everything from a single website. For example, the British chain Woolworths disappeared in 2008 and 2009, and Amazon has pushed many independent bookshops out of business.","A viable solution is for governments and communities to support small businesses directly. If authorities reduce taxes and rents for small firms, they can compete more easily, and if local people are encouraged to shop locally, money stays in the community. In this way, shops, producers and customers all benefit. For instance, \"Small Business Saturday\", which started in the United States, encourages residents to spend their money in local shops on one day of the year.","In conclusion, small businesses are under pressure because chain stores offer lower prices and online convenience, but tax relief and \"buy local\" campaigns can protect them. With these measures, town centres can stay active and varied. Governments, councils and customers all have a role to play in this process."],"wc":273},{"id":"business4","topic":"business","type":"Discussion","key":true,"q":"People should be encouraged to buy only domestic products, not foreign products. Other people think we should be able to buy what we prefer. Discuss both sides and give your opinion.","ideas":["Buying domestic products supports the national economy","Consumers should be free to buy what they prefer"],"ex":["For example, the Buy American Act of 1933 requires the US government to prefer American-made goods in its purchases.","For instance, many families around the world rely on affordable imported clothes and electronics that local factories cannot produce at the same price."],"essay":["Some people believe that citizens should be encouraged to buy only products made in their own country, while others argue that consumers should be free to choose. This essay agrees with the second view, although it recognises the value of local products.","On the one hand, buying domestic products supports the national economy. This is because money spent on local goods pays the salaries of local workers and the taxes of local companies, so jobs stay in the country. In addition, domestic products travel shorter distances, which can reduce pollution. However, I believe that this should be a choice, not a rule. In addition, a strong domestic industry reduces a country's dependence on foreign suppliers during crises. For example, the Buy American Act of 1933 requires the US government to prefer American-made goods in its purchases.","On the other hand, consumers should be free to buy what they prefer. That is to say, foreign products are often cheaper, of better quality or simply not available at home, and people with low incomes need the lowest prices. Forcing shoppers to buy only local goods would raise prices and reduce choice, and it could also provoke trade restrictions from other countries. For instance, many families around the world rely on affordable imported clothes and electronics that local factories cannot produce at the same price.","In conclusion, although domestic purchases help the local economy, this essay believes that people should be free to choose their products. Governments can still promote local goods through advertising rather than through obligation. This approach respects both national interests and individual freedom."],"wc":264},{"id":"business5","topic":"business","type":"Opinion","key":false,"q":"No children should leave school without learning how to manage money. Do you agree?","ideas":["Teaching money management early prevents debt in adulthood","Financial skills help young people to make wise decisions about spending and saving"],"ex":["For example, England made financial education part of the secondary school curriculum in 2014 in order to prepare teenagers for adult life.","For instance, the 2008 financial crisis began partly because many households had borrowed more than they could repay, which shows how costly a lack of financial knowledge can be."],"essay":["It is argued that every pupil should be taught how to handle money before finishing school. This essay agrees with this statement because early financial education prevents debt in adulthood, and because it helps young people to make wise everyday decisions.","The primary reason is that teaching money management early prevents debt in adulthood. This is because many young adults receive their first credit card without understanding interest, so they spend more than they earn. If schools teach budgeting and saving, students will recognise these risks and avoid them. As a result, fewer families will fall into serious debt. In this way, young people learn that every purchase has a long-term cost. For example, England made financial education part of the secondary school curriculum in 2014 in order to prepare teenagers for adult life.","Another important point is that financial skills help young people to make wise decisions about spending and saving. That is to say, pupils who learn to compare prices, plan a budget and resist advertising become more independent. This knowledge is useful in every career and protects people from scams and unfair loans. For instance, the 2008 financial crisis began partly because many households had borrowed more than they could repay, which shows how costly a lack of financial knowledge can be.","In conclusion, this essay agrees that children should learn to manage money before they leave school because it reduces future debt and improves everyday decisions. Therefore, schools should include practical lessons on budgeting in their regular timetable. Early lessons will help future citizens to manage their own households responsibly."],"wc":264},{"id":"business6","topic":"business","type":"Problem / Solution","key":false,"q":"Many people go into personal debt. Why is this? What can be done about it?","ideas":["The main cause of personal debt is easy credit combined with advertising and social comparison","Teach financial skills and to regulate credit more strictly"],"ex":["For example, the 2008 financial crisis began partly because households had borrowed far more than they could repay.","For instance, Singapore requires workers to save part of their salary in the compulsory Central Provident Fund, which encourages saving from the beginning of working life."],"essay":["A large number of people owe more money than they can easily repay. The main causes of this problem are easy credit and social pressure to spend, and the most effective solutions are financial education and stricter rules for lenders.","The main cause of personal debt is easy credit combined with advertising and social comparison. This is because credit cards and \"buy now, pay later\" services make purchases feel painless, while social media encourages people to compare their lifestyle with others. As a result, many consumers buy things they cannot afford and then struggle with interest payments, stress and, in some cases, bankruptcy. Many people also take loans to maintain a lifestyle that is above their real income. For example, the 2008 financial crisis began partly because households had borrowed far more than they could repay.","A viable solution is to teach financial skills and to regulate credit more strictly. If schools and employers teach people how to budget, they will make more careful decisions, and if governments limit high interest rates and aggressive advertising of loans, fewer people will be trapped. In this way, both individuals and lenders share responsibility. Employers could also offer short courses on personal finance to their staff. For instance, Singapore requires workers to save part of their salary in the compulsory Central Provident Fund, which encourages saving from the beginning of working life.","In conclusion, personal debt is caused by easy credit and the pressure to buy, but it can be reduced through financial education and stronger regulation of lenders. These measures would protect families and improve the stability of the economy. Responsibility must be shared between borrowers, schools and banks."],"wc":277},{"id":"business7","topic":"business","type":"Two-part Question","key":false,"q":"Many people spend too much money on luxury goods and special events. Why is this? Is this a positive or negative trend?","ideas":["People spend heavily on luxury and events mainly because of social status and social media","Negative because it creates debt and stress"],"ex":["For example, weddings in the UK often cost tens of thousands of pounds, partly because couples want to match the celebrations they see online.","For instance, the 2008 financial crisis began partly because households had taken on more debt than they could repay."],"essay":["It is common for people to spend large amounts on expensive products and celebrations. This is mainly due to social pressure and the influence of social media, and it should be seen as a negative trend because it often leads to debt.","People spend heavily on luxury and events mainly because of social status and social media. This is because owning expensive items or organising a grand party is seen as a sign of success, and photos on Instagram or TikTok make people want to imitate what they see. As a result, many feel that they must spend more in order to be accepted by their friends and family. For example, weddings in the UK often cost tens of thousands of pounds, partly because couples want to match the celebrations they see online.","This trend is negative because it creates debt and stress. That is to say, people who spend more than they earn borrow money, and the repayments can limit their lives for years. It is true that luxury spending creates jobs in shops and event companies, but this advantage is small compared with the personal cost for families. For instance, the 2008 financial crisis began partly because households had taken on more debt than they could repay.","In conclusion, people overspend on luxury and special occasions because of status and social media, and this is a negative trend because it often ends in debt. Families should set a budget for celebrations and avoid comparing themselves with others. Teaching young people to value experiences and savings more than status symbols would also help."],"wc":264},{"id":"business8","topic":"business","type":"Opinion","key":false,"q":"Having a good product is the best way to be successful in business. Do you agree?","ideas":["A good product does not guarantee success without marketing and timing","Quality matters mostly for keeping customers over time"],"ex":["For example, Betamax video tapes were technically superior to VHS, but VHS won the market because it had more partners and cheaper recorders.","For instance, many small restaurants with excellent food still close in their first year because few people know that they exist."],"essay":["Some people believe that a high-quality product is the surest route to business success. This essay partly disagrees with this view because marketing and timing are equally important, although quality does keep customers in the long term.","The primary reason is that a good product does not guarantee success without marketing and timing. This is because customers can only buy what they know about, and a company that does not advertise or reach the right market at the right moment will be overlooked. As a result, even excellent products can fail when competitors are better at selling. In a competitive market, the company that explains its advantages best will usually gain the most customers. For example, Betamax video tapes were technically superior to VHS, but VHS won the market because it had more partners and cheaper recorders.","Another important point is that quality matters mostly for keeping customers over time. That is to say, a good product encourages repeat purchases and positive reviews, which build a loyal customer base. However, this benefit appears only after the customer has been attracted by effective promotion and a fair price. Therefore, quality is necessary, but it is not sufficient on its own. For instance, many small restaurants with excellent food still close in their first year because few people know that they exist.","In conclusion, this essay disagrees that a good product alone is the best way to succeed because marketing and timing decide whether people notice it. Quality remains important, but it works best together with good promotion. Entrepreneurs should plan their marketing as carefully as their production."],"wc":266},{"id":"business9","topic":"business","type":"Advantages / Disadvantages","key":false,"q":"Many companies use open-plan office spaces. What are the advantages and disadvantages of this?","ideas":["They encourage collaboration and reduce costs","Open-plan offices are noisy and offer little privacy"],"ex":["For example, many technology start-ups use shared desks in one large room so that teams can solve problems together at the same moment.","For instance, a 2018 Harvard study found that employees interacted less face to face after their company moved to an open-plan office."],"essay":["More and more employers prefer large shared offices to separate rooms. The main advantages of this layout are better collaboration and lower costs, whereas the main disadvantages are noise and a lack of privacy.","The main advantage of open-plan offices is that they encourage collaboration and reduce costs. This is because employees sit close to each other, so they can ask questions quickly and share ideas without arranging meetings. In addition, companies need less space and fewer walls, which lowers rent and building costs, so they can invest more in staff and equipment. Furthermore, managers can see more easily whether teams need help, and new employees learn quickly by observing colleagues. For example, many technology start-ups use shared desks in one large room so that teams can solve problems together at the same moment.","One major disadvantage is that open-plan offices are noisy and offer little privacy. In other words, phone calls, conversations and movement constantly interrupt people who need to concentrate, and confidential discussions become difficult. As a result, workers may feel stressed and less productive. Employees also find it difficult to take private phone calls or to discuss salary or health problems. For instance, a 2018 Harvard study found that employees interacted less face to face after their company moved to an open-plan office.","In conclusion, open-plan offices bring cooperation and savings, but noise and lack of privacy can harm concentration. Companies should therefore provide quiet rooms next to shared spaces so that employees can enjoy the benefits of both. This balance could keep staff both productive and satisfied."],"wc":261},{"id":"health1","topic":"health","type":"Opinion","key":true,"q":"The government should spend less money on trying to cure illnesses and more money on preventing them. Do you agree?","ideas":["Prevention is more cost-effective than treatment","Prevention improves the health and productivity of the whole population"],"ex":["For example, vaccination programmes led to the worldwide eradication of smallpox in 1980, which saved billions in treatment costs and millions of lives.","For instance, the North Karelia project in Finland in the 1970s changed local eating and smoking habits and greatly reduced heart disease in the region."],"essay":["It is suggested that public budgets should focus on stopping diseases before they appear rather than on treating patients. This essay agrees with this view because prevention costs less in the long term, and because it improves the quality of life of the whole population.","The primary reason is that prevention is more cost-effective than treatment. This is because stopping an illness before it develops avoids expensive hospital stays, operations and long-term medicines, so every unit of money spent on prevention saves much more later. As a result, governments can use the savings to improve other services. It is true that people who are already ill need urgent care, so both approaches are necessary, but the balance should change. For example, vaccination programmes led to the worldwide eradication of smallpox in 1980, which saved billions in treatment costs and millions of lives.","Another important point is that prevention improves the health and productivity of the whole population. That is to say, when people eat well, exercise and avoid smoking, they stay healthy for longer and can keep working and caring for their families. Public campaigns and rules are also able to change the habits of millions of people at once. For instance, the North Karelia project in Finland in the 1970s changed local eating and smoking habits and greatly reduced heart disease in the region.","In conclusion, this essay agrees that governments should invest more in prevention because it saves money and keeps people healthier. Treatment will still be needed for those who are sick, but prevention should receive a larger share of the budget."],"wc":265},{"id":"health2","topic":"health","type":"Opinion","key":false,"q":"Health care should be free for all people. To what extent do you agree?","ideas":["Free health care guarantees equal access to treatment","A healthy population is more productive"],"ex":["For example, the National Health Service in the UK has offered care free at the point of use since 1948, and in the United States medical bills are a common cause of personal debt.","For instance, Thailand introduced universal health coverage in 2002, and the programme improved access for millions of low-income citizens."],"essay":["It is argued that medical services should not cost anything for any citizen. This essay agrees with this statement because free care gives everyone equal access to treatment, and because a healthy population benefits the whole economy.","The primary reason is that free health care guarantees equal access to treatment. This is because when patients must pay, poor people delay going to the doctor until their illness becomes serious, so they suffer more and the final treatment is more expensive. Health is a basic need, and nobody should be refused help because of low income. As a result, society becomes fairer. For example, the National Health Service in the UK has offered care free at the point of use since 1948, and in the United States medical bills are a common cause of personal debt.","Another important point is that a healthy population is more productive. That is to say, people who receive treatment quickly return to work earlier and spread fewer infections, which supports the economy. It is true that free care requires high taxes and may create waiting lists, but good management can reduce these problems. For instance, Thailand introduced universal health coverage in 2002, and the programme improved access for millions of low-income citizens.","In conclusion, this essay agrees that health care should be free because it creates equal opportunities and supports a productive society. Governments must manage the costs carefully so that the quality of service remains high."],"wc":242},{"id":"health3","topic":"health","type":"Problem / Solution","key":true,"q":"Too many children spend most of their free time using screens. Why is this a problem? What solutions can you suggest?","ideas":["Excessive screen time harms children's health","Set time limits and to offer attractive alternatives"],"ex":["For example, the World Health Organization recommends at least 60 minutes of physical activity every day for children, but many children do far less.","For instance, Australia passed a law in 2024 that sets a minimum age of 16 for accounts on social media platforms."],"essay":["A large number of children spend nearly all of their leisure time looking at phones, tablets and televisions. This is a problem because it harms physical health and sleep, and the most effective solutions are time limits and attractive outdoor activities.","The main problem is that excessive screen time harms children's health. This is because children who sit for hours do not exercise, so they have a higher risk of obesity, and bright screens in the evening disturb sleep and weaken eyesight. In addition, tiredness reduces concentration at school. As a result, their physical and mental development suffers. For example, the World Health Organization recommends at least 60 minutes of physical activity every day for children, but many children do far less.","A viable solution is to set time limits and to offer attractive alternatives. If parents agree on clear rules for devices, for example no screens during meals or before bed, and if schools and local clubs provide sports and outdoor games, children will find other ways to enjoy their free time. Governments can also regulate what young people see online. For instance, Australia passed a law in 2024 that sets a minimum age of 16 for accounts on social media platforms.","In conclusion, too much screen time damages the health and sleep of children, but family rules, active hobbies and sensible legislation can reduce the problem. Adults should also set a good example in their own use of technology."],"wc":241},{"id":"health4","topic":"health","type":"Two-part Question","key":false,"q":"Many schools teach team sports in schools. Why is this?","ideas":["Schools teach team sports first because they improve the health of children","Team sports teach cooperation, discipline and resilience"],"ex":["For example, Finland's \"Schools on the Move\" programme increases the amount of physical activity during the school day.","For instance, school sports clubs are an important part of student life in Japan, and many schools also provide swimming, dance or cycling for those who prefer them."],"essay":["Team sports such as football and basketball are part of the timetable in many schools. This is mainly because they improve health and because they teach cooperation and discipline, although schools should also offer other activities for pupils who dislike competition.","Schools teach team sports first because they improve the health of children. This is because regular exercise strengthens the body, reduces the risk of obesity and also improves concentration in lessons. Team games are enjoyable, so children want to take part and develop a habit of being active. As a result, they are more likely to stay fit as adults. For example, Finland's \"Schools on the Move\" programme increases the amount of physical activity during the school day.","Another reason is that team sports teach cooperation, discipline and resilience. That is to say, children must follow rules, work together to reach a goal and accept both victory and defeat, and these are skills that are useful at work and in life. However, pupils who are less competitive may feel excluded, so schools should also offer individual options. For instance, school sports clubs are an important part of student life in Japan, and many schools also provide swimming, dance or cycling for those who prefer them.","In conclusion, schools teach team sports because they promote health and develop teamwork and discipline. By offering a variety of activities, they can make sure that every child benefits."],"wc":235},{"id":"health5","topic":"health","type":"Opinion","key":false,"q":"Schools should increase the number of sports and exercise classes to help children develop into healthy adults. What is your opinion?","ideas":["Regular exercise improves both physical health and learning","Sport teaches discipline, teamwork and resilience"],"ex":["For example, the World Health Organization recommends 60 minutes of physical activity every day for children, which is often impossible without school lessons.","For instance, the Finnish programme \"Schools on the Move\" adds active breaks to the school day and encourages pupils to move more."],"essay":["Some people argue that physical education should take up more time in the school week in order to build healthier adults. This essay agrees with this idea because exercise improves health and learning, and because it teaches valuable life skills.","The primary reason is that regular exercise improves both physical health and learning. This is because active children have stronger bodies and a lower risk of obesity, and movement increases blood flow to the brain, which helps them to concentrate. As a result, a lesson of sport can even raise performance in academic subjects. Children who learn to enjoy exercise usually remain active in adult life. For example, the World Health Organization recommends 60 minutes of physical activity every day for children, which is often impossible without school lessons.","Another important point is that sport teaches discipline, teamwork and resilience. That is to say, pupils learn to follow rules, cooperate with others and deal with failure, and these qualities help them in their future jobs. Schools can include dance, swimming or cycling, so that children who are not competitive also feel welcome. For instance, the Finnish programme \"Schools on the Move\" adds active breaks to the school day and encourages pupils to move more.","In conclusion, this essay agrees that schools should offer more sport and exercise because it improves health, concentration and character. A wider choice of activities would help every pupil to find something enjoyable."],"wc":237},{"id":"health6","topic":"health","type":"Opinion","key":false,"q":"It is the school's responsibility, not the parents' responsibility, to teach children what healthy food is. To what extent do you agree?","ideas":["Parents form the eating habits of their children","Schools help but cannot do the job alone"],"ex":["For example, in many families where the parents cook vegetables regularly, children grow up accepting them as normal food.","For instance, Jamie Oliver's campaign for better school dinners in the UK in 2005 showed that schools can improve what children eat, and Japan teaches food education as part of its school programme."],"essay":["It is claimed that only schools should teach children about healthy eating, not their families. This essay disagrees with this view because parents form the first eating habits, although schools can support them.","The primary reason is that parents form the eating habits of their children. This is because children eat most of their meals at home and copy what they see on the family table, so parents decide what food is available and how often sweets are eaten. As a result, a school lesson cannot change a diet that is poor at home. Parents who cook fresh meals and eat together teach healthy habits every day. For example, in many families where the parents cook vegetables regularly, children grow up accepting them as normal food.","Another important point is that schools help but cannot do the job alone. That is to say, schools reach all children and can explain nutrition in an organised way, which is especially valuable when parents lack knowledge or time. However, the best results appear when teachers and parents share the same message. For instance, Jamie Oliver's campaign for better school dinners in the UK in 2005 showed that schools can improve what children eat, and Japan teaches food education as part of its school programme.","In conclusion, this essay disagrees that healthy eating is only the school's task because parents shape daily habits, and schools can only strengthen them. Cooperation between both is the key to healthier children."],"wc":244},{"id":"health7","topic":"health","type":"Two-part Question","key":true,"q":"More and more adults are starting to prioritise their mental health. Why is this? Do you think this is a positive or negative trend?","ideas":["Adults pay more attention to mental health because stigma has decreased and stress has increased","Positive because it allows people to get support before problems become serious"],"ex":["For example, the tennis player Naomi Osaka spoke publicly about her mental health at the French Open in 2021, and the World Health Organization reported a strong increase in anxiety and depression during the pandemic.","For instance, many companies now offer counselling services and flexible schedules to protect the wellbeing of their employees."],"essay":["An increasing number of adults now give priority to their mental wellbeing. This is mainly due to reduced stigma and growing pressure at work and online, and I believe that it is a positive trend because it allows people to receive help earlier.","Adults pay more attention to mental health because stigma has decreased and stress has increased. This is because celebrities and public figures now talk openly about their experiences, which makes it easier for ordinary people to ask for help. At the same time, social media, uncertain jobs and the pandemic created new sources of anxiety. As a result, many people realise that they must protect their mind in the same way as their body. For example, the tennis player Naomi Osaka spoke publicly about her mental health at the French Open in 2021, and the World Health Organization reported a strong increase in anxiety and depression during the pandemic.","This trend is positive because it allows people to get support before problems become serious. That is to say, early help through counselling or lifestyle changes can prevent depression and keep people working and enjoying life. There is a small risk that some people may diagnose themselves incorrectly or feel pressure to be happy all the time, but this risk is smaller than the benefit. For instance, many companies now offer counselling services and flexible schedules to protect the wellbeing of their employees.","In conclusion, adults prioritise mental health because of less stigma and more stress, and this is a positive trend because it leads to earlier help. Society should continue to support open conversations and professional services."],"wc":270},{"id":"health8","topic":"health","type":"Advantages / Disadvantages","key":true,"q":"Many people commute to work by car or public transport, but not by cycling or walking. What are the advantages and disadvantages of this?","ideas":["It is fast and comfortable","This habit causes traffic, pollution and inactivity"],"ex":["For example, an employee who lives twenty kilometres from the office can arrive in half an hour by train, while cycling would take more than an hour.","For instance, in Copenhagen around half of all trips to work or school are made by bicycle, and the city is known for its clean air and healthy residents."],"essay":["Most workers travel to their jobs by car or by bus and train instead of on foot or by bicycle. The main advantages of this are comfort and speed, whereas the main disadvantages are traffic, pollution and a lack of exercise.","The main advantage of using a car or public transport is that it is fast and comfortable. This is because motor vehicles cover long distances in a short time and protect passengers from rain and cold, and public transport allows people to read or rest during the journey. In addition, many workplaces are too far away to reach on foot. As a result, motorised transport saves time and energy for the working day. For example, an employee who lives twenty kilometres from the office can arrive in half an hour by train, while cycling would take more than an hour.","One major disadvantage is that this habit causes traffic, pollution and inactivity. In other words, millions of cars create traffic jams and harmful emissions in cities, and people who sit during the commute miss an easy opportunity to exercise. In the long term, this contributes to obesity and heart disease. For instance, in Copenhagen around half of all trips to work or school are made by bicycle, and the city is known for its clean air and healthy residents.","In conclusion, commuting by car or public transport saves time and offers comfort, but it increases traffic, pollution and physical inactivity. Cities should therefore build safe cycle lanes to encourage healthier alternatives."],"wc":252},{"id":"health9","topic":"health","type":"Two-part Question","key":false,"q":"International sporting events are very popular. Why is this?","ideas":["International sporting events are popular because they create national pride and a shared experience","These events bring economic benefits and renewal to host cities"],"ex":["For example, the Olympic Games and the football World Cup are watched by billions of people in total during each tournament.","For instance, the London Olympics of 2012 helped to regenerate a poor area in the east of the city."],"essay":["Events such as the Olympic Games and the World Cup attract huge audiences around the world. This is mainly due to national pride and the shared experience that they create, and they also bring benefits to host cities, although their costs can be very high.","International sporting events are popular because they create national pride and a shared experience. This is because people support their own country, feel united with other fans and celebrate victories together, even when they have never met. In addition, television and social media allow billions of viewers to follow the competitions at the same time. As a result, these events become moments that people remember for many years. For example, the Olympic Games and the football World Cup are watched by billions of people in total during each tournament.","Another reason is that these events bring economic benefits and renewal to host cities. That is to say, visitors spend money in hotels and shops, and governments build stadiums, transport links and parks that remain useful afterwards. However, critics point out that the cost for the state can be enormous, so careful planning is needed. For instance, the London Olympics of 2012 helped to regenerate a poor area in the east of the city.","In conclusion, international sporting events are popular because of national pride, shared excitement and economic benefits for hosts. Cities should plan them carefully so that the long-term benefits are larger than the costs."],"wc":241},{"id":"health10","topic":"health","type":"Opinion","key":false,"q":"Hobbies are healthy activities all adults should have. Do you agree?","ideas":["Hobbies reduce stress and improve mental wellbeing","Hobbies build social connections and new skills"],"ex":["For example, Winston Churchill took up painting as a hobby in order to relax during difficult times in his life.","For instance, the Men's Sheds movement in Australia gives older men a workshop where they build things together, and it helps to fight isolation."],"essay":["It is said that every adult should have a regular activity that they enjoy in their free time. This essay agrees with this statement because hobbies reduce stress, and because they improve social relationships and personal skills.","The primary reason is that hobbies reduce stress and improve mental wellbeing. This is because a pleasant activity gives people a break from their duties and allows them to relax and concentrate on something different. As a result, they return to work feeling calmer and more positive. This effect is especially important for adults who spend long hours at a desk. For example, Winston Churchill took up painting as a hobby in order to relax during difficult times in his life.","Another important point is that hobbies build social connections and new skills. That is to say, people who join a club, a team or a class meet others with similar interests, which reduces loneliness, and they learn new abilities that give them confidence. Even a quiet hobby can have this effect when it is shared with other people. For instance, the Men's Sheds movement in Australia gives older men a workshop where they build things together, and it helps to fight isolation.","In conclusion, this essay agrees that adults should have hobbies because they reduce stress and bring people together. Employers and governments could encourage this by supporting clubs and offering flexible working hours."],"wc":232},{"id":"health11","topic":"health","type":"Problem / Solution","key":false,"q":"People are living longer than in the past. Why is this? What problems does this cause?","ideas":["People live longer mainly because of better medicine and better living conditions"],"ex":["For example, life expectancy in many developed countries has increased by more than a decade since the middle of the twentieth century.","For instance, Denmark links its retirement age to life expectancy, which helps to keep the pension system stable."],"essay":["The average person now lives much longer than in earlier decades. This is mainly due to progress in medicine and a better quality of life, and it causes problems such as pressure on pensions and health services, which can be reduced through reforms.","People live longer mainly because of better medicine and better living conditions. This is because vaccines, antibiotics and modern surgery cure diseases that were once deadly, and cleaner water, safer food and healthier lifestyles reduce the risk of illness. In addition, governments now provide screening and preventive care. As a result, life expectancy has risen significantly in most countries. For example, life expectancy in many developed countries has increased by more than a decade since the middle of the twentieth century. The main problem is the pressure that an ageing population puts on pensions, health care and the labour market. That is to say, there are fewer workers to pay for the pensions and medical needs of a growing number of older citizens. A solution is to raise the retirement age gradually, to encourage preventive health care and to allow flexible work for older people. For instance, Denmark links its retirement age to life expectancy, which helps to keep the pension system stable.","In conclusion, people live longer because of medical progress and better living standards, but this creates financial and social pressure that governments must manage through reform. With good planning, longer lives can also be a great benefit."],"wc":243},{"id":"health12","topic":"health","type":"Two-part Question","key":false,"q":"An increasing number of people are choosing cosmetic surgeries. Why do people have operations to change the way they look? Do you think this is a positive or negative development?","ideas":["People choose cosmetic surgery mainly because of beauty standards and the wish to feel more confident","Reconstructive surgery that helps accident victims is valuable, but this is different from purely cosmetic changes"],"ex":["For example, South Korea has one of the highest rates of cosmetic procedures in the world, and plastic surgery is widely accepted there.","For instance, surgeons regularly warn that patients who have unrealistic expectations of cosmetic surgery are more likely to be disappointed with the results."],"essay":["More and more people decide to change their appearance through surgery. This is mainly due to beauty standards promoted by social media and the desire for more confidence, and I believe that it is a negative development because of the risks and unrealistic expectations.","People choose cosmetic surgery mainly because of beauty standards and the wish to feel more confident. This is because celebrities and social media constantly show perfect faces and bodies, which makes ordinary people feel that they must look similar. In addition, some individuals believe that a better appearance will help their careers and relationships. As a result, procedures that were once rare are now very common. For example, South Korea has one of the highest rates of cosmetic procedures in the world, and plastic surgery is widely accepted there. This development is mostly negative because it carries medical risks and creates unrealistic expectations. That is to say, every operation can lead to complications, and many people remain unhappy with their appearance even after surgery, so they repeat the procedures.","On the other hand, reconstructive surgery that helps accident victims is valuable, but this is different from purely cosmetic changes. For instance, surgeons regularly warn that patients who have unrealistic expectations of cosmetic surgery are more likely to be disappointed with the results.","In conclusion, people have cosmetic operations because of social pressure and the search for confidence, but this trend is negative because it brings health risks and encourages unrealistic ideas of beauty. Education about body image would be a better solution."],"wc":256},{"id":"health13","topic":"health","type":"Problem / Solution","key":false,"q":"In many professional sports, there is an increase in the number of athletes using banned substances to improve their performance. What are the causes of the phenomenon and what are some of the possible solutions?","ideas":["The main cause is the fierce competition and the high rewards in professional sport","Impose heavier punishments and to improve testing"],"ex":["For example, many top athletes have admitted that they used banned substances for years before they were detected.","For instance, random out-of-competition testing is now a standard part of anti-doping programmes, and many athletes have been caught after organisations invested in better laboratories."],"essay":["In several professional sports, more athletes are taking forbidden drugs to improve their results. The main causes of this are intense competition and weak testing systems, and the most suitable solutions are heavier punishments and more frequent random tests.","The main cause is the fierce competition and the high rewards in professional sport. This is because winning brings huge prizes, sponsorships and fame, so some athletes feel that they must take banned substances to have an advantage over opponents. In addition, testing procedures sometimes have gaps, so cheaters believe that they will not be caught. As a result, the temptation to cheat increases. For example, many top athletes have admitted that they used banned substances for years before they were detected.","A viable solution is to impose heavier punishments and to improve testing. If athletes risk long bans and high fines, they will think twice before cheating, and if organisations carry out frequent and unannounced tests with modern equipment, it becomes much harder to avoid detection. This protects honest competitors and keeps sport fair. For instance, random out-of-competition testing is now a standard part of anti-doping programmes, and many athletes have been caught after organisations invested in better laboratories.","In conclusion, doping is caused by intense competition and weak tests, but stricter penalties and better control can reduce it. These measures will protect the honesty of professional sport."],"wc":228},{"id":"health14","topic":"health","type":"Opinion","key":false,"q":"Some sports professionals earn more money than doctors or nurses. Do you think this is right?","ideas":["The primary reason why it is not fair is that doctors and nurses contribute more to society","Athletes earn so much because of the market, not because of their social value"],"ex":["For example, during the Covid-19 pandemic, people in the UK applauded nurses and doctors every week in the \"Clap for Carers\" campaign, but their salaries remained modest.","For instance, television rights for major football leagues are worth billions of dollars, which explains the huge salaries of top players."],"essay":["It is often noted that famous athletes receive much higher salaries than medical professionals. This essay believes that this is not fully fair, although it can be explained by market forces.","The primary reason why it is not fair is that doctors and nurses contribute more to society. This is because they save lives and protect public health every day, after years of difficult training, while sport is entertainment. As a result, many people feel that these essential workers deserve much higher pay. Low salaries also make it harder to attract enough staff to hospitals. For example, during the Covid-19 pandemic, people in the UK applauded nurses and doctors every week in the \"Clap for Carers\" campaign, but their salaries remained modest.","Another important point is that athletes earn so much because of the market, not because of their social value. That is to say, millions of fans pay for tickets and television, and sponsors invest large sums in famous names, so the income is enormous even though sports careers are short. Governments cannot easily change this, but they can raise public salaries and tax very high incomes progressively. For instance, television rights for major football leagues are worth billions of dollars, which explains the huge salaries of top players.","In conclusion, this essay believes that it is unfair for athletes to earn more than doctors and nurses, although market demand explains the difference. Public salaries for medical staff should be increased to reflect their value."],"wc":246},{"id":"food1","topic":"food","type":"Two-part Question","key":true,"q":"Traditional food is becoming less popular. Do you think this is a problem? What can be done about it?","ideas":["The decline of traditional food is a problem because it harms both health and cultural heritage","Teaching cooking in schools and protecting traditional products"],"ex":["For example, the Slow Food movement began in Italy in 1986 as a protest against the spread of fast-food restaurants.","For instance, the European Union protects traditional foods with PDO and PGI labels, and in Romania the Sibiu salami carries this protection."],"essay":["Traditional dishes are losing popularity in many countries. This essay believes that this is a problem because it affects health and cultural heritage, and it will suggest that cooking lessons in schools and protection of local products are the best solutions.","The decline of traditional food is a problem because it harms both health and cultural heritage. This is because traditional meals are usually cooked at home from fresh ingredients, while fast food and ready meals contain more sugar, salt and fat, so people who abandon them eat less healthily. In addition, recipes are part of a community's identity, and when they are forgotten, a piece of history disappears. The main causes are busy lifestyles, cheap fast food, advertising and urbanisation. For example, the Slow Food movement began in Italy in 1986 as a protest against the spread of fast-food restaurants.","Two effective solutions are teaching cooking in schools and protecting traditional products. If children learn to prepare local dishes in class, they will appreciate them and continue to cook them as adults, and food festivals can make traditional cuisine attractive to young people. Quality labels also help farmers to sell authentic products at a fair price, which encourages them to keep producing them. For instance, the European Union protects traditional foods with PDO and PGI labels, and in Romania the Sibiu salami carries this protection.","In conclusion, the decline of traditional food is a problem because it damages health and cultural identity, but school lessons, festivals and quality labels can protect it. With these measures, future generations will still enjoy their national cuisine."],"wc":264},{"id":"food2","topic":"food","type":"Problem / Solution","key":true,"q":"More and more children are eating junk food. What are the reasons for this? What can be done to solve this?","ideas":["Junk food is heavily advertised, cheap and convenient","Restrict advertising and to improve school meals"],"ex":["For example, in many countries a fast-food meal costs less than a fresh meal in a restaurant, and advertisements for sweets appear during children's television programmes.","For instance, Chile introduced black warning labels and limited advertising to children in 2016, the chef Jamie Oliver's campaign improved school dinners in the UK in 2005, and Britain introduced a tax on sugary drinks in 2018."],"essay":["An increasing number of children eat food that is high in sugar and fat. The main reasons for this are advertising and the low price and convenience of such food, and the most effective solutions are limits on advertising to children and healthier school meals.","The main cause is that junk food is heavily advertised, cheap and convenient. This is because companies use cartoons and free toys to attract children, and busy parents often choose quick meals that are easy to prepare and cost little. In addition, children copy their friends, so a snack that is popular at school spreads quickly. As a result, healthy food seems boring in comparison. For example, in many countries a fast-food meal costs less than a fresh meal in a restaurant, and advertisements for sweets appear during children's television programmes.","A viable solution is to restrict advertising and to improve school meals. If governments limit marketing to children and put clear warning labels on unhealthy products, families make better choices, and if schools serve nutritious lunches, pupils learn good habits every day. Taxes on sugary drinks also make these products less attractive. For instance, Chile introduced black warning labels and limited advertising to children in 2016, the chef Jamie Oliver's campaign improved school dinners in the UK in 2005, and Britain introduced a tax on sugary drinks in 2018.","In conclusion, children eat more junk food because of advertising, low prices and convenience, but stricter rules on marketing and better school meals can change this habit. Parents also have a role to play by cooking simple meals at home."],"wc":265},{"id":"food3","topic":"food","type":"Two-part Question","key":false,"q":"Some people eat only organic food. What is the reason for this? Do you think it is a positive or negative trend?","ideas":["People choose organic food mainly because of concerns about health and the environment","Positive for the environment, but it has limits","Moreover, the evidence for nutritional advantages is limited"],"ex":["For example, organic fields usually support a greater variety of insects and plants than fields that use intensive chemical methods.","For instance, a basket of organic vegetables can cost noticeably more than the same vegetables in a regular supermarket, which makes it a choice mainly for wealthier households."],"essay":["Some people buy only organic products. This is mainly because they care about their health and the environment, and I believe that it is a positive trend for nature, although it is not affordable for everyone.","People choose organic food mainly because of concerns about health and the environment. This is because organic farms use fewer chemical pesticides and artificial fertilisers, which protects soil, water and wildlife, and many consumers believe that the food tastes better and is safer. For some, buying organic products is also a sign of a responsible lifestyle. As a result, demand for these products has grown steadily. For example, organic fields usually support a greater variety of insects and plants than fields that use intensive chemical methods.","This trend is positive for the environment, but it has limits. That is to say, organic farming reduces pollution and helps biodiversity, yet it usually produces less food on the same land and its products cost more, so poorer families cannot always afford them.","Moreover, the evidence for nutritional advantages is limited. Therefore, organic food should be one option among others, not the only solution. Fairer prices would let more households make this choice. For instance, a basket of organic vegetables can cost noticeably more than the same vegetables in a regular supermarket, which makes it a choice mainly for wealthier households.","In conclusion, people eat organic food because of health and environmental concerns, and this trend is positive for nature, but its high prices prevent many families from joining it. Governments could support organic farmers so that prices become fairer."],"wc":263},{"id":"food4","topic":"food","type":"Discussion","key":true,"q":"Some people think cooking should be taught in schools, while other people think children should learn this at home. Discuss both sides and give your opinion.","ideas":["Children can learn to cook at home because families pass on traditions","Schools should teach cooking because every child should have this life skill"],"ex":["For example, in many families the recipes of a grandmother are passed on from one generation to the next simply by cooking together in the kitchen.","For instance, England has included cooking and nutrition in the compulsory curriculum up to the age of 14 since 2014, and Japan teaches food education in its schools."],"essay":["Some people believe that cooking lessons belong in school, while others think that children should learn to cook at home. This essay believes that schools should teach cooking because it reaches every child, although family traditions also matter.","On the one hand, children can learn to cook at home because families pass on traditions. This is because parents and grandparents teach recipes, habits and values while they prepare meals together, and children enjoy this time with their family. Cooking at home also shows the dishes of their own culture. However, not every parent has the time or the skills to teach. This also encourages families to spend time together. For example, in many families the recipes of a grandmother are passed on from one generation to the next simply by cooking together in the kitchen.","On the other hand, schools should teach cooking because every child should have this life skill. That is to say, a school programme reaches all pupils, including those whose parents are very busy, and it can combine practical lessons with information about healthy eating. As a result, young people can prepare balanced meals when they live alone and are less likely to depend on fast food. For instance, England has included cooking and nutrition in the compulsory curriculum up to the age of 14 since 2014, and Japan teaches food education in its schools.","In conclusion, although families can pass on traditional recipes, this essay believes that schools should teach cooking because it gives all children a basic skill for healthy living. A combination of both would be ideal. Cooperation between teachers and parents would give the best results."],"wc":274},{"id":"food5","topic":"food","type":"Discussion","key":false,"q":"Technological and scientific advances are changing the food people eat. Some believe that these changes can be harmful to the health of the people. Others challenge this. Discuss both views and give your opinion.","ideas":["This essay believes that the changes are mostly beneficial, provided that strict rules and clear labels exist","Critics fear that new food technologies may damage health","Scientific progress produces more food, makes it cheaper and can improve nutrition"],"ex":["For example, Singapore approved the sale of meat grown in a laboratory in 2020, which led to a public debate about safety and labelling.","For instance, Golden Rice was developed to contain beta-carotene in order to fight vitamin A deficiency in children."],"essay":["Science and technology are changing the way that food is produced and eaten. Some people believe that these changes are harmful to health, while others disagree.","This essay believes that the changes are mostly beneficial, provided that strict rules and clear labels exist.","On the one hand, critics fear that new food technologies may damage health. This is because the long-term effects of genetically modified crops and laboratory-grown products are still uncertain, and large corporations control much of the technology, so consumers may not know what they are eating. Processed food can also contain additives and large amounts of salt and sugar. However, I think that these risks can be managed. Reliable information from scientists can calm these fears. For example, Singapore approved the sale of meat grown in a laboratory in 2020, which led to a public debate about safety and labelling.","On the other hand, scientific progress produces more food, makes it cheaper and can improve nutrition. That is to say, modern methods increase harvests and help to feed a growing population, and some crops are designed to contain vitamins that poor communities lack. If governments introduce regulation and clear labels, people can make informed choices and enjoy the benefits. In this way, technology can make healthy food more available. For instance, Golden Rice was developed to contain beta-carotene in order to fight vitamin A deficiency in children.","In conclusion, although new food technologies raise legitimate concerns about long-term safety, this essay believes that their benefits for production and nutrition are greater. Independent testing and honest labels will protect consumers."],"wc":262},{"id":"food6","topic":"food","type":"Opinion","key":false,"q":"In many countries, traditional foods are being replaced by fast foods. This has a negative impact on families, individuals and society. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","ideas":["Fast food harms families and individuals"],"ex":["For example, in many families the shared dinner is replaced by takeaway meals that are eaten in front of a screen.","For instance, the Slow Food movement and the EU quality labels for traditional products were created to protect local cuisine from this decline."],"essay":["In a growing number of countries, fast food is taking the place of traditional dishes. This essay agrees that this has a negative impact on families, individuals and society because it reduces time together, damages health and increases public costs.","Fast food harms families and individuals. This is because traditional meals are cooked and eaten together, while fast food is eaten quickly and often alone, so families lose an important moment of contact. For individuals, regular consumption of food that is high in fat, salt and sugar leads to obesity and diabetes. As a result, health problems appear at younger ages. Over time, children who grow up on fast food may never learn to enjoy local dishes. For example, in many families the shared dinner is replaced by takeaway meals that are eaten in front of a screen. Fast food also harms society, both financially and culturally. That is to say, diseases linked to poor diets increase medical costs for the state, and when local dishes disappear, a part of the national culture disappears with them. Fast food provides cheap and convenient meals, but these short-term benefits do not outweigh the long-term damage. Moreover, national dishes attract tourists, so their loss also reduces income. For instance, the Slow Food movement and the EU quality labels for traditional products were created to protect local cuisine from this decline.","In conclusion, this essay agrees that replacing traditional meals with fast food has a negative effect on families, individuals and society. Governments and schools should therefore promote home cooking and healthy local dishes. Early education can help to reverse this trend."],"wc":268},{"id":"family1","topic":"family","type":"Problem / Solution","key":true,"q":"Children have less respect for their elders than in the past. Why is this? What can be done about it?","ideas":["Family structures and sources of information have changed","Bring generations together and to teach respect in schools"],"ex":["For example, millions of workers migrate from villages to large cities every year, which leaves many grandparents living alone, far from their grandchildren.","For instance, in the Humanitas care home in Deventer in the Netherlands, university students live for free next to elderly residents, and China's law on the rights of older people even requires adult children to visit their parents."],"essay":["Young people today seem to show less respect for older relatives than earlier generations did. This is mainly caused by smaller families and the influence of the internet, and the most effective solutions are shared programmes between generations and lessons about values at school.","The main cause of this problem is that family structures and sources of information have changed. This is because families are now smaller and often live far from grandparents, who stay in villages while the young move to cities for work, so children rarely spend time with older people. In addition, young people can find any information on the internet by themselves, and the media often present elderly people as old-fashioned, so they no longer see them as a source of knowledge. As a result, respect gradually weakens. For example, millions of workers migrate from villages to large cities every year, which leaves many grandparents living alone, far from their grandchildren.","A viable solution is to bring generations together and to teach respect in schools. If young and old people share projects and daily activities, children discover the experience and kindness of older adults, and they begin to value them. At the same time, teachers can include lessons on family values, so that respect is taught as a habit and not only as a rule. In this way, both sides understand each other better. For instance, in the Humanitas care home in Deventer in the Netherlands, university students live for free next to elderly residents, and China's law on the rights of older people even requires adult children to visit their parents.","In conclusion, children respect their elders less because of smaller families and independent access to information, but shared programmes and values education can restore this respect. These measures would strengthen family ties and benefit the whole of society."],"wc":304},{"id":"family2","topic":"family","type":"Discussion","key":true,"q":"Some people think grandparents have a lot to teach their grandchildren, while others think they are too out of date to connect with them. Discuss both sides and give your opinion.","ideas":["Grandparents have a lot to teach because they carry traditions and life experience","Critics argue that grandparents may be out of date"],"ex":["For example, in southern European countries such as Italy and Spain, grandparents are often the main source of childcare while the parents are at work.","For instance, many families use video calls to teach grandparents how to use smartphones, while the grandparents teach the children traditional recipes in return."],"essay":["Some people believe that grandparents can pass on valuable lessons to their grandchildren, while others think that they are too old-fashioned to understand young people. This essay agrees with the first view because grandparents transmit traditions and give practical help, although they cannot replace parents.","On the one hand, grandparents have a lot to teach because they carry traditions and life experience. This is because they know family stories, customs and skills such as cooking or gardening that cannot be found in school books, and they usually have more patience than busy parents. As a result, children gain a stronger sense of identity and learn values through personal stories. In many families, grandparents also provide daily care, which allows parents to work. For example, in southern European countries such as Italy and Spain, grandparents are often the main source of childcare while the parents are at work.","On the other hand, critics argue that grandparents may be out of date. That is to say, they often know little about modern technology, and they may use old educational methods that disagree with the parents' approach, which can cause conflicts. However, this does not make their experience useless, because it complements the role of parents instead of replacing it, and both generations can learn from each other. For instance, many families use video calls to teach grandparents how to use smartphones, while the grandparents teach the children traditional recipes in return.","In conclusion, although some grandparents may not understand modern technology or methods, this essay believes that they have a great deal to teach because of their traditions and experience. Their guidance works best as a complement to the role of parents."],"wc":280},{"id":"family3","topic":"family","type":"Discussion","key":false,"q":"Some people think the government should take care of the elderly, while others think the family should. Discuss both sides and give your opinion.","ideas":["Governments should care for the elderly because they can provide money and medical services on a large scale","Families are better placed to provide emotional support"],"ex":["For example, Japan introduced public long-term care insurance in 2000, which pays for nursing services for its older citizens.","For instance, in Romania and in many other countries, families traditionally look after older relatives at home, which keeps public costs low and prevents isolation."],"essay":["Some people believe that the state should look after older citizens, while others think that this duty belongs to their families. This essay believes that the government should cover financial and medical needs, while families provide emotional support.","On the one hand, governments should care for the elderly because they can provide money and medical services on a large scale. This is because pensions, hospitals and care homes cost far more than most families can pay, and illnesses in old age often need professional treatment. If the state organises this, every older person receives help, including those who have no children. However, public care alone cannot meet every need. Without this support, many older people would live in poverty. For example, Japan introduced public long-term care insurance in 2000, which pays for nursing services for its older citizens.","On the other hand, families are better placed to provide emotional support. That is to say, elderly people need love and company, which professional staff cannot fully offer, and care at home is usually cheaper for the state. Moreover, children who care for their parents show gratitude and strengthen family bonds. This also reduces loneliness, which is a serious problem for many older people. For instance, in Romania and in many other countries, families traditionally look after older relatives at home, which keeps public costs low and prevents isolation.","In conclusion, although families give essential emotional care, this essay believes that governments should carry the main financial and medical responsibility for the elderly. Both sides should work together so that no older person is left alone. Together, they can ensure dignity in old age."],"wc":272},{"id":"family4","topic":"family","type":"Two-part Question","key":true,"q":"In the modern world, fewer families are eating their meals together. Why is this? Is it a positive or negative trend?","ideas":["Families eat together less often because of long working hours, screens and busy schedules","Negative because it weakens family bonds and leads to poorer eating habits"],"ex":["For example, in many cities both parents work until the evening, and children eat sandwiches or ready meals while using their devices.","For instance, the French gastronomic meal was added to UNESCO's list of intangible cultural heritage in 2010, partly because of its social role in bringing people together."],"essay":["Fewer families now sit down together for meals. This is mainly due to long working hours and the use of screens, and it should be seen as a negative trend because it weakens family relationships and harms diet.","Families eat together less often because of long working hours, screens and busy schedules. This is because parents often return home late, children have extra activities after school and everyone looks at a phone or a television instead of talking. In addition, cheap fast food makes it easy for each person to eat alone at a different time. As a result, the shared dinner is slowly disappearing from daily life. For example, in many cities both parents work until the evening, and children eat sandwiches or ready meals while using their devices.","This trend is negative because it weakens family bonds and leads to poorer eating habits. That is to say, the family meal is often the only time when everyone talks about their day, so without it, communication and trust decrease. Moreover, home-cooked meals are usually healthier than takeaway food, so the risk of obesity increases. A simple solution is to fix one meal every week without phones. For instance, the French gastronomic meal was added to UNESCO's list of intangible cultural heritage in 2010, partly because of its social role in bringing people together.","In conclusion, families eat together less because of busy schedules and technology, and this is a negative trend because it damages relationships and health. Setting a regular screen-free meal can bring the family back to the table."],"wc":261},{"id":"family5","topic":"family","type":"Problem / Solution","key":false,"q":"Many families do not spend much time communicating with each other. What are the reasons for this? What can be done about it?","ideas":["Devices and busy lives take attention away from family members","Create shared rituals and screen-free rules"],"ex":["For example, in a typical household, parents answer work messages in the evening while teenagers play online games in their rooms.","For instance, during the Covid-19 lockdowns many families rediscovered how valuable time together can be, as they cooked, played and talked every day."],"essay":["In a large number of homes, family members talk to each other very little. The main reasons are electronic devices and tiredness after work or school, and the most effective solutions are shared rituals and screen-free rules.","The main cause of poor communication is that devices and busy lives take attention away from family members. This is because everyone returns home tired and spends the evening with a phone, a game or a series, and different timetables mean that people are rarely at home together. Differences between generations can also make conversations difficult, since parents and teenagers often have different interests. As a result, family members share fewer thoughts and feelings. For example, in a typical household, parents answer work messages in the evening while teenagers play online games in their rooms.","A viable solution is to create shared rituals and screen-free rules. If families eat dinner together, go for walks or play games on fixed days, they have natural moments to talk, and a rule that phones stay away during these moments protects the conversation. Parents should also listen openly and avoid criticising immediately, so that children feel safe to speak. In this way, communication becomes a habit. For instance, during the Covid-19 lockdowns many families rediscovered how valuable time together can be, as they cooked, played and talked every day.","In conclusion, families communicate less because of devices, fatigue and different schedules, but shared rituals and phone-free time can improve the situation. Small daily habits would make family relationships stronger. Parents who lead by example will find that children follow."],"wc":262},{"id":"family6","topic":"family","type":"Two-part Question","key":false,"q":"Some women choose not to have children. Why is this? Is this a positive or negative trend?","ideas":["Many women choose not to have children because of careers, costs and an unequal share of childcare","Negative for society because it leads to an ageing population and a shortage of workers"],"ex":["For example, South Korea has a fertility rate below one child per woman, with high housing prices and long working hours among the reasons.","For instance, Sweden's long parental leave, with days reserved for each parent, helps couples to combine work and family life."],"essay":["A growing number of women decide not to become mothers. This is mainly due to career ambitions and the high cost of raising a child, and I believe that it is a negative trend for society in the long term, although it gives individuals more freedom.","Many women choose not to have children because of careers, costs and an unequal share of childcare. This is because education and professional goals take up much of early adulthood, so women often marry later, and housing and childcare are very expensive. In addition, mothers still do most of the childcare, which makes some women fear that they will lose their independence and their career progress. For example, South Korea has a fertility rate below one child per woman, with high housing prices and long working hours among the reasons.","This trend is negative for society because it leads to an ageing population and a shortage of workers. That is to say, when fewer children are born, there are fewer taxpayers to support pensions and healthcare for the growing number of older citizens. On the positive side, individuals gain personal freedom, but governments can reduce the problem by offering generous parental leave and affordable nurseries, so that women do not have to choose between a career and a family. For instance, Sweden's long parental leave, with days reserved for each parent, helps couples to combine work and family life.","In conclusion, women choose not to have children because of careers and costs, and this trend creates demographic problems for society. Supportive policies on leave and childcare can make parenthood easier and more attractive."],"wc":269},{"id":"family7","topic":"family","type":"Discussion","key":false,"q":"Some people think parents should be strict, but others think children need more freedom to make their own choices. Discuss both sides and give your opinion.","ideas":["Strict parents give children structure and safety","Freedom helps children to become independent and responsible"],"ex":["For example, the \"tiger mother\" style described by Amy Chua stresses rigid rules and very high expectations, but it has been criticised for the pressure that it puts on children.","For instance, in 2018 the US state of Utah passed a \"free-range parenting\" law that allows children to play and travel alone at a reasonable age."],"essay":["Some people believe that parents should apply strict rules, while others think that children need more freedom to decide for themselves. This essay believes that freedom within clear limits, combined with warmth, is the best approach.","On the one hand, strict parents give children structure and safety. This is because clear rules teach discipline and protect young children from dangers that they cannot yet understand, such as risky websites or bad company. However, parents who are too strict can cause anxiety or rebellion, since children may start to hide their behaviour instead of talking honestly. For example, the \"tiger mother\" style described by Amy Chua stresses rigid rules and very high expectations, but it has been criticised for the pressure that it puts on children.","On the other hand, freedom helps children to become independent and responsible. That is to say, when children make small decisions, they learn from their mistakes and gain confidence, whereas parents who control everything prevent them from developing. However, too much freedom leads to a lack of discipline, so limits remain necessary. The psychologist Diana Baumrind linked the \"authoritative\" style, which combines clear rules with warmth, to the best results. Parents should also explain the reasons for each rule. For instance, in 2018 the US state of Utah passed a \"free-range parenting\" law that allows children to play and travel alone at a reasonable age.","In conclusion, although strict rules protect children, this essay believes that they should be balanced with freedom and affection, because this combination develops responsible and confident adults. Parents should explain their rules and listen to their children. Both rules and love are therefore essential."],"wc":274},{"id":"world1","topic":"world","type":"Opinion","key":true,"q":"Poor countries need financial aid from richer countries. Do you think rich countries have an obligation to financially support poorer countries?","ideas":["Wealthy countries have a moral obligation to help people in need","Aid creates stability and benefits donors too"],"ex":["For example, the Marshall Plan helped to rebuild Western Europe after the Second World War, and South Korea, which received aid in the past, is now a donor country.","For instance, the Grameen Bank in Bangladesh gave small loans to poor families, which helped millions of people to start businesses and escape poverty."],"essay":["It is often argued that wealthy nations must give money to those that are poorer. This essay agrees with this view because rich countries have a moral duty to help, and because aid creates stability and economic partners for everyone.","The primary reason is that wealthy countries have a moral obligation to help people in need. This is because millions of people suffer from hunger, disease and poor education through no fault of their own, while richer nations have far more resources than they need. In addition, many rich countries benefited from colonial history and cheap imports, so support is also a matter of fairness. As a result, aid is an expression of basic human solidarity. For example, the Marshall Plan helped to rebuild Western Europe after the Second World War, and South Korea, which received aid in the past, is now a donor country.","Another important point is that aid creates stability and benefits donors too. That is to say, poverty can cause conflict, migration and the spread of diseases across borders, while a growing economy becomes a trading partner. Corruption and dependence are real risks, so aid should be monitored and combined with trade, debt relief and education. For instance, the Grameen Bank in Bangladesh gave small loans to poor families, which helped millions of people to start businesses and escape poverty.","In conclusion, this essay agrees that rich countries have an obligation to help poorer ones because of moral duty and shared interests. This help should be carefully managed so that it creates independence and not dependence."],"wc":260},{"id":"world2","topic":"world","type":"Problem / Solution","key":true,"q":"People are living longer than in the past. Why is this? What problems does this cause?","ideas":["People live longer mainly because of improved medicine, hygiene and nutrition"],"ex":["For example, in many developed countries, people live more than a decade longer than they did in the middle of the twentieth century.","For instance, Denmark links its retirement age to life expectancy, and Japan, which has one of the oldest populations in the world, is investing in care robots."],"essay":["The average life span is much longer today than it was several generations ago. This is mainly due to progress in medicine and better living standards, and it causes problems such as financial pressure on pensions and health services, which can be reduced by suitable reforms.","People live longer mainly because of improved medicine, hygiene and nutrition. This is because vaccines, antibiotics and surgery cure illnesses that were once deadly, and clean water and a better diet prevent many diseases. In addition, governments now organise health checks and public campaigns. As a result, life expectancy has increased significantly in most countries. Better treatment of heart disease and cancer has also added many years of life. For example, in many developed countries, people live more than a decade longer than they did in the middle of the twentieth century. The main problem is that an ageing population puts pressure on pensions, health care and the labour market. That is to say, there are fewer workers to pay taxes for a growing number of retired people, and older patients need more medical care. Governments can respond by raising the retirement age gradually, by using more automation and by accepting skilled immigrants. For instance, Denmark links its retirement age to life expectancy, and Japan, which has one of the oldest populations in the world, is investing in care robots.","In conclusion, people live longer because of better medicine and living conditions, but this creates financial and social pressure that must be managed with reforms. With good planning, longer lives can be a benefit for everyone. Cooperation between governments and employers is essential."],"wc":269},{"id":"world3","topic":"world","type":"Advantages / Disadvantages","key":true,"q":"Some schools encourage children to do volunteer work. What are the advantages and disadvantages of this?","ideas":["It develops empathy and social skills","Volunteering adds pressure and may lose its true meaning"],"ex":["For example, the US state of Maryland requires students to complete service-learning hours in order to graduate.","For instance, some students in schools with compulsory hours choose the easiest task just to complete the number required."],"essay":["In some countries, schools urge their pupils to help the community as volunteers. The main advantages of this practice are the development of empathy and social skills, whereas the main disadvantages are the extra workload and the risk that volunteering loses its true meaning.","The main advantage of school volunteering is that it develops empathy and social skills. This is because pupils who help others, for example by visiting elderly people or cleaning a park, understand the problems of their community and learn to cooperate. As a result, they become more responsible citizens, and they also gain experience that helps them in later applications. Many pupils also discover possible careers in healthcare or education. Volunteering also teaches pupils to be grateful for what they have. For example, the US state of Maryland requires students to complete service-learning hours in order to graduate.","One major disadvantage is that volunteering adds pressure and may lose its true meaning. In other words, pupils who already have a heavy timetable may feel tired, and if the activity is compulsory, they may do it only to collect hours without real interest. Moreover, children from poor families may have less time because they help at home. Teachers may also find it difficult to supervise so many separate projects. For instance, some students in schools with compulsory hours choose the easiest task just to complete the number required.","In conclusion, volunteering in schools builds empathy and teamwork, but it can increase workload and become a formality. Schools should therefore offer a free choice of projects and a reasonable number of hours. Voluntary participation keeps the activity meaningful."],"wc":270},{"id":"world4","topic":"world","type":"Two-part Question","key":false,"q":"In some countries, many people are moving out of cities to the countryside. Why is this? Is this a positive or negative trend?","ideas":["People move to the countryside mainly because of high costs in cities and the freedom to work from home","Positive because it reduces pressure on cities and revives rural communities"],"ex":["For example, after the pandemic many people in big cities decided to work from home and moved to cheaper areas with more space.","For instance, many small towns in Europe have introduced programmes to attract remote workers, with cheap housing and fast internet."],"essay":["In several countries, a growing number of residents leave big cities for villages and small towns. This is mainly due to expensive housing and the possibility of working remotely, and I believe that it is a positive trend because it relieves cities and brings life to rural areas.","People move to the countryside mainly because of high costs in cities and the freedom to work from home. This is because rents and house prices in large cities are very high, while rural homes are cheaper and offer more space, cleaner air and a calmer life. In addition, remote work allows many employees to live far from the office. As a result, families look for a better quality of life outside the city. For example, after the pandemic many people in big cities decided to work from home and moved to cheaper areas with more space.","This trend is positive because it reduces pressure on cities and revives rural communities. That is to say, fewer people in the city means less traffic and lower rents, and newcomers bring money, skills and children to villages that were losing population. However, rural areas need good internet, schools and doctors so that this change is successful. For instance, many small towns in Europe have introduced programmes to attract remote workers, with cheap housing and fast internet.","In conclusion, people move to the countryside because of high living costs and remote work, and this is a positive trend if rural services are improved. Governments should invest in rural infrastructure to support this change. Fair planning would help both cities and villages."],"wc":266},{"id":"world5","topic":"world","type":"Opinion","key":false,"q":"The government should focus on building new housing for the growing population, not renovating existing old buildings. To what extent do you agree?","ideas":["New housing is necessary to solve the housing shortage","Renovating old buildings is cheaper and more sustainable"],"ex":["For example, many large cities face a housing crisis with rents that are too high for ordinary workers.","For instance, the European Union launched the \"Renovation Wave\" strategy in 2020 to improve millions of existing buildings."],"essay":["It is suggested that authorities should build more new homes instead of repairing old ones. This essay partly disagrees with this view because renovation protects heritage and is more sustainable, although new housing is also needed.","The primary reason is that new housing is necessary to solve the housing shortage. This is because growing cities need more homes for young families, and new buildings can be designed with modern insulation, which saves energy. As a result, new construction can lower rents and give more people a place to live. Therefore, a large building programme cannot be avoided. Moreover, young families often cannot afford to buy a home in the city centre. For example, many large cities face a housing crisis with rents that are too high for ordinary workers.","Another important point is that renovating old buildings is cheaper and more sustainable. That is to say, repairing an existing building uses fewer materials and creates less pollution than building a new one, and it protects the historic character of a city. Old buildings can be made energy-efficient and turned into modern flats. Many old buildings also have thick walls and good materials that are worth keeping. Renovation also provides jobs for local craftspeople. For instance, the European Union launched the \"Renovation Wave\" strategy in 2020 to improve millions of existing buildings.","In conclusion, this essay partly disagrees that the government should focus only on new construction because renovation is cheaper, greener and protects heritage, although new homes are also needed. A balanced programme would give the best results. Governments should support both because the needs are different in each city."],"wc":270},{"id":"world6","topic":"world","type":"Opinion","key":false,"q":"Due to the growing population, some people think that companies should be relocated to the countryside. Do you agree?","ideas":["Relocating companies reduces pressure on cities and helps rural areas","Many companies cannot easily operate outside cities"],"ex":["For example, Brazil moved its capital to the newly built city of Brasília in 1960 to develop the interior of the country.","For instance, technology firms group together in places such as Silicon Valley, where they find talent, investors and partners within a short distance."],"essay":["It is proposed that businesses should move away from crowded cities because of the increase in population. This essay partly agrees with this idea because it reduces traffic and helps rural areas, although companies need skilled workers and good infrastructure.","The primary reason is that relocating companies reduces pressure on cities and helps rural areas. This is because fewer offices in the centre means less traffic, lower pollution and cheaper land, and rural communities receive jobs and investment that slow down depopulation. As a result, population growth is spread more evenly across the country. Companies would also pay lower rents, which could increase their profits. For example, Brazil moved its capital to the newly built city of Brasília in 1960 to develop the interior of the country.","Another important point is that many companies cannot easily operate outside cities. That is to say, firms need skilled employees, customers, suppliers and fast transport, and these are concentrated in urban areas. Companies in the same industry also gain from being close to each other. Therefore, governments should offer incentives and infrastructure instead of forcing relocation. Workers may also refuse to move far from their families, schools and friends. For instance, technology firms group together in places such as Silicon Valley, where they find talent, investors and partners within a short distance.","In conclusion, this essay partly agrees that relocating companies to the countryside would reduce pressure on cities, but it believes that it should be encouraged by incentives and good infrastructure and not by orders. Voluntary change is more likely to succeed. Cooperation between companies and local authorities is essential."],"wc":268},{"id":"world7","topic":"world","type":"Opinion","key":false,"q":"In some countries, many workers move to the city to find work. Is this a positive or negative trend?","ideas":["Migration to cities creates overcrowding and housing problems","Rural areas lose their workers and young people"],"ex":["For example, Dharavi in Mumbai is a very crowded area where hundreds of thousands of people live in small homes with limited services.","For instance, millions of rural migrants in China work in big cities without full access to local services because of the \"hukou\" system, and many Romanian villages have lost a large part of their population to the biggest cities."],"essay":["In a number of countries, large numbers of people leave villages to look for jobs in cities. This essay believes that this is a negative trend overall because it causes overcrowding in cities and the decline of rural areas, although it gives individuals better opportunities.","The primary reason is that migration to cities creates overcrowding and housing problems. This is because cities cannot build homes and services as quickly as people arrive, so rents become very high and many migrants live in poor neighbourhoods. As a result, traffic, pollution and unemployment increase. It is true that workers often find better salaries, education and health care, but these benefits are not available to everyone. For example, Dharavi in Mumbai is a very crowded area where hundreds of thousands of people live in small homes with limited services.","Another important point is that rural areas lose their workers and young people. That is to say, when the young leave, villages have fewer children, schools close and businesses disappear, so the remaining residents are mostly elderly. Governments can reduce this problem by investing in rural infrastructure, encouraging remote work and giving incentives to companies that create jobs outside cities. For instance, millions of rural migrants in China work in big cities without full access to local services because of the \"hukou\" system, and many Romanian villages have lost a large part of their population to the biggest cities.","In conclusion, this essay believes that migration to cities is a negative trend because it overloads cities and weakens villages, although it can help individuals. Investment in rural areas would balance this movement."],"wc":268},{"id":"world8","topic":"world","type":"Problem / Solution","key":false,"q":"Many major cities have a problem with homelessness. How can this be solved?","ideas":["The main causes of homelessness are expensive housing, unemployment and personal crises","Provide housing first and support afterwards"],"ex":["For example, in many large cities a single month of unemployment can make it impossible for a family to pay the rent.","For instance, Finland adopted the \"Housing First\" approach and has strongly reduced homelessness, while Vienna provides affordable municipal housing to a large share of its population."],"essay":["Large cities often have many people who sleep on the streets. The main causes of this problem are high rents and unemployment, and the most effective solutions are giving people a stable home first and then training and support.","The main causes of homelessness are expensive housing, unemployment and personal crises. This is because rents in large cities are higher than many salaries, so a lost job or a family breakdown can quickly lead to the loss of a home. In addition, mental illness and addiction make it harder for some people to keep a job or an apartment. As a result, they end up on the street without help. Moreover, in winter the lack of shelter can threaten the lives of people on the street. For example, in many large cities a single month of unemployment can make it impossible for a family to pay the rent.","A viable solution is to provide housing first and support afterwards. If homeless people receive a stable home, they can find a job, receive treatment and rebuild their lives much more easily than if they live in temporary shelters. Governments should also build affordable housing and offer rent support and professional training. For instance, Finland adopted the \"Housing First\" approach and has strongly reduced homelessness, while Vienna provides affordable municipal housing to a large share of its population.","In conclusion, homelessness is caused by high rents, unemployment and personal problems, but it can be solved by giving people homes first and offering training and care. Such a policy is often cheaper than leaving people on the streets. Prevention and early help would also reduce the number of new cases."],"wc":277},{"id":"world9","topic":"world","type":"Opinion","key":false,"q":"Houses are essential for people. Some argue that governments should offer free houses for people who cannot afford to pay for it. Do you agree or disagree?","ideas":["Housing is a basic human need","Completely free houses are expensive and may be unfair, so subsidised housing is a better solution"],"ex":["For example, Finland has strongly reduced homelessness by giving people homes before other support.","For instance, a large share of residents in Vienna live in municipal or subsidised flats, and about four-fifths of people in Singapore live in public housing built by the HDB."],"essay":["It is argued that the state should give free homes to those who cannot pay for them. This essay partly agrees with this idea because housing is a basic need, but it believes that affordable social housing is better than completely free houses.","The primary reason is that housing is a basic human need. This is because people without a safe home cannot keep a job, stay healthy or educate their children, so the whole of society suffers. If governments help the poorest citizens, they reduce homelessness and the costs of emergency services. As a result, support for housing is an investment and not only an expense. Moreover, wealthy and middle-income families often receive more help than the poorest groups, so priorities should be clear. For example, Finland has strongly reduced homelessness by giving people homes before other support.","Another important point is that completely free houses are expensive and may be unfair, so subsidised housing is a better solution. That is to say, giving homes for nothing would cost taxpayers enormous amounts and could discourage people from working, while social housing with low rents helps people and keeps costs reasonable. Priority should be given to those with the greatest need. For instance, a large share of residents in Vienna live in municipal or subsidised flats, and about four-fifths of people in Singapore live in public housing built by the HDB.","In conclusion, this essay partly agrees that governments should help those who cannot afford homes, but it believes that low-cost social housing is fairer and more sustainable than free houses. This approach protects both vulnerable people and public finances. Such a policy would also reduce public spending in the long term."],"wc":282},{"id":"transport1","topic":"transport","type":"Problem / Solution","key":true,"q":"More and more cities are struggling with air pollution. Why is this? What solutions can you suggest?","ideas":["The main cause of urban air pollution is the large number of vehicles, together with heating and industry","Reduce the use of polluting vehicles and to offer clean alternatives"],"ex":["For example, the World Health Organization estimates that air pollution causes millions of premature deaths every year around the world.","For instance, London introduced the Ultra Low Emission Zone in 2019, and roadside pollution in the centre fell significantly afterwards."],"essay":["A growing number of urban areas suffer from dirty air. The main causes of this problem are heavy traffic and the burning of fuel for heating and industry, and the most effective solutions are low-emission zones and better public transport.","The main cause of urban air pollution is the large number of vehicles, together with heating and industry. This is because millions of cars burn petrol and diesel in a small area every day, and factories and old heating systems release additional smoke and dust. As a result, harmful particles accumulate in the air and cause breathing diseases, especially in children and elderly people. In winter, these effects are even stronger. Moreover, dust from tyres and brakes adds to the problem even in electric traffic. For example, the World Health Organization estimates that air pollution causes millions of premature deaths every year around the world.","A viable solution is to reduce the use of polluting vehicles and to offer clean alternatives. If cities charge older and more polluting cars for entering the centre, and if they invest in frequent and cheap buses, trams and metro lines, many people will leave their cars at home. Electric vehicles and cycle lanes also help to clean the air. For instance, London introduced the Ultra Low Emission Zone in 2019, and roadside pollution in the centre fell significantly afterwards.","In conclusion, urban air pollution is caused mainly by traffic, heating and industry, but low-emission zones, public transport and cleaner vehicles can reduce it. Cities that act early will protect the health of their citizens. Clean air is a basic right for every resident."],"wc":269},{"id":"transport2","topic":"transport","type":"Problem / Solution","key":false,"q":"Budget air travel has become increasingly popular. What problems has this caused?","ideas":["Cheap flights increase pollution and climate change"],"ex":["For example, France restricted domestic flights on routes where a train journey takes only a few hours.","For instance, cities such as Venice and Barcelona have introduced measures to control the effects of mass tourism."],"essay":["Low-cost airlines have made flying affordable for millions of people. The main problems caused by this development are environmental damage and overcrowded tourist destinations, and these can be reduced through taxes and limits.","The main problem is that cheap flights increase pollution and climate change. This is because tickets that cost very little encourage people to fly many times a year, and aircraft burn large amounts of fuel and release carbon dioxide. In addition, cheap flights compete with trains, so people choose the most polluting option for short distances. As a result, the number of flights and emissions continues to grow. Many travellers also take several short trips a year instead of one longer holiday. For example, France restricted domestic flights on routes where a train journey takes only a few hours. Another problem is overtourism and pressure on employees. That is to say, cheap flights bring huge crowds to small cities, which raises rents and disturbs the daily life of residents, and airlines keep their prices low by cutting costs, which can mean low pay and long hours for staff. Governments can respond with taxes on frequent flyers, limits on visitor numbers and better train services. Local residents are rarely asked whether they want these crowds. For instance, cities such as Venice and Barcelona have introduced measures to control the effects of mass tourism.","In conclusion, budget air travel makes journeys accessible but it harms the environment, burdens popular destinations and pressures staff. Fair taxes and good rail alternatives can limit these problems. Responsible travel policies protect both nature and communities."],"wc":262},{"id":"transport3","topic":"transport","type":"Opinion","key":true,"q":"Long distance flights are bad for the environment and should be discouraged. To what extent do you agree?","ideas":["Aviation produces a significant share of greenhouse gas emissions","Long-distance flights cannot be removed completely because they serve important needs"],"ex":["For example, aviation produces about two and a half per cent of global carbon dioxide emissions, and Austria's ÖBB Nightjet trains offer a lower-carbon alternative on many European routes.","For instance, many emigrants depend on direct flights to visit their relatives, and tourism is a major source of income for island countries."],"essay":["It is argued that international flights harm the planet and that governments should discourage them. This essay partly agrees with this view because aviation produces significant emissions, but it believes that these flights are also necessary for business and families.","The primary reason is that aviation produces a significant share of greenhouse gas emissions. This is because aircraft burn huge amounts of fuel at high altitude, and one long journey can create more carbon dioxide per passenger than many months of driving. As a result, frequent flying makes it harder to meet climate targets. Governments could use taxes on frequent flyers and support night trains and cleaner fuels. Many airlines are also working on cleaner fuels, but these are not yet widely available. For example, aviation produces about two and a half per cent of global carbon dioxide emissions, and Austria's ÖBB Nightjet trains offer a lower-carbon alternative on many European routes.","Another important point is that long-distance flights cannot be removed completely because they serve important needs. That is to say, they connect businesses, allow families to meet and support tourism, which provides jobs in many developing countries. Therefore, a better approach is to reduce unnecessary flights and develop sustainable fuels instead of banning travel. For instance, many emigrants depend on direct flights to visit their relatives, and tourism is a major source of income for island countries.","In conclusion, this essay partly agrees that long flights should be discouraged because of their emissions, but it believes that they should be reduced and cleaned instead of banned. Taxes and cleaner technology are fair and practical solutions. A balanced policy is more realistic than a ban."],"wc":276},{"id":"transport4","topic":"transport","type":"Opinion","key":false,"q":"Electric cars are the way forward to a cleaner world. Do you agree?","ideas":["Electric cars have no exhaust emissions and are cheap to run","Electric cars have limits, so they are only a part of the solution"],"ex":["For example, Norway has one of the highest shares of electric cars in the world, and its cities have noticeably cleaner air than before.","For instance, mining of cobalt for batteries has raised concerns about working conditions and environmental damage in several countries."],"essay":["It is often stated that electric vehicles will make the world cleaner. This essay partly agrees with this idea because electric cars reduce local pollution, but it believes that they are not enough without public transport and cleaner electricity.","The primary reason is that electric cars have no exhaust emissions and are cheap to run. This is because they use electricity instead of petrol, so they do not release harmful gases in city streets, and their running costs are lower. As a result, air quality improves and drivers save money. When the electricity comes from wind or solar power, the benefit for the climate is even greater. In some countries, a large part of the electricity still comes from coal, which reduces the benefit. For example, Norway has one of the highest shares of electric cars in the world, and its cities have noticeably cleaner air than before.","Another important point is that electric cars have limits, so they are only a part of the solution. That is to say, making batteries requires mining materials such as cobalt and a lot of energy, charging stations are still limited and prices are high. Moreover, millions of electric cars still cause traffic jams, so cities also need public transport, bicycles and walking. For instance, mining of cobalt for batteries has raised concerns about working conditions and environmental damage in several countries.","In conclusion, this essay partly agrees that electric cars lead to a cleaner world, but they work best together with renewable electricity and strong public transport. A single technology cannot solve every transport problem. Cities should invest in all forms of clean transport."],"wc":272},{"id":"transport5","topic":"transport","type":"Problem / Solution","key":false,"q":"City centres often have traffic congestion. What problems does this cause? How can traffic congestion be resolved?","ideas":["The main problems of traffic congestion are lost time, stress and pollution","Charge drivers for entering the centre and to provide attractive alternatives"],"ex":["For example, in some of the busiest cities, drivers lose dozens of hours every year in traffic jams.","For instance, London introduced a congestion charge in 2003, Stockholm in 2007, and both cities recorded a clear reduction in traffic."],"essay":["Many city centres are filled with slow-moving traffic. The main problems that this causes are wasted time and stress as well as pollution, and the most viable solutions are congestion charges and better alternatives to the car.","The main problems of traffic congestion are lost time, stress and pollution. This is because drivers spend long periods stuck in queues, which makes them late for work and frustrated, and idling engines release harmful gases. In addition, delayed deliveries and buses increase costs for businesses. As a result, the quality of life in the city falls. Moreover, noise and stress from traffic affect the health of people who live near busy roads. For example, in some of the busiest cities, drivers lose dozens of hours every year in traffic jams.","A viable solution is to charge drivers for entering the centre and to provide attractive alternatives. If people have to pay to drive into the centre, many will choose public transport, and safe cycle lanes, flexible working hours and remote work can reduce the number of journeys at the busiest times. The money raised can also be used to improve buses and trains. Cities can also give priority to buses so that they move faster than cars. For instance, London introduced a congestion charge in 2003, Stockholm in 2007, and both cities recorded a clear reduction in traffic.","In conclusion, traffic congestion wastes time, creates stress and increases pollution, but congestion charges and better alternatives can reduce it. Cities should combine these measures with flexible working patterns. These changes would make city centres more pleasant for everyone."],"wc":265},{"id":"transport6","topic":"transport","type":"Discussion","key":true,"q":"Some countries invest more money in public transport, while other countries invest more money in expanding roads for private vehicles. Discuss both sides and give your opinion.","ideas":["New roads are important for freight and for rural areas","Public transport carries more people, produces fewer emissions and offers equal access"],"ex":["For example, farmers in remote regions depend on roads to bring their products to market.","For instance, the Katy Freeway in Houston was widened to a very large size, but congestion did not disappear, while Curitiba in Brazil has used dedicated rapid bus lanes since the 1970s and Vienna offers a cheap annual ticket."],"essay":["Some governments spend most of their transport budget on buses and trains, while others focus on building more roads for cars. This essay believes that investing in public transport is the better choice, although roads remain necessary in certain areas.","On the one hand, new roads are important for freight and for rural areas. This is because lorries need good roads to deliver goods, and people in villages often have no train station nearby, so a car is their only option. A well-built road network also helps emergency services to reach remote places quickly. However, building roads cannot solve city congestion. Moreover, buses also need roads, so a good road network is useful for public transport too. For example, farmers in remote regions depend on roads to bring their products to market.","On the other hand, public transport carries more people, produces fewer emissions and offers equal access. That is to say, one train can replace hundreds of cars, and cheap tickets allow people without a vehicle to reach jobs and schools. In contrast, wider roads often attract more cars, so the traffic quickly returns, which is known as \"induced demand\". For instance, the Katy Freeway in Houston was widened to a very large size, but congestion did not disappear, while Curitiba in Brazil has used dedicated rapid bus lanes since the 1970s and Vienna offers a cheap annual ticket.","In conclusion, although roads are necessary for rural areas and freight, this essay believes that public transport deserves more investment because it is cleaner and fairer. A balanced plan should include both, with priority for public services. Governments should plan transport with the environment in mind."],"wc":276},{"id":"transport7","topic":"transport","type":"Problem / Solution","key":false,"q":"Nowadays public transport prices are constantly increasing. Why do you think this is happening? How can this problem be solved?","ideas":["Operating costs are increasing while subsidies and passenger numbers are falling","Increase subsidies and to make fares simpler and fairer"],"ex":["For example, many operators in Europe reported higher energy bills in 2022, which pushed fares up.","For instance, Germany introduced a nine-euro monthly ticket in the summer of 2022 and the Deutschlandticket in 2023, and Austria offers the Klimaticket for travel across the country."],"essay":["The cost of buses, trams and trains is rising year after year. The main reasons for this are higher energy and staff costs and reduced public funding, and the most effective solutions are subsidies and integrated tickets.","The main cause is that operating costs are increasing while subsidies and passenger numbers are falling. This is because energy prices, wages and maintenance of old infrastructure have risen with inflation, and after the pandemic, fewer people use public transport, so ticket income is lower. In addition, some governments have reduced their financial support. As a result, companies raise prices to cover their losses. Small towns and rural lines suffer most, because fewer people use them. For example, many operators in Europe reported higher energy bills in 2022, which pushed fares up.","A viable solution is to increase subsidies and to make fares simpler and fairer. If governments finance public transport as an essential service, using money from congestion charges or fuel taxes, prices can stay low and more people will use it. Integrated tickets and discounts for students and elderly people also help. Low prices also attract new passengers, which improves the financial results of the service. For instance, Germany introduced a nine-euro monthly ticket in the summer of 2022 and the Deutschlandticket in 2023, and Austria offers the Klimaticket for travel across the country.","In conclusion, public transport prices rise because of higher costs and lower funding, but subsidies and integrated tickets can keep them affordable. Cheaper fares would reduce traffic and pollution as well. Fair prices would help low-income families in particular."],"wc":262},{"id":"technology1","topic":"technology","type":"Opinion","key":true,"q":"Some people think that teachers will one day be replaced in the classroom by technology. To what extent do you agree?","ideas":["Teachers provide support that computers cannot offer","Technology makes teachers more effective instead of replacing them"],"ex":["For example, the application Grammarly can find grammar mistakes, but it cannot explain to a learner why the mistake was made or how to avoid it in the future.","For instance, Khan Academy offers free online lessons, but it is mostly used to support teachers in classrooms and not to replace them."],"essay":["It is predicted that artificial intelligence and computers will eventually take over the role of teachers in schools. This essay disagrees with this prediction because teachers give feedback, motivation and discipline that machines cannot provide, and because technology works best as a tool for teachers.","The primary reason is that teachers provide support that computers cannot offer. This is because students need feedback, encouragement and discipline, and a human teacher notices when a pupil is confused, tired or unhappy. As a result, learners stay motivated and complete their work. A program can check answers, but it cannot understand the emotions of a child or control a noisy class. Teachers also teach social skills, which a screen cannot replace. For example, the application Grammarly can find grammar mistakes, but it cannot explain to a learner why the mistake was made or how to avoid it in the future.","Another important point is that technology makes teachers more effective instead of replacing them. That is to say, online tools allow the best teachers to reach more students, give practice exercises and save time on marking, so teachers can focus on personal support. In this way, education improves without removing the human element. Schools that combine both approaches get the best results. For instance, Khan Academy offers free online lessons, but it is mostly used to support teachers in classrooms and not to replace them.","In conclusion, this essay disagrees that technology will replace teachers because pupils need human guidance and motivation. Schools should therefore use technology as an assistant that makes good teachers even better. Good teaching will therefore remain a human profession."],"wc":271},{"id":"technology2","topic":"technology","type":"Opinion","key":false,"q":"Some people think that the more technology develops, the fewer jobs there will be. To what extent do you agree?","ideas":["The primary reason for concern is that technology eliminates some jobs","Technology also creates new jobs"],"ex":["For example, the World Economic Forum estimated in 2020 that automation would remove millions of jobs by the middle of this decade.","For instance, the same World Economic Forum report estimated that even more new jobs would appear than disappear, and countries such as Denmark and Singapore support workers with retraining schemes like \"flexicurity\" and SkillsFuture."],"essay":["It is feared that as machines become more advanced, human workers will lose their jobs. This essay partly agrees with this view because automation removes some occupations, but it believes that technology also creates new jobs.","The primary reason for concern is that technology eliminates some jobs. This is because machines and software can perform repetitive tasks faster and more cheaply than people, so companies need fewer workers in factories, offices and shops. As a result, employees who do routine work may find it difficult to find new employment without retraining. Even skilled employees can be replaced when a program becomes cheaper than a salary. For example, the World Economic Forum estimated in 2020 that automation would remove millions of jobs by the middle of this decade.","Another important point is that technology also creates new jobs. That is to say, new industries need programmers, engineers, data analysts and technicians, and cheaper products create demand in other sectors. However, workers must learn new skills, so governments and employers should invest in retraining and continuous learning. In this way, society can adapt. Governments should also support workers who lose their jobs during this transition. For instance, the same World Economic Forum report estimated that even more new jobs would appear than disappear, and countries such as Denmark and Singapore support workers with retraining schemes like \"flexicurity\" and SkillsFuture.","In conclusion, this essay partly agrees that technology eliminates some jobs, but it believes that new occupations will appear if workers can retrain. Education systems should therefore teach skills that machines cannot easily copy. Societies that prepare early will gain the most."],"wc":269},{"id":"technology3","topic":"technology","type":"Advantages / Disadvantages","key":true,"q":"Most people own a smart phone. What are the benefits of a smart phone? Are there any disadvantages?","ideas":["The main benefit of a smartphone is that it provides communication, information and services in one device","Smartphones can cause addiction, distraction and a loss of privacy"],"ex":["For example, the mobile money system M-Pesa, launched in Kenya in 2007, brought financial services to people who had no access to banks.","For instance, many schools now ban phones during lessons because pupils were distracted by messages and social media."],"essay":["Smartphones have become a normal part of daily life for most people. The main benefits of these devices are communication and access to information and services, whereas the main disadvantages are addiction and loss of privacy.","The main benefit of a smartphone is that it provides communication, information and services in one device. This is because a phone allows users to call, send messages, find directions, bank, shop and call for help in an emergency, so people save time and feel safer. In addition, it gives easy access to news and education. As a result, many tasks that once needed several devices can be done anywhere. Many people also use their phones as cameras, music players and learning tools. For example, the mobile money system M-Pesa, launched in Kenya in 2007, brought financial services to people who had no access to banks.","One major disadvantage is that smartphones can cause addiction, distraction and a loss of privacy. In other words, notifications constantly interrupt concentration, and many users spend hours scrolling, which reduces sleep and face-to-face conversation. Moreover, apps collect large amounts of personal data, which can be misused. Parents and employers therefore need to set sensible limits. Excessive screen time can also harm eyesight and concentration. For instance, many schools now ban phones during lessons because pupils were distracted by messages and social media.","In conclusion, smartphones offer communication, information and useful services, but they can lead to dependence and privacy problems. Users should therefore control their phone time instead of letting the phone control them. Balanced use is the key."],"wc":261},{"id":"technology4","topic":"technology","type":"Advantages / Disadvantages","key":false,"q":"Some images and videos online are created by AI. What are the advantages and disadvantages of this?","ideas":["AI allows more people to create content quickly and cheaply","AI images can spread false information and harm creative professionals"],"ex":["For example, teachers can generate pictures to illustrate a history lesson in a few minutes instead of searching for suitable images.","For instance, a fake video of President Zelensky asking soldiers to surrender spread online in 2022, and in 2023 an image of the Pope in a white jacket went viral, although it was not real."],"essay":["Artificial intelligence is now able to produce realistic pictures and videos. The main advantages of this are creativity and lower costs, whereas the main disadvantages are false information and threats to creative workers.","The main advantage is that AI allows more people to create content quickly and cheaply. This is because anybody can produce an illustration or a video without expensive equipment or years of training, so small businesses, teachers and students can make professional-looking material. In addition, the technology can be used for education, for example to recreate historical events. As a result, creativity becomes more accessible. Moreover, translation and editing tools save hours of routine work. For example, teachers can generate pictures to illustrate a history lesson in a few minutes instead of searching for suitable images.","One major disadvantage is that AI images can spread false information and harm creative professionals. In other words, realistic fake videos can be used to deceive the public, and artists and photographers fear that their work is being copied and that they will lose income. There are also unresolved questions about copyright. Clear labelling and laws are therefore necessary. For instance, a fake video of President Zelensky asking soldiers to surrender spread online in 2022, and in 2023 an image of the Pope in a white jacket went viral, although it was not real.","In conclusion, AI-generated media offer creativity and low costs, but they can spread misinformation and threaten the work of artists. Rules such as clear labels, like those in the EU AI Act, are needed to reduce these risks. Society must learn to use this technology responsibly."],"wc":269},{"id":"technology5","topic":"technology","type":"Opinion","key":false,"q":"Some people think that AI will one day dominate humanity. To what extent do you agree?","ideas":["Today's AI is narrow and controlled by people","The real dangers come from misuse, and these can be managed with regulation"],"ex":["For example, the computer Deep Blue defeated the chess champion Garry Kasparov in 1997, but it could not do anything except play chess.","For instance, in 2023 the United Kingdom hosted the Bletchley Park summit on AI safety, where many countries agreed to work together on this issue."],"essay":["It is sometimes feared that artificial intelligence will become so powerful that it will rule over people. This essay disagrees with this view because current AI is limited and controlled by humans, although real risks exist and need regulation.","The primary reason is that today's AI is narrow and controlled by people. This is because every system is designed for specific tasks and depends on human-made data, hardware and energy, so it cannot act freely or want anything. Humans can also switch these systems off or change their rules. As a result, the idea of machines taking control remains closer to fiction than to reality. In addition, no current system understands the world in the way that a human does. For example, the computer Deep Blue defeated the chess champion Garry Kasparov in 1997, but it could not do anything except play chess.","Another important point is that the real dangers come from misuse, and these can be managed with regulation. That is to say, risks such as biased algorithms, autonomous weapons and false information are caused by human decisions, so governments must set clear rules and cooperate internationally. This is a more realistic task than preparing for machine domination. International agreements can reduce the danger of reckless development. For instance, in 2023 the United Kingdom hosted the Bletchley Park summit on AI safety, where many countries agreed to work together on this issue.","In conclusion, this essay disagrees that AI will dominate humanity because it is limited and human-controlled, but it recognises that regulation is needed to prevent misuse. Careful cooperation will keep this technology useful. Fear of the future should not prevent sensible progress."],"wc":276},{"id":"technology6","topic":"technology","type":"Advantages / Disadvantages","key":false,"q":"Some countries spend a lot of money on space exploration. What are the advantages and disadvantages of this?","ideas":["It produces useful technology and knowledge","Space missions are extremely expensive"],"ex":["For example, the James Webb Space Telescope has sent back images that help scientists to study the early universe, and satellites now provide weather forecasts and GPS to billions of people.","For instance, a single large space telescope or a mission to another planet can cost billions of dollars, which is more than the annual health budget of some small countries."],"essay":["Several governments invest large sums in missions beyond our planet. The main advantages of this are useful technology and scientific knowledge, whereas the main disadvantage is the high cost when serious problems exist on Earth.","The main advantage of space exploration is that it produces useful technology and knowledge. This is because space programmes develop new materials, medical devices and satellites that improve daily life, for example in weather forecasting, navigation and communication. In addition, discoveries about other planets help scientists to understand the Earth and its climate. As a result, much of the investment returns to society. Moreover, space projects inspire young people to study science and engineering. For example, the James Webb Space Telescope has sent back images that help scientists to study the early universe, and satellites now provide weather forecasts and GPS to billions of people.","One major disadvantage is that space missions are extremely expensive. In other words, the same money could be used to reduce poverty, improve hospitals or fight climate change, which affect people immediately. Critics also point out that many results of space research are uncertain and slow. For this reason, governments should balance space budgets with urgent social needs. Private companies could pay for a larger share of these projects. For instance, a single large space telescope or a mission to another planet can cost billions of dollars, which is more than the annual health budget of some small countries.","In conclusion, space exploration brings technology and knowledge, but it costs a great deal of money that could solve problems on Earth. A balanced budget and international cooperation can make it more worthwhile."],"wc":272},{"id":"technology7","topic":"technology","type":"Two-part Question","key":true,"q":"Nowadays more and more tasks at home and at work are being performed by robots. Why do you think it is happening? Is it a negative or positive development?","ideas":["Robots are used more often because they are cheaper in the long term, accurate and able to do dangerous work"],"ex":["For example, Amazon uses thousands of robots in its warehouses, and Japan uses care robots to assist its growing elderly population.","For instance, surgical robots allow doctors to perform operations through very small cuts, which shortens recovery times for patients."],"essay":["Robots are taking over a growing number of tasks in homes and workplaces. This is mainly due to lower costs and the need for accuracy and safety, and it should be seen as a positive development because it increases efficiency and protects workers.","Robots are used more often because they are cheaper in the long term, accurate and able to do dangerous work. This is because a machine does not get tired, does not need a salary and makes few mistakes, so companies save money and improve quality. In addition, populations are ageing, and robots can help to do tasks that fewer workers are available to do. As a result, they appear in factories, warehouses and even homes. For example, Amazon uses thousands of robots in its warehouses, and Japan uses care robots to assist its growing elderly population. This is a positive development because it increases efficiency and safety, although it may cause unemployment. That is to say, robots can handle dangerous tasks such as bomb disposal and assist in surgery with great precision, which saves lives, and they free people from boring work. The danger is that some workers lose their jobs, but retraining programmes can help them move to new roles. For instance, surgical robots allow doctors to perform operations through very small cuts, which shortens recovery times for patients.","In conclusion, robots are spreading because they save money, work accurately and handle dangerous jobs, and this is a positive development if workers are helped to retrain. Governments and companies should plan this change carefully. With wise planning, everyone can profit from this change."],"wc":267},{"id":"technology8","topic":"technology","type":"Opinion","key":false,"q":"There have been many inventions in human history, such as the wheel. Some people think the most important thing is the internet. To what extent do you agree or disagree with this opinion?","ideas":["The internet could not exist without older and more basic inventions","Some inventions have saved far more lives"],"ex":["For example, a power cut makes the internet unusable within minutes, which shows how dependent it is on electricity.","For instance, penicillin, discovered in 1928, has saved millions of lives by treating bacterial infections that were once fatal."],"essay":["It is argued that the internet is the greatest of all human inventions. This essay disagrees with this opinion because the internet depends on earlier discoveries, and because other inventions have saved more lives and shaped civilisation more deeply.","The primary reason is that the internet could not exist without older and more basic inventions. This is because it depends on electricity, which powers every server and device, and on the printing press and the wheel, which spread knowledge and trade across the world. As a result, these earlier discoveries are the foundation of modern life, and without them there would be no network at all. Other basic inventions, such as writing and the compass, also made the modern world possible. For example, a power cut makes the internet unusable within minutes, which shows how dependent it is on electricity.","Another important point is that some inventions have saved far more lives. That is to say, medical discoveries have increased life expectancy and removed deadly diseases, which no communication technology can do. It is true that the internet provides access to information and global communication, but this benefit is less essential than survival. Without health, people could not use any technology at all. For instance, penicillin, discovered in 1928, has saved millions of lives by treating bacterial infections that were once fatal.","In conclusion, this essay disagrees that the internet is the most important invention because it depends on earlier discoveries and has saved fewer lives than medicine. Fundamental inventions such as electricity and antibiotics remain more important. The internet is important, but it is not the most fundamental invention."],"wc":270},{"id":"technology9","topic":"technology","type":"Discussion","key":true,"q":"Some people think that scientific research should be shared freely, while others think it should belong to the companies that fund it. Discuss both views and give your opinion.","ideas":["Companies argue that they should own the results of research that they finance","Sharing research accelerates progress and benefits everyone"],"ex":["For example, pharmaceutical companies use patents to recover the high cost of developing new drugs, which often takes more than ten years.","For instance, the genetic sequence of the Covid-19 virus was published in January 2020, which helped scientists to develop vaccines in record time, and the data of the Human Genome Project were made public."],"essay":["Some people believe that discoveries should be open to everyone, while others argue that they should be protected by the organisations that pay for them. This essay believes that basic research should be shared, although companies can protect commercial applications for a limited time.","On the one hand, companies argue that they should own the results of research that they finance. This is because medical and technological research costs billions, and without patents, rivals could copy the product and the company would not recover its investment. As a result, firms might stop investing in new medicines. National security can also be a reason for secrecy in some technologies. However, I believe that this protection should be limited. Companies also argue that secrecy prevents competitors from copying them. For example, pharmaceutical companies use patents to recover the high cost of developing new drugs, which often takes more than ten years.","On the other hand, sharing research accelerates progress and benefits everyone. That is to say, when scientists around the world can use the same data, they avoid repeating work and solve global problems faster. This is especially important for health and the environment, where delays cost lives. For instance, the genetic sequence of the Covid-19 virus was published in January 2020, which helped scientists to develop vaccines in record time, and the data of the Human Genome Project were made public.","In conclusion, although companies need some protection for their investments, this essay believes that basic scientific research should be shared because it speeds up progress for all of humanity. Commercial products can remain protected for a limited period. Open science and fair business can therefore exist together."],"wc":277},{"id":"media1","topic":"media","type":"Opinion","key":true,"q":"The internet has changed the way we shop. Do you think this is a positive or negative trend?","ideas":["Online shopping is convenient and offers a wide choice","The negative effects can be limited"],"ex":["For example, the pandemic accelerated online commerce, and companies such as Amazon delivered essential goods to millions of people who could not leave their homes.","For instance, many high streets in the UK have lost shops to online competition, but some of them have recovered by adding cafés, markets and local events."],"essay":["It is clear that online platforms have transformed how people buy goods. This essay believes that this is a positive trend because it offers convenience and choice, although it also harms traditional shops.","The primary reason is that online shopping is convenient and offers a wide choice. This is because customers can order products at any time from their homes, compare prices in a few seconds and read reviews from other buyers, so they spend less money and time. People who live in small towns or who have a disability also gain access to goods that were previously unavailable. As a result, shopping has become easier and fairer for many consumers. For example, the pandemic accelerated online commerce, and companies such as Amazon delivered essential goods to millions of people who could not leave their homes.","Another important point is that the negative effects can be limited. That is to say, online shopping causes a decline of town-centre shops, impulse purchases and packaging waste, and fraud is also possible. However, shops can respond by offering personal service and by selling online too, and consumers can protect themselves by using secure sites and by returning fewer items. For instance, many high streets in the UK have lost shops to online competition, but some of them have recovered by adding cafés, markets and local events.","In conclusion, this essay believes that the internet has changed shopping in a positive way because it provides convenience, choice and lower prices. The risks to town centres and the environment can be reduced by wise decisions of both shops and customers."],"wc":265},{"id":"media2","topic":"media","type":"Opinion","key":false,"q":"Has the internet made our world less safe? What is your opinion?","ideas":["The internet has created new opportunities for fraud and data theft","The internet allows harassment and the spread of false information"],"ex":["For example, the 2017 data breach at the credit agency Equifax exposed the personal data of tens of millions of people.","For instance, the World Health Organization used the word \"infodemic\" during the pandemic to describe the flood of false information, and the European Union introduced the GDPR in 2018 to protect personal data."],"essay":["Some people believe that online technology has created new dangers for modern society. This essay agrees that the internet has made the world less safe in certain ways, because of fraud and data theft and because of harassment and false information, although solutions exist.","The primary reason is that the internet has created new opportunities for fraud and data theft. This is because criminals can send fake messages to millions of people at the same time and can steal passwords and personal information from companies that store them. As a result, ordinary people can lose money or their identity without ever meeting the criminal. For example, the 2017 data breach at the credit agency Equifax exposed the personal data of tens of millions of people.","Another important point is that the internet allows harassment and the spread of false information. That is to say, people can hide behind anonymous accounts to bully others, and rumours or fake news spread faster than corrections, which can harm public health and trust. Fortunately, education and regulation can reduce these dangers, for example through strong passwords, two-step verification and clear laws. For instance, the World Health Organization used the word \"infodemic\" during the pandemic to describe the flood of false information, and the European Union introduced the GDPR in 2018 to protect personal data.","In conclusion, this essay agrees that the internet has made the world less safe because of fraud, harassment and misinformation, but digital education and strong laws can control these risks. Users must also take responsibility for their own security. Safe habits online are therefore as important as safe habits in the street."],"wc":272},{"id":"media3","topic":"media","type":"Two-part Question","key":true,"q":"More people are watching Netflix and other streaming services online. Why is this? Do you think this is a good change?","ideas":["People choose streaming services because they are flexible, affordable and full of original content"],"ex":["For example, the Korean series Squid Game, produced by Netflix, became a worldwide phenomenon in 2021.","For instance, many viewers now follow series from Spain, Korea or Turkey, which were hard to find on traditional television a few years ago."],"essay":["An increasing number of viewers choose online streaming platforms instead of traditional television. This is mainly due to flexibility and quality content, and I believe that it is a good change, although it has some disadvantages.","People choose streaming services because they are flexible, affordable and full of original content. This is because viewers can watch what they want at any time and on any device, without waiting for a programme schedule or sitting through advertisements. In addition, monthly subscriptions cost less than traditional cable packages, and the platforms invest in exciting productions from many countries. As a result, they have become the first choice of many families. Many viewers also appreciate subtitles, which help them to learn languages. For example, the Korean series Squid Game, produced by Netflix, became a worldwide phenomenon in 2021. This change is mostly good because it gives viewers freedom and access to diverse stories. That is to say, people can discover films from other cultures, learn languages with subtitles and control what children watch. However, there are some negative aspects, such as binge-watching, less shared experience and the cost of several subscriptions, but these problems are manageable. Parents can also create profiles for children with suitable programmes only. For instance, many viewers now follow series from Spain, Korea or Turkey, which were hard to find on traditional television a few years ago.","In conclusion, people watch streaming services because they are flexible, cheap and full of original shows, and this is a good change because it offers variety and control. Viewers should still limit their screen time and choose programmes carefully."],"wc":267},{"id":"media4","topic":"media","type":"Discussion","key":true,"q":"Some people get their news from the internet, while other people do not trust the news they read online. Discuss both sides and give your opinion.","ideas":["Online news is fast and easy to access","Distrust is understandable because the internet spreads false information"],"ex":["For example, during major events such as elections or natural disasters, many people follow updates on social media before television reports appear.","For instance, the World Health Organization described an \"infodemic\" of false claims during the Covid-19 pandemic, and Finland is known for teaching media literacy in its schools."],"essay":["Some people rely on the internet for the news, while others do not trust what they read online. This essay agrees with the sceptics to some extent, but it believes that online news is useful if readers check their sources.","On the one hand, online news is fast and easy to access. This is because stories appear within minutes of an event and can be read on any phone for free, and readers can compare many different sources and opinions. As a result, people in remote areas or in countries with strict state media can also receive information. However, speed sometimes comes at the cost of accuracy. Moreover, online news is often free, which makes it accessible to students and people with low incomes. For example, during major events such as elections or natural disasters, many people follow updates on social media before television reports appear.","On the other hand, distrust is understandable because the internet spreads false information. That is to say, anyone can publish a story without editors or fact-checking, and some websites deliberately create false stories to attract clicks or to influence opinion. Therefore, readers should rely on well-known sources and check unusual claims. Schools should also teach media literacy. For instance, the World Health Organization described an \"infodemic\" of false claims during the Covid-19 pandemic, and Finland is known for teaching media literacy in its schools.","In conclusion, although the internet offers fast and open news, this essay believes that readers are right to be careful because misinformation is common. With media education, people can enjoy the benefits and avoid the risks. Critical readers will therefore get the best of both worlds."],"wc":276},{"id":"media5","topic":"media","type":"Opinion","key":false,"q":"Some people think newspapers will one day die out in favour of online news. To what extent do you agree?","ideas":["The primary reason why printed newspapers are declining is that readers and advertisers have moved online","Journalism itself will continue in digital form"],"ex":["For example, the British newspaper The Independent stopped printing in 2016 and became a digital-only publication.","For instance, major quality newspapers around the world now have millions of digital subscribers, and they publish investigations that are read on phones and tablets."],"essay":["It is predicted that printed papers will disappear and that digital media will replace them completely. This essay partly agrees with this prediction because paper editions are declining quickly, but it believes that quality journalism will survive online.","The primary reason why printed newspapers are declining is that readers and advertisers have moved online. This is because online news is free, immediate and available on every phone, while printed papers are published only once a day and are more expensive. As a result, circulation figures fall, and many local papers cannot cover their costs and close. Digital editions are also cheaper to produce and can include videos and live updates. For example, the British newspaper The Independent stopped printing in 2016 and became a digital-only publication.","Another important point is that journalism itself will continue in digital form. That is to say, readers still need reliable reporting, and many respected newspapers have found new readers through paid online subscriptions. Some people may still enjoy the feel of paper, but this will be a small niche in the future. Some readers will always prefer paper, but they are becoming a small group. For instance, major quality newspapers around the world now have millions of digital subscribers, and they publish investigations that are read on phones and tablets.","In conclusion, this essay agrees that printed newspapers are likely to disappear, but it believes that journalism will continue in an online form. The important thing is that readers continue to pay for reliable information. Quality journalism, not paper, is what society needs to protect."],"wc":261},{"id":"media6","topic":"media","type":"Opinion","key":false,"q":"More people are becoming famous because of reality shows. Do you think this is a positive or negative trend?","ideas":["Reality shows promote superficial role models","Sudden fame can damage the mental health of participants"],"ex":["For example, many winners of reality competitions appear in advertisements and magazines for a short time, but few of them build lasting careers.","For instance, after the deaths of several former participants of the British show Love Island, the producers introduced psychological support for contestants."],"essay":["An increasing number of ordinary people become celebrities after appearing on reality television. This essay believes that this is a negative trend because it promotes superficial role models, and because it puts participants under serious psychological pressure.","The primary reason is that reality shows promote superficial role models. This is because participants become famous for their appearance or their conflicts instead of talent or hard work, so young viewers may believe that a quick path to fame is better than education or professional effort. As a result, they may develop unrealistic goals for their own lives. In the long term, this attitude can reduce interest in education and training. Moreover, producers often encourage arguments and drama to attract viewers. For example, many winners of reality competitions appear in advertisements and magazines for a short time, but few of them build lasting careers.","Another important point is that sudden fame can damage the mental health of participants. That is to say, people with no experience of the media face public criticism and online hate, and when their popularity fades, they may feel lost. Although some contestants benefit from new opportunities, these are exceptions. Television companies should provide counselling before and after the show. For instance, after the deaths of several former participants of the British show Love Island, the producers introduced psychological support for contestants.","In conclusion, this essay believes that becoming famous through reality shows is a negative trend because it encourages superficial values and harms participants. Television companies should provide better care for their contestants. Viewers should also remember that these programmes are edited for entertainment."],"wc":267},{"id":"media7","topic":"media","type":"Advantages / Disadvantages","key":true,"q":"Many young children look up to actors and actresses as role models. What are the advantages and disadvantages of this?","ideas":["Famous actors can inspire children to develop good values","Celebrity lifestyles can be unrealistic and may promote harmful behaviour"],"ex":["For example, the footballer Marcus Rashford campaigned for free school meals in the UK in 2020, and the government changed its policy after his campaign.","For instance, many film stars promote cosmetic products or luxury brands on social media, and young fans can feel unhappy when they cannot imitate this lifestyle."],"essay":["Many young children admire film stars and try to copy them. The main advantage of this is that celebrities can inspire positive behaviour, whereas the main disadvantage is that their lifestyle can be unrealistic and sometimes harmful.","The main advantage is that famous actors can inspire children to develop good values. This is because children listen to people they admire, so when a celebrity supports a good cause or talks about hard work, young fans are motivated to follow their example. In addition, successful actors can show that creativity and discipline lead to results. As a result, they can have a positive influence on young viewers. In addition, fans often follow their favourite stars on social media, which strengthens this influence. For example, the footballer Marcus Rashford campaigned for free school meals in the UK in 2020, and the government changed its policy after his campaign.","One major disadvantage is that celebrity lifestyles can be unrealistic and may promote harmful behaviour. In other words, children may believe that wealth and beauty are the main measures of success, and they may copy habits such as extreme diets or the use of dangerous products that stars advertise. A better choice of role models might be teachers, scientists or doctors, who work for the benefit of others. Parents can also explain that a film role is not the same as real life. For instance, many film stars promote cosmetic products or luxury brands on social media, and young fans can feel unhappy when they cannot imitate this lifestyle.","In conclusion, actors can inspire children with positive messages, but they can also encourage unrealistic and harmful ideas. Parents and teachers should therefore guide young people to choose role models carefully."],"wc":286},{"id":"media8","topic":"media","type":"Opinion","key":false,"q":"Children enjoy socialising online. Is this a positive or negative trend?","ideas":["Online socialising exposes children to cyberbullying and other dangers","Online socialising can lead to addiction and weaker real-life skills"],"ex":["For example, the United Kingdom passed the Online Safety Act in 2023 to force platforms to protect children from harmful content.","For instance, many schools have introduced rules that restrict phone use during lessons because pupils were distracted by their messages."],"essay":["More and more children spend their free time chatting with friends on the internet. This essay believes that this is a negative trend because of the danger of cyberbullying and the risk of addiction, although it also has some benefits.","The primary reason is that online socialising exposes children to cyberbullying and other dangers. This is because young people can be insulted anonymously, and they may meet strangers who pretend to be friends. Messages are also permanent, so a cruel comment can hurt a child for a long time. As a result, many pupils feel anxious and unsafe, even when they are at home. This is a particular risk for shy children who spend a lot of time online. For example, the United Kingdom passed the Online Safety Act in 2023 to force platforms to protect children from harmful content.","Another important point is that online socialising can lead to addiction and weaker real-life skills. That is to say, children who spend many hours on their phones sleep less, exercise less and practise face-to-face conversation less often. It is true that the internet allows them to keep contact with distant friends and to learn digital skills, but these advantages do not remove the need for limits. Parents who talk to their children about online behaviour can reduce these dangers. For instance, many schools have introduced rules that restrict phone use during lessons because pupils were distracted by their messages.","In conclusion, this essay believes that online socialising is a negative trend for children because of bullying and overuse, although it has some benefits. Age limits, parental supervision and clear time limits would reduce the risks."],"wc":275},{"id":"media9","topic":"media","type":"Opinion","key":false,"q":"Many celebrities complain about their lack of privacy from the media. Do you think celebrities have the right to privacy?","ideas":["Everyone has a basic right to a private life, including famous people","The media must respect clear limits, even though public figures accept some attention"],"ex":["For example, Princess Diana died in 1997 in a car accident while she was being followed by photographers.","For instance, the phone-hacking scandal in the British press led to the Leveson Inquiry in 2011, which examined how newspapers collect information."],"essay":["Famous people often say that journalists and photographers invade their private lives. This essay believes that celebrities have the right to privacy because constant pursuit can cause tragedies, and because their family members did not choose fame.","The primary reason is that everyone has a basic right to a private life, including famous people. This is because fame is connected to a profession, not to every moment of a person's day, so a celebrity should be able to walk with their family or visit a doctor without being followed. When photographers chase them, they may also act dangerously. As a result, the lack of privacy can seriously damage mental health. In addition, constant attention can make it hard for them to have normal friendships. Moreover, families of celebrities, especially children, never chose a public life. For example, Princess Diana died in 1997 in a car accident while she was being followed by photographers.","Another important point is that the media must respect clear limits, even though public figures accept some attention. That is to say, journalists may report on a politician's work or a celebrity's public statements, but not on their children or private health. Illegal methods, such as hacking phones, should be punished. This balance protects both press freedom and personal dignity. For instance, the phone-hacking scandal in the British press led to the Leveson Inquiry in 2011, which examined how newspapers collect information.","In conclusion, this essay believes that celebrities have the right to privacy because they are ordinary people outside their job, and the media must respect clear limits. Strong laws can balance public interest and personal safety."],"wc":271},{"id":"media10","topic":"media","type":"Opinion","key":false,"q":"Many celebrities have cosmetic surgery. Is this a positive or negative trend?","ideas":["Celebrities with cosmetic surgery create unrealistic standards of beauty","Cosmetic surgery involves risks and sets a bad example"],"ex":["For example, South Korea has one of the highest rates of cosmetic procedures in the world, and young people there often feel pressure to match the faces of famous singers.","For instance, surgeons often warn that patients with unrealistic expectations are more likely to be disappointed with the results of cosmetic operations."],"essay":["It is increasingly common for famous people to change their appearance with surgery. This essay believes that this is a negative trend because it creates unrealistic beauty standards, and because it carries medical risks.","The primary reason is that celebrities with cosmetic surgery create unrealistic standards of beauty. This is because millions of fans see perfect faces and bodies on screens and social media, and they may not realise that these images are the result of surgery and editing. As a result, many ordinary people, especially teenagers, feel dissatisfied with their own bodies and may decide to have unnecessary operations. Fans may also copy their idols without thinking about the cost. For example, South Korea has one of the highest rates of cosmetic procedures in the world, and young people there often feel pressure to match the faces of famous singers.","Another important point is that cosmetic surgery involves risks and sets a bad example. That is to say, every operation can cause complications, and some people continue to have repeated procedures because they are never satisfied. Of course, surgery that repairs injuries is valuable, but this is different from changes that follow fashion. Doctors and educators could also explain these risks in schools. For instance, surgeons often warn that patients with unrealistic expectations are more likely to be disappointed with the results of cosmetic operations.","In conclusion, this essay believes that celebrity cosmetic surgery is a negative trend because it spreads unrealistic beauty standards and encourages risky decisions. Celebrities could set a better example by being honest about their use of editing and surgery."],"wc":265},{"id":"media11","topic":"media","type":"Two-part Question","key":false,"q":"Adverts for products often entice people to buy products they do not need. Why is this? Is this a problem?","ideas":["Advertisements are successful because they appeal to emotions and repeat their message constantly"],"ex":["For example, many people buy the newest smartphone every year because of advertising, even if their old phone still works well.","For instance, Quebec has banned commercial advertising aimed at children under thirteen since 1980, and Chile restricted marketing of unhealthy products to children in 2016."],"essay":["Advertisements regularly persuade consumers to purchase things that they do not really need. This is mainly due to emotional marketing and constant repetition, and it is a problem because it leads to waste and financial difficulties.","Advertisements are successful because they appeal to emotions and repeat their message constantly. This is because companies do not simply describe a product; they connect it with happiness, success or beauty, so people feel that they will be happier if they buy it. In addition, ads appear on television, phones and street posters every day, which creates a desire to buy. As a result, shoppers often purchase items on impulse. Moreover, personalised advertising on the internet uses data to show people exactly what tempts them. For example, many people buy the newest smartphone every year because of advertising, even if their old phone still works well. This is a problem because it causes waste, debt and unrealistic expectations. That is to say, unnecessary purchases produce more rubbish and use up natural resources, and families may spend money that they cannot afford. Children are especially vulnerable, since they cannot judge the intention of an advertisement. Although advertising also informs people about new products, this benefit is smaller than the harm. Therefore, shoppers need to ask themselves whether they really need a product before they buy it. For instance, Quebec has banned commercial advertising aimed at children under thirteen since 1980, and Chile restricted marketing of unhealthy products to children in 2016.","In conclusion, ads encourage unnecessary buying by using emotion and repetition, and this is a problem because it creates waste and debt. Governments should limit advertising to children and teach media literacy in schools."],"wc":280},{"id":"media12","topic":"media","type":"Problem / Solution","key":true,"q":"Advertising influences people to buy things such as clothes and shoes. What are the problems caused by that? What solutions can be given?","ideas":["Advertising pushes people to spend money on things that they do not need","Regulate advertising more strictly and to teach people how it works"],"ex":["For example, fast fashion companies release new collections every few weeks, supported by constant advertising on social media.","For instance, Quebec has banned commercial advertising aimed at children under thirteen since 1980, and Chile restricted marketing to children in 2016."],"essay":["Commercial messages have a strong effect on what people buy, especially clothes and shoes. The main problems caused by this are overspending and waste, and the most effective solutions are stricter advertising rules and education about marketing.","The main problem is that advertising pushes people to spend money on things that they do not need. This is because advertisements create the idea that old clothes are out of fashion, so consumers replace them often, and they buy expensive brands to show status. As a result, people get into debt, and the production of cheap fashion creates huge amounts of waste and pollution. Many young people also take loans to follow fashion trends. In addition, people often feel pressure from friends to wear the same brands. For example, fast fashion companies release new collections every few weeks, supported by constant advertising on social media.","A viable solution is to regulate advertising more strictly and to teach people how it works. If governments limit misleading claims and the targeting of children, and if schools teach pupils how advertisements motivate buyers, consumers will make more careful decisions. Companies can also be required to show the real cost of a product. Customers who understand advertising are less easily manipulated. For instance, Quebec has banned commercial advertising aimed at children under thirteen since 1980, and Chile restricted marketing to children in 2016.","In conclusion, advertising leads to overspending and waste, but better regulation and media education can protect consumers. A more critical public would also encourage companies to be more honest. Companies would also benefit from building trust with honest messages."],"wc":266},{"id":"language1","topic":"language","type":"Advantages / Disadvantages","key":true,"q":"More and more people are doing business and conducting meetings through video call online. What are the advantages and disadvantages of this?","ideas":["They save time and money","Video calls are tiring and make personal relationships weaker"],"ex":["For example, during the Covid-19 pandemic, millions of companies moved their meetings online, and many of them continued this practice afterwards because of the savings.","For instance, researchers at Stanford University described \"Zoom fatigue\" and found that long video meetings leave participants exhausted."],"essay":["Business discussions are increasingly held through online video calls instead of face-to-face meetings. The main advantages of this practice are savings of time and money and lower pollution, whereas the main disadvantages are fatigue and weaker relationships.","The main advantage of video meetings is that they save time and money. This is because participants do not need to travel to another city or country, so companies spend less on flights and hotels, and employees can attend several meetings in one day. In addition, people who live far away or have a disability can take part easily, and fewer journeys mean lower emissions. As a result, businesses become more efficient and more inclusive. Online meetings can also be recorded, which helps people who missed them. For example, during the Covid-19 pandemic, millions of companies moved their meetings online, and many of them continued this practice afterwards because of the savings.","One major disadvantage is that video calls are tiring and make personal relationships weaker. In other words, looking at a screen for hours requires intense concentration, and technical problems often interrupt the discussion. Moreover, it is harder to read body language and to build trust with new partners through a screen, which matters in negotiations. Workers who spend their whole day in calls also lose spontaneous conversations with colleagues. For instance, researchers at Stanford University described \"Zoom fatigue\" and found that long video meetings leave participants exhausted.","In conclusion, video meetings save time, money and the environment, but they can cause tiredness and reduce personal contact. Companies should therefore combine online calls with occasional face-to-face meetings for important decisions."],"wc":268},{"id":"language2","topic":"language","type":"Opinion","key":false,"q":"Globalisation has brought the world closer together. Some people think countries will eventually become very similar. To what extent do you agree?","ideas":["The primary reason why countries are becoming more similar is the spread of global brands and media","Local identity does not disappear easily"],"ex":["For example, the South Korean film Parasite won the Oscar for Best Picture in 2020 and the series Squid Game became a worldwide success in 2021, so entertainment travels across borders in a few days.","For instance, a Starbucks café in the Forbidden City in Beijing closed in 2007 after public protests, which showed how strongly people can defend their heritage."],"essay":["It is often said that globalisation is connecting countries and that they will gradually become almost identical. This essay partly agrees with this idea because global brands and media spread the same habits, but it believes that local cultures will survive.","The primary reason why countries are becoming more similar is the spread of global brands and media. This is because the same films, music, fashion and fast-food chains appear in almost every city, and young people everywhere follow the same online trends. As a result, shopping streets and everyday habits look increasingly alike in different parts of the world. Social media accelerates this process because trends spread across borders within hours. For example, the South Korean film Parasite won the Oscar for Best Picture in 2020 and the series Squid Game became a worldwide success in 2021, so entertainment travels across borders in a few days.","Another important point is that local identity does not disappear easily. That is to say, people value their language, food and traditions as a source of pride, and communities actively protect them through festivals, education and local products. Therefore, global culture is usually added to local culture instead of replacing it. Many cities also promote their local food, festivals and crafts to attract visitors. For instance, a Starbucks café in the Forbidden City in Beijing closed in 2007 after public protests, which showed how strongly people can defend their heritage.","In conclusion, this essay partly agrees that countries will become more alike in their entertainment and consumption, but it believes that national identities will survive. Governments can support this by protecting local languages and traditions."],"wc":272},{"id":"language3","topic":"language","type":"Opinion","key":false,"q":"The world will one day have only one language. To what extent do you agree?","ideas":["Translation technology makes a single language unnecessary","Languages carry identity, and communities work hard to protect them"],"ex":["For example, English is the official language of aviation, but pilots and passengers around the world still speak thousands of different languages at home.","For instance, the Maori language has been strengthened by \"language nests\" for young children since 1982, Welsh is taught to all pupils in Wales, and Duolingo now offers courses in Hawaiian and Navajo."],"essay":["It is predicted that all people will eventually communicate in a single language. This essay disagrees with this prediction because technology reduces the need for one common language, and because communities fight to keep their own languages alive.","The primary reason is that translation technology makes a single language unnecessary. This is because automatic translation tools allow people to speak and write to each other in their own languages, so they do not need to give up their mother tongue. Although English helps in fields such as aviation and science, it does not have to replace everything else. As a result, a world with many languages can still communicate easily. For example, English is the official language of aviation, but pilots and passengers around the world still speak thousands of different languages at home.","Another important point is that languages carry identity, and communities work hard to protect them. That is to say, each language contains stories, humour and a unique way of thinking, so people do not want to lose it. Some minority languages are in danger because of migration and economic pressure, but education and technology can help them survive. For instance, the Maori language has been strengthened by \"language nests\" for young children since 1982, Welsh is taught to all pupils in Wales, and Duolingo now offers courses in Hawaiian and Navajo.","In conclusion, this essay disagrees that the world will have only one language because of translation technology and the determination of communities to protect their identity. Governments should support minority languages in schools and online."],"wc":260},{"id":"language4","topic":"language","type":"Opinion","key":true,"q":"Children should all learn a foreign language to thrive in today's modern world. Do you agree?","ideas":["Knowing a foreign language improves career opportunities","Learning languages develops the mind and encourages openness to other cultures"],"ex":["For example, the European Union promotes the goal that every citizen should speak their mother tongue plus two other languages, because this improves mobility and employment.","For instance, Singapore has a bilingual education policy in which pupils study English and their mother tongue, and its graduates are highly valued in global business."],"essay":["It is argued that every child needs to study a foreign language in order to succeed in the modern world. This essay agrees with this statement because languages improve career opportunities, and because they develop the mind and cultural awareness.","The primary reason is that knowing a foreign language improves career opportunities. This is because many companies operate internationally and look for employees who can communicate with clients and partners in other countries. As a result, people who speak more than one language often find better jobs and can work abroad. It is easier to learn a language in childhood, so schools should start early. Many universities also require knowledge of another language for exchange programmes. For example, the European Union promotes the goal that every citizen should speak their mother tongue plus two other languages, because this improves mobility and employment.","Another important point is that learning languages develops the mind and encourages openness to other cultures. That is to say, children who study another language become more flexible thinkers and better at solving problems, and they learn to respect people from different backgrounds. Although machine translation exists, it cannot replace the understanding that comes from speaking with others directly. For instance, Singapore has a bilingual education policy in which pupils study English and their mother tongue, and its graduates are highly valued in global business.","In conclusion, this essay agrees that all children should learn a foreign language because it improves their careers and widens their minds. Schools should therefore make language learning a priority from primary level. Early lessons also make pronunciation easier to learn."],"wc":268},{"id":"language5","topic":"language","type":"Opinion","key":false,"q":"With an increasing population communicating via internet and text messaging, face to face communication will become a thing of the past. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","ideas":["People need physical contact for trust and empathy","Technology complements face-to-face communication instead of replacing it"],"ex":["For example, the UK appointed a Minister for Loneliness in 2018 because isolation had become a serious public health problem.","For instance, video calls keep families together when relatives live abroad, and many people travel home for holidays so that they can see each other in person."],"essay":["It is claimed that because people increasingly use messages and online platforms, personal meetings will disappear. This essay disagrees with this view because humans need physical contact to build trust and empathy, and because technology often supports meetings instead of replacing them.","The primary reason is that people need physical contact for trust and empathy. This is because facial expressions, tone of voice and body language help us to understand feelings, and these signals are weaker through a screen. As a result, important matters such as job interviews, business deals and family problems are still discussed in person. Loneliness also increases when people depend only on messages. Even people who chat online all day often feel lonely without direct contact. For example, the UK appointed a Minister for Loneliness in 2018 because isolation had become a serious public health problem.","Another important point is that technology complements face-to-face communication instead of replacing it. That is to say, people use messages to arrange meetings and video calls to stay in touch with those who live far away, but they still want to meet in person whenever possible. These tools therefore make relationships stronger. Teachers, doctors and managers also prefer to explain serious matters in person. For instance, video calls keep families together when relatives live abroad, and many people travel home for holidays so that they can see each other in person.","In conclusion, this essay disagrees that personal communication will disappear because humans need physical contact and because technology supports meetings. Face-to-face conversation will remain essential in the future. Technology will therefore change how we meet, not whether we meet."],"wc":270},{"id":"language6","topic":"language","type":"Opinion","key":false,"q":"Some people think that because of email, social media and telephones, communication among people is less personal now than it used to be. To what extent do you agree or disagree?","ideas":["The primary reason why communication has become less personal is that digital messages lose tone and emotion","Technology also helps people to keep personal relationships alive"],"ex":["For example, a short e-mail that sounds neutral to the writer may seem cold or angry to the reader, which often causes conflicts at work.","For instance, millions of emigrants use video calls every week to speak to their relatives and to take part in family celebrations from a distance."],"essay":["It is suggested that modern tools such as e-mail, social networks and mobile phones have made human contact less personal. This essay partly agrees with this view because messages lose tone and depth, but it believes that technology also helps people to stay close.","The primary reason why communication has become less personal is that digital messages lose tone and emotion. This is because short texts and emojis replace long conversations, and without a voice or a face, it is easy to misunderstand a message. In addition, people often talk to many contacts quickly instead of building deep relationships with a few. As a result, many conversations stay on the surface. For example, a short e-mail that sounds neutral to the writer may seem cold or angry to the reader, which often causes conflicts at work.","Another important point is that technology also helps people to keep personal relationships alive. That is to say, families and friends who live in different countries can talk daily by video and share photos instantly, which was impossible in the past. The quality of communication depends on how people use these tools, not on the tools themselves. Older relatives in particular enjoy seeing the faces of their grandchildren. For instance, millions of emigrants use video calls every week to speak to their relatives and to take part in family celebrations from a distance.","In conclusion, this essay partly agrees that digital communication can be less personal, but it believes that quality depends on the user and that technology can strengthen relationships. People should therefore use calls and meetings for important conversations. Both forms of contact have a role."],"wc":274},{"id":"work1","topic":"work","type":"Opinion","key":true,"q":"Working hard is the only way to be successful. Do you agree?","ideas":["Hard work alone does not guarantee success","Efficient work is often more effective than long hours"],"ex":["For example, in Japan the word \"karoshi\" describes death from overwork, which shows that extreme effort can destroy health without bringing success.","For instance, Finland has relatively short working hours but still has very good productivity, and in the United Kingdom many companies that tested a four-day week continued it afterwards."],"essay":["It is claimed that effort is the only route to success in life. This essay disagrees with this statement because hard work does not guarantee results without opportunity and luck, and because working smart is often more effective than working long hours.","The primary reason is that hard work alone does not guarantee success. This is because success also depends on opportunities, contacts, timing and sometimes luck, so two people who put in the same effort may achieve very different results. As a result, many dedicated people remain poor or unrecognised through no fault of their own. Talent and education also play an important role. Even very talented people sometimes fail because they never receive a chance. Others succeed with moderate effort because they are in the right place at the right time. For example, in Japan the word \"karoshi\" describes death from overwork, which shows that extreme effort can destroy health without bringing success.","Another important point is that efficient work is often more effective than long hours. That is to say, people who plan well, focus on important tasks and rest properly achieve more in less time. Companies that respect this principle often find that their employees are more creative and productive. For instance, Finland has relatively short working hours but still has very good productivity, and in the United Kingdom many companies that tested a four-day week continued it afterwards.","In conclusion, this essay disagrees that hard work is the only way to succeed because opportunities, luck and smart methods are also important. Effort is necessary, but it must be combined with good planning and chances. Success is therefore a mixture of several factors."],"wc":277},{"id":"work2","topic":"work","type":"Two-part Question","key":true,"q":"Why do so many people prefer working remotely from home or elsewhere? Are there any downsides to this?","ideas":["Many people prefer remote work because it saves time and money and gives them flexibility"],"ex":["For example, a study by Nick Bloom of Stanford University at the Chinese company Ctrip found that employees working from home were more productive than those in the office.","For instance, France introduced the \"right to disconnect\" in 2017 and Portugal in 2021 to protect remote workers from constant messages after hours."],"essay":["An increasing number of employees want to work outside the office. This is mainly due to savings of time and money and greater flexibility, and the downsides are isolation and a blurred line between work and private life.","Many people prefer remote work because it saves time and money and gives them flexibility. This is because employees do not have to commute, so they save hours of travel and the cost of fuel or tickets, and they can arrange their day to fit family needs. In addition, companies can also benefit from lower office costs and access to talent from other regions. As a result, productivity often stays high or even improves. For example, a study by Nick Bloom of Stanford University at the Chinese company Ctrip found that employees working from home were more productive than those in the office. However, remote work has some downsides for both employees and employers. That is to say, people who work alone may feel isolated and find it hard to stop working in the evening, while managers find it more difficult to supervise and to build a team culture. For this reason, a hybrid model, with some days at home and some in the office, is often the best compromise. For instance, France introduced the \"right to disconnect\" in 2017 and Portugal in 2021 to protect remote workers from constant messages after hours.","In conclusion, people prefer remote work because it saves time and offers flexibility, but it can cause isolation and weaker teamwork. A hybrid arrangement allows employees and employers to enjoy the benefits and reduce the downsides."],"wc":267},{"id":"work3","topic":"work","type":"Two-part Question","key":false,"q":"Many people around the world work very long hours. Why is this? Do you think this is a good thing?","ideas":["People work long hours mainly because of the cost of living, strong competition and workplace culture"],"ex":["For example, in Japan and South Korea long working hours are part of company culture, and South Korea introduced a limit of 52 hours a week in 2018.","For instance, the Japanese word \"karoshi\" describes death caused by overwork, which shows how extreme the consequences can be."],"essay":["In many countries, employees spend far more hours at work than they should. This is mainly due to economic pressure and workplace culture, and I believe that it is not a good thing because it harms health and family life.","People work long hours mainly because of the cost of living, strong competition and workplace culture. This is because salaries are often too low to cover rent and bills, so many workers take extra shifts or a second job. In addition, employees fear that they will lose their job if they leave work earlier than their colleagues, and in some cultures long hours are seen as a sign of loyalty. As a result, overtime becomes normal. Moreover, in some industries, a low basic salary encourages employees to count on overtime pay. For example, in Japan and South Korea long working hours are part of company culture, and South Korea introduced a limit of 52 hours a week in 2018. This situation is not good because it damages health, relationships and even productivity. That is to say, tired workers make more mistakes, suffer from stress and burnout and have little time for family or rest. In the long term, this is bad for companies as well, because exhausted employees leave or become ill. For instance, the Japanese word \"karoshi\" describes death caused by overwork, which shows how extreme the consequences can be.","In conclusion, long working hours are caused by economic pressure and company culture, and they are harmful because they damage health and family life. Governments should set sensible limits and employers should value results rather than hours. Better work-life balance should be a goal for every society."],"wc":278},{"id":"work4","topic":"work","type":"Discussion","key":true,"q":"Some people do work they love, others do work solely for money. Discuss both sides and give your opinion.","ideas":["Working solely for money provides security","Work that you love brings motivation and satisfaction"],"ex":["For example, many parents choose stable jobs in banks or offices because they provide regular income for their children's education.","For instance, surveys by the research company Gallup show that a large share of employees around the world are not engaged in their work, which affects both their wellbeing and their productivity."],"essay":["Some people choose a career according to their passion, while others work only for their salary. This essay believes that a balance is best, but that doing work that you enjoy is more valuable in the long term.","On the one hand, working solely for money provides security. This is because a good salary allows people to pay their bills, support a family and plan for the future, and not every job that people love is well paid. Many workers therefore accept less interesting jobs to protect their household. However, a job that is only a source of income can become boring and tiring. Many people also feel proud when they can support their relatives financially. For example, many parents choose stable jobs in banks or offices because they provide regular income for their children's education.","On the other hand, work that you love brings motivation and satisfaction. That is to say, people who enjoy their jobs are more creative and persistent, and they feel that their life has meaning. They are also less likely to suffer from stress or to change careers. When a person is passionate, success and income often follow. Of course, not everybody can find such a job immediately, but they can develop interests gradually. For instance, surveys by the research company Gallup show that a large share of employees around the world are not engaged in their work, which affects both their wellbeing and their productivity.","In conclusion, although a stable salary is essential, this essay believes that people should look for work that they enjoy because it brings satisfaction and long-term success. A realistic combination of both is ideal."],"wc":275},{"id":"work5","topic":"work","type":"Discussion","key":false,"q":"Some companies seek people who are team players, while other companies prefer people who are independent workers. Discuss both sides and give your opinion.","ideas":["Team players bring collaboration and a variety of skills","Independent workers show initiative and can concentrate deeply"],"ex":["For example, Google's \"Project Aristotle\" in 2015 found that psychological safety, which means feeling safe to speak and share ideas, is the most important factor in effective teams.","For instance, a software developer often works alone for hours to solve a technical problem and then shares the solution with the rest of the team."],"essay":["Some employers look for workers who cooperate well, while others prefer employees who can work alone. This essay believes that team players are generally more valuable, although independence is important in certain jobs.","On the one hand, team players bring collaboration and a variety of skills. This is because most modern projects are too complex for one person, so employees who share ideas and support each other solve problems faster. In addition, a team combines different abilities, which leads to better decisions. As a result, companies usually value cooperation highly. Employers also like team players because they are easier to manage and adapt quickly to new tasks. For example, Google's \"Project Aristotle\" in 2015 found that psychological safety, which means feeling safe to speak and share ideas, is the most important factor in effective teams.","On the other hand, independent workers show initiative and can concentrate deeply. That is to say, some jobs, such as programming, writing or research, require long periods of quiet focus, and employees who can organise themselves need less supervision. Such people can also work in remote or flexible conditions. However, even independent professionals must communicate with colleagues from time to time. Therefore, companies often combine both types to build strong departments. For instance, a software developer often works alone for hours to solve a technical problem and then shares the solution with the rest of the team.","In conclusion, although independent workers are valuable in jobs that need concentration, this essay believes that team players are generally more useful because most work requires cooperation. The best employees can do both. Employees should practise both abilities to be successful."],"wc":273},{"id":"work6","topic":"work","type":"Two-part Question","key":false,"q":"In many families, both parents work. How does this impact their children? Is this a positive or negative trend?","ideas":["Children are affected negatively because parents spend less time with them"],"ex":["For example, many children in cities spend the afternoon at after-school clubs or with grandparents until their parents come home.","For instance, Sweden has a long parental leave system with days reserved for each parent, which encourages both mothers and fathers to share childcare."],"essay":["In a large number of households, mothers and fathers both have jobs. This affects children in both good and bad ways, and I believe that it is a mostly positive trend because it brings financial security and a model of independence.","Children are affected negatively because parents spend less time with them. This is because after a long day at work, parents are often tired, so they have less energy to play, help with homework or talk, and children may spend more hours with screens or caregivers. As a result, some of them feel lonely or lack attention. This effect can be reduced if families protect a fixed time for each other every day. Older children may also learn to be responsible by helping with small household tasks. For example, many children in cities spend the afternoon at after-school clubs or with grandparents until their parents come home. However, the trend is positive overall because it increases family income and teaches independence and equality. That is to say, two salaries allow families to afford better housing, education and health care, and children see both parents working and sharing responsibilities, which prepares them for adult life. Governments and employers can help by offering flexible hours and parental leave. For instance, Sweden has a long parental leave system with days reserved for each parent, which encourages both mothers and fathers to share childcare.","In conclusion, when both parents work, children may receive less attention, but they gain financial stability and positive role models. With flexible work and parental leave, this trend can be beneficial for families. Support from society is therefore very important."],"wc":271},{"id":"work7","topic":"work","type":"Opinion","key":false,"q":"Some countries have recently passed laws limiting the daily working hours of employees. Do you think this will have a positive or negative impact?","ideas":["Limits on working hours protect health and family life","Shorter hours can increase productivity, although there are some costs"],"ex":["For example, Japan reformed its rules on overtime in 2019 after cases of death from overwork, and France introduced a 35-hour working week in 2000.","For instance, in trials of a four-day week in Iceland and in the UK in 2022, many companies kept or increased productivity, and a large number of them continued the new schedule."],"essay":["Several governments have introduced legal limits on how many hours people can work each day. This essay believes that this will have a positive impact because it protects health and improves family life and productivity, although some companies will face difficulties.","The primary reason is that limits on working hours protect health and family life. This is because employees who work fewer hours have more time to sleep, exercise and be with their families, which reduces stress and burnout. As a result, they are healthier and more satisfied, and public health costs can fall. Rested workers also make fewer mistakes. Fewer working hours also give people time to learn new skills or to take care of relatives. For example, Japan reformed its rules on overtime in 2019 after cases of death from overwork, and France introduced a 35-hour working week in 2000.","Another important point is that shorter hours can increase productivity, although there are some costs. That is to say, people who are rested concentrate better and work more efficiently, so output may remain the same. Small businesses may find it difficult and workers may earn less overtime, but governments can offer support during the change. For instance, in trials of a four-day week in Iceland and in the UK in 2022, many companies kept or increased productivity, and a large number of them continued the new schedule.","In conclusion, this essay believes that laws limiting working hours will have a positive impact because they improve health, family life and efficiency. Governments should help small companies to adapt so that no one is left behind. Fair rules make the labour market healthier."],"wc":273},{"id":"work8","topic":"work","type":"Problem / Solution","key":false,"q":"The world of work is changing rapidly and people cannot depend on the same job or the same conditions of work for life. Discuss the possible causes for these changes and give your suggestions on how people should prepare for work.","ideas":["The main causes of change are automation, globalisation and the growth of flexible \"gig\" work","Moreover, the pandemic accelerated the use of online work, which made some office jobs less secure"],"ex":["For example, the company Kodak, which once dominated photography, collapsed when digital cameras replaced film.","For instance, Germany retrained former coal miners in the Ruhr region for new industries when the mines closed."],"essay":["The labour market is changing so fast that a lifelong job is no longer realistic. The main causes of this are automation and globalisation, and the best way for people to prepare is by learning continuously and by developing flexible skills.","The main causes of change are automation, globalisation and the growth of flexible \"gig\" work. This is because machines now perform many tasks that used to need people, companies can move production to other countries and platforms allow people to work on short contracts. As a result, some professions disappear completely and new ones appear in a few years. Technology also changes so quickly that skills become old-fashioned.","Moreover, the pandemic accelerated the use of online work, which made some office jobs less secure. Many young people also change employers several times in their careers. For example, the company Kodak, which once dominated photography, collapsed when digital cameras replaced film. People can prepare by learning throughout their lives and by developing transferable skills. If workers keep updating their digital abilities and strengthen skills such as communication, problem-solving and adaptability, they can move to new fields when necessary. Governments and employers can help by funding courses and retraining programmes. Short online courses and certificates are an easy and cheap way to continue learning. Workers who understand their own strengths find it easier to change direction. For instance, Germany retrained former coal miners in the Ruhr region for new industries when the mines closed.","In conclusion, the world of work changes because of automation, globalisation and flexible employment, and people should prepare through lifelong learning and adaptable skills. Support from governments and companies will make this transition easier. Those who adapt early will have the best opportunities."],"wc":286},{"id":"work9","topic":"work","type":"Opinion","key":false,"q":"Making age discrimination in hiring illegal is a good idea. To what extent do you agree?","ideas":["A ban on age discrimination promotes fairness and equal opportunities","The law allows society to benefit from an ageing workforce"],"ex":["For example, the United States passed the Age Discrimination in Employment Act in 1967, and the European Union adopted an equality directive on employment in 2000.","For instance, some employers use applications without dates of birth, and many Japanese firms re-employ retired workers on new contracts."],"essay":["It is proposed that employers should be legally forbidden from refusing candidates because of their age. This essay agrees with this proposal because it promotes fairness, and because it allows society to use the experience of older workers.","The primary reason is that a ban on age discrimination promotes fairness and equal opportunities. This is because people should be judged on their skills and experience and not on their date of birth, and many capable candidates are rejected only because they are considered too old or too young. As a result, the law helps to reduce stereotypes and protects people in difficult financial situations. It also sends a clear message that all workers deserve respect. For example, the United States passed the Age Discrimination in Employment Act in 1967, and the European Union adopted an equality directive on employment in 2000.","Another important point is that the law allows society to benefit from an ageing workforce. That is to say, older employees have valuable experience and reliability, and as people live longer, economies need them to stay in work. It is true that such rules can be difficult to enforce, because employers may hide their real reasons, but training and anonymous applications can help. For instance, some employers use applications without dates of birth, and many Japanese firms re-employ retired workers on new contracts.","In conclusion, this essay agrees that age discrimination in hiring should be illegal because it is fair and makes full use of experienced workers. Strong enforcement and training for employers will make the law effective. Fair hiring benefits employers as well as workers."],"wc":267}];